{"topic": "Maternal Health", "competency": "Complications", "stem": "A pregnant woman at 32 weeks gestation is diagnosed with severe preeclampsia. What is the most significant risk to her and her fetus?", "options": {"A": "Placental abruption", "B": "HELLP syndrome", "C": "Pulmonary embolism", "D": "Cardiopulmonary arrest"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Preeclampsia complicated by generalized tonic-clonic convulsions appreciably raises the risk to both mother and fetus, with major maternal complications including placental abruption.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Obstentrics_Williams_5042", "title": "Obstentrics_Williams", "span_text": "Preeclampsia complicated by generalized tonic-clonic convulsions appreciably raises the risk to both mother and fetus. In an earlier report, Mattar and Sibai (2000) described outcomes in 399 consecutive women with eclampsia from 1977 through 1998. Major maternal complications included placental abruption-10 percent, neurological deicits-7 percent, aspiration pneumonia-7 percent, pulmonary edema-5 percent, cardiopulmonary arrest-4 percent, and acute renal failure4 percent."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"time": 100, "doc_ids": ["Obstentrics_Williams_7747", "Obstentrics_Williams_8118", "Obstentrics_Williams_7746", "pubmed23n0003_1971", "Obstentrics_Williams_5042", "Obstentrics_Williams_6551", "Obstentrics_Williams_5153", "pubmed23n0031_1455", "Obstentrics_Williams_6524", "Gynecology_Novak_5209", "pubmed23n0019_10680", "Obstentrics_Williams_3401", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3692", "Obstentrics_Williams_2151", "Obstentrics_Williams_3813", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1498", "Surgery_Schwartz_5346", "Gynecology_Novak_4331", "pubmed23n0023_5712", "pubmed23n0025_2859", "Obstentrics_Williams_6655", "Obstentrics_Williams_3655", "Obstentrics_Williams_4093", "Obstentrics_Williams_1", "pubmed23n0017_3532", "pubmed23n0031_9913", "Obstentrics_Williams_5624", "pubmed23n0001_2108", "Obstentrics_Williams_4187", "pubmed23n0029_32