{"topic": "Iron deficiency anemia", "competency": "Pathophysiology", "stem": "What is the primary mechanism by which iron deficiency anemia leads to impaired cellular defense mechanisms in children?", "options": {"A": "Impaired delayed hypersensitivity skin responses and decreased lymphocyte function", "B": "Increased bactericidal function and normal nitroblue tetrazolium dye reduction", "C": "Normal humoral and cellular defense mechanisms", "D": "Enhanced chemotactic activity and normal immunoglobulin concentrations"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Iron deficiency anemia can lead to impaired cellular defense mechanisms in children, characterized by impaired delayed hypersensitivity skin responses and decreased lymphocyte function, as well as decreased bactericidal function and increased chemotactic activity.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0037_10037", "title": "The immune response in iron-deficient children: Impaired cellular defense mechanisms with altered humoral components", "span_text": "Tests of lymphocyte function showed impaired delayed hypersensitivity skin responses in vivo and decreased in vitro H-3-thymidine incorporation following stimulation with phytohemagglutinin and candida antigen. Tests of neutrophil function showed normal nitroblue tetrazolium dye reduction, decreased bactericidal function, and increased chemotactic activity."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"time": 100, "method": "keyword search", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0047_13369", "InternalMed_Harrison_7953", "pubmed23n0060_15742", "pubmed23n0037_10037", "pubmed23n0052_18492", "InternalMed_Harrison_8001", "pubmed23n0010_7933", "InternalMed_Harrison_8000", "pubmed23n0012_9799", "First_Aid_Step1_412", "First_Aid_Step1_460", "Pathology_Robbins_3004", "pubmed23n0003_7426", "InternalMed_Harrison_22826", "pubmed23n0053_8948", "pubmed23n0043_17170", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3173", "pubmed23n0024_1740", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3192", "pubmed23n0056_15545", "pubmed23n0052_18