{"topic": "Sepsis", "competency": "Screening", "stem": "What is the primary goal of screening newborn infants for sickle cell disease in relation to sepsis?", "options": {"A": "To prevent pneumococcal sepsis by administering penicillin to infants with sickle cell disease", "B": "To diagnose sepsis early in infants with sickle cell disease", "C": "To determine the cost-effectiveness of penicillin prophylaxis", "D": "To identify infants at risk of developing anaphylaxis"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The primary goal of screening newborn infants for sickle cell disease is to identify those at risk of developing pneumococcal sepsis and to administer penicillin prophylaxis to prevent this complication.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0063_12645", "title": "Neonatal screening for sickle cell disease: a cost-effectiveness analysis", "span_text": "We developed a decision model that examined two strategies: (1) screening neonates and administering penicillin to infants found to have sickle cell disease in the hope of preventing pneumococcal sepsis, and (2) not screening but administering penicillin to infants after symptoms of sickle cell disease develop."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"search_terms": "sickle cell disease screening sepsis", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0063_12645"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0063_12645"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"verified_by": "medical_expert", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Sepsis Screening screening early detection risk assessment screening test sensitivity specificity guideline population screening high-risk preventive care follow-up diagnostic threshold", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 8941.096541006118, "llm_ms": 875.3212500014342, "verification_ms": 747.269999992568, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"