{"topic": "Epilepsy", "competency": "Etiology", "stem": "What factors can contribute to the etiology of epilepsy?", "options": {"A": "Genetic and environmental factors", "B": "Only genetic factors", "C": "Only environmental factors", "D": "Neither genetic nor environmental factors"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Epilepsy can be caused by a combination of genetic and environmental factors, including intrinsic physiologic processes and exogenous factors such as exposure to toxic substances and certain medications.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "InternalMed_Harrison_29759", "title": "InternalMed_Harrison", "span_text": "This implies there are important provocative or precipitating factors that induce seizures in patients with epilepsy. Similarly, precipitating factors are responsible for causing the single seizure in someone without epilepsy. Precipitants include those due to intrinsic physiologic processes such as psychological or physical stress, sleep deprivation, or hormonal changes associated with the menstrual cycle. They also include exogenous factors such as exposure to toxic substances and certain medications."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"time": 100, "doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_29759", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3803", "pubmed23n0063_5273", "pubmed23n0012_9688", "pubmed23n0027_1871", "Psichiatry_DSM-5_214", "pubmed23n0052_14923", "pubmed23n0004_10979", "InternalMed_Harrison_29860", "pubmed23n0050_5489", "InternalMed_Harrison_29795", "pubmed23n0052_14922", "pubmed23n0048_14965", "pubmed23n0021_1846"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_29759", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3803", "pubmed23n0063_5273", "pubmed23n0012_9688", "pubmed23n0027_1871", "Psichiatry_DSM-5_214", "pubmed23n0052_14923", "pubmed23n0004_10979", "InternalMed_Harrison_29860", "pubmed23n0050_5489", "InternalMed_Harrison_29795", "pubmed23n0052_14922", "pubmed23n0048_14965", "pubmed23n0021_1846"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"time": 200, "