{"topic": "Immunization", "competency": "Screening", "stem": "What is the relationship between immunization and subsequent use of public health clinics for well child care?", "options": {"A": "Immunization is associated with an increased percentage of infants who return to the clinics at two and three years of age.", "B": "Immunization has no effect on subsequent use of public health clinics for well child care.", "C": "Immunization is associated with a decreased percentage of infants who return to the clinics at two and three years of age.", "D": "The relationship between immunization and subsequent use of public health clinics for well child care is unknown."}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Immunization was associated with an increased percentage of infants who returned to the CHCs at two and three years of age.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0054_14129", "title": "Follow-up of the use of local health department clinics for preventive care among young children.", "span_text": "We followed 18,490 infants from their first visit to a county child health clinic (CHC) in Maryland through visits through their third year of age to investigate whether their continued use of the CHCs was related to their characteristics or to the services they were provided as an infant. We classified as provided services immunization, an Early and Periodic Screening, Diagnosis, and Treatment Program (EPSDT) recommended screening, and number of visits. Immunization was associated with an increased percentage of infants who returned to the CHCs at two and three years of age."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"source": "pubmed", "query": "immunization and public health clinics", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0054_14129", "First_Aid_Step2_237"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0054_14129", "First_Aid_Step2_237"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "manual review", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_histo