{"topic": "Appendicitis", "competency": "Prognosis", "stem": "What is the relationship between the normal appendectomy rate and the perforated appendicitis rate in patients suspected of having acute appendicitis?", "options": {"A": "There is a direct relationship between the two rates", "B": "There is an inverse relationship between the two rates", "C": "The rates are unrelated", "D": "The normal appendectomy rate always exceeds the perforated appendicitis rate"}, "answer_key": "B", "explanation": "The inverse relationship between the normal appendectomy rate and the perforated appendicitis rate suggests that as one rate increases, the other decreases. This is because a lower perforated appendicitis rate is often achieved by performing more appendectomies, which can result in a higher normal appendectomy rate.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0053_1832", "title": "Balancing the normal appendectomy rate with the perforated appendicitis rate: implications for quality assurance", "span_text": "There is an inverse relationship between the normal appendectomy rate and perforated appendicitis rate."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"source": "pubmed", "query": "appendicitis prognosis", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0053_1832", "Surgery_Schwartz_8806", "pubmed23n0034_664"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0053_1832", "Surgery_Schwartz_8806", "pubmed23n0034_664"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "manual review", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Appendicitis Prognosis prognosis outcomes mortality survival complications disease course recurrence risk stratification severity long-term outcomes quality of life predictors", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 8032.761332986411, "llm_ms": 925.2462500007823, "verification_ms": 819.3461250048131, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybr