{"topic": "Immunization", "competency": "Screening", "stem": "What is the primary method by which vaccines prevent future infections?", "options": {"A": "By mimicking infections and triggering an immune response", "B": "By providing a physical barrier against pathogens", "C": "By directly killing pathogens", "D": "By altering the host's DNA"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Vaccines work by mimicking infections, which triggers an immune response and leads to the formation of memory cells. These memory cells recognize and fight any future infection, thus preventing the disease.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "First_Aid_Step2_237", "title": "First_Aid_Step2", "span_text": "Vaccines work by mimicking infections and triggering an immune response in which memory cells are formed to recognize and fght any future infection."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"method": "database query", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0054_14129", "First_Aid_Step2_237"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0054_14129", "First_Aid_Step2_237"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "expert review", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Immunization Screening screening early detection risk assessment screening test sensitivity specificity guideline population screening high-risk preventive care follow-up diagnostic threshold", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 6469.600249954965, "llm_ms": 2628.10349999927, "verification_ms": 0.3208750276826322, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 0, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 1}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0054_14129", "First_Aid_Step2_237"], "evidence_reused": false}, {"attempt": 2, "reason": "v4_jso