{"topic": "GERD", "competency": "Treatment", "stem": "What is the first-line therapy for the treatment of GERD?", "options": {"A": "Antisecretory medication, usually PPIs", "B": "Endoscopic examination", "C": "Antireflux surgery", "D": "Lifestyle modifications"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The first-line therapy for the treatment of GERD is antisecretory medication, usually PPIs, as it helps to reduce acid secretions and alleviate symptoms.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Surgery_Schwartz_6929", "title": "Surgery_Schwartz", "span_text": "First-line therapy entails antisecretory medication, usually PPIs, in most patients."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"method": "keyword search", "doc_ids": ["Surgery_Schwartz_6929", "Gynecology_Novak_944", "Surgery_Schwartz_7803", "Gynecology_Novak_945"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Surgery_Schwartz_6929", "Gynecology_Novak_944", "Surgery_Schwartz_7803", "Gynecology_Novak_945"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "expert review", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "GERD Treatment treatment therapy management drug dosage medication pharmacotherapy first-line second-line guideline intervention acute management long-term management", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 8738.472125027329, "llm_ms": 943.3776249643415, "verification_ms": 820.8416249835864, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Surgery_Schwartz_6929", "Gynecology_Novak_944", "Surgery_Schwartz_7803", "Gynecology_Novak_945"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 100.0, "verification": 200.0, "llm": 943.3776249643415}}}
{"topic": "Smoking cessation", "competency": "Pharmacology", "stem": "What is the mechanism by which bupropion is helpful in smoking cessation?", "options": {"A": "Bupropion mimics nicotine's effects on dopamine and norepinephrine and may antagonize nicotinic receptors", "B": "Bupropion increases the release of inflammatory mediators, cytokines, and nitric oxide", "C": "Bupropion enhances the degradation of P450s", "D": "Bupropion has no effect on dopamine and norepinephrine"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Bupropion appears to be about as effective as nicotine patches in smoking cessation. The mechanism by which bupropion is helpful in this application is unknown, but the drug may mimic nicotine\u2019s effects on dopamine and norepinephrine and may antagonize nicotinic receptors.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Pharmacology_Katzung_3305", "title": "Pharmacology_Katzung", "span_text": "Bupropion was approved in 1997 as a treatment for smoking cessation. Approximately twice as many people treated with bupropion as with placebo have a reduced urge to smoke. In addition, patients taking bupropion appear to experience fewer mood symptoms and possibly less weight gain while withdrawing from nicotine dependence. Bupropion appears to be about as effective as nicotine patches in smoking cessation. The mechanism by which bupropion is helpful in this application is unknown, but the drug may mimic nicotine\u2019s effects on dopamine and norepinephrine and may antagonize nicotinic receptors."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"time": 100, "doc_ids": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_3608", "Pharmacology_Katzung_722", "Pharmacology_Katzung_717", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4925", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1051", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5775", "Pharmacology_Katzung_456", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3305", "pubmed23n0047_9520", "pubmed23n0051_19949", "pubmed23n0060_14091", "pubmed23n0062_4306"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_3608", "Pharmacology_Katzung_722", "Pharma