{"topic": "Dehydration", "competency": "Investigation", "stem": "A 25-year-old male patient presents with symptoms of excessive thirst and polyuria. What diagnostic test can be used to evaluate the relationship between plasma and urine osmolality in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Dehydration test", "B": "Water deprivation test", "C": "Vasopressin stimulation test", "D": "Glucose tolerance test"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The dehydration test is used to evaluate the relationship between plasma and urine osmolality, which can help diagnose conditions such as diabetes insipidus.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0052_14933", "title": "Vasopressin-sensitive diabetes insipidus. Case histories", "span_text": "Diagnosis was made on the basis of both dehydration test and sensitivity to exogenous vasopressin."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"method": "keyword search", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0052_14933"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0052_14933"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "manual review", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Dehydration Investigation investigation diagnostic test laboratory findings imaging workup evaluation differential diagnosis clinical criteria biomarkers screening confirmatory test diagnostic accuracy", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 7068.681208009366, "llm_ms": 1156.437790952623, "verification_ms": 780.4942500079051, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0052_14933"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 100.0, "verification": 200.0, "llm": 1156.437790952623}}}
{"topic": "Heart failure", "competency": "Screening", "stem": "What is the primary consideration when evaluating the results of exercise testing for coronary artery disease in asymptomatic men?", "options": {"A": "The test's high sensitivity and specificity", "B": "The test's ability to separate false positives from true positives with certainty", "C": "The predictive value limitations and the false-positive problem", "D": "The test's ability to rule out coronary artery disease with absolute certainty"}, "answer_key": "C", "explanation": "The primary consideration when evaluating the results of exercise testing for coronary artery disease in asymptomatic men is the predictive value limitations and the false-positive problem, as the test has a sensitivity of approximately 60% and a specificity of approximately 90%, and there is no second line of noninvasive studies that can separate an exercise-test false positive from a true positive with certainty.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0028_12412", "title": "The detection of asympatomatic coronary artery disease", "span_text": "In summary, near-maximal or maximal exercise testing has a sensitivity of approximately 60% and a specificity of approximately 90% for coronary atherosclerotic heart disease. When screening asympatomatic men with exercise testing, an abnormal response identifies a group of men at very high risk for coronary artery disease. However, the predictive value limitations are obvious and the false-positive problem must be realized."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"time": 100, "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0028_12412", "InternalMed_Harrison_10756", "pubmed23n0058_693", "pubmed23n0053_5136", "pubmed23n0047_9261", "pubmed23n0024_423", "pubmed23n0018_7002", "pubmed23n0059_18931", "pubmed23n0063_9823", "pubmed23n0047_4026", "pubmed23n0055_21475", "pubmed23n0013_7729", "pubmed23n0020_5171", "pubmed23n0020_6943", "InternalMed_Harrison_6072", "Pediatrics_Nelson_130", "pubmed23n0048_1062