{"topic": "Schizophrenia", "competency": "Complications", "stem": "What is the relationship between obstetric complications and the onset of schizophrenia?", "options": {"A": "Obstetric complications are associated with a younger age at onset of schizophrenia", "B": "Obstetric complications have no effect on the onset of schizophrenia", "C": "Obstetric complications are associated with a later age at onset of schizophrenia", "D": "Obstetric complications are only associated with the severity of schizophrenia"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The presence of one or more obstetric complications recorded in maternity notes of patients with schizophrenia was significantly more likely than controls, and these complications were associated with a younger age at onset of illness.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0049_10101", "title": "Risk of schizophrenia in adults born after obstetric complications and their association with early onset of illness: a controlled study", "span_text": "Patients with schizophrenia, particularly males, have an excess of obstetric complications in their early developmental histories, and such complications are associated with a younger age at onset of their disease."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"source": "pubmed", "query": "obstetric complications and schizophrenia", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0054_5508", "pubmed23n0049_10101", "pubmed23n0022_10489", "pubmed23n0056_1432", "pubmed23n0047_5343", "pubmed23n0062_650", "pubmed23n0016_11256"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0054_5508", "pubmed23n0049_10101", "pubmed23n0022_10489", "pubmed23n0056_1432", "pubmed23n0047_5343", "pubmed23n0062_650", "pubmed23n0016_11256"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "manual review", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Schizophrenia Complications complications adverse outcomes sequelae comorbidity disease progression risk factors mortality morbidity hospitalization long-term effects severity clinical deterioration", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 9377.145000034943, "llm_ms": 1095.961042039562, "verification_ms": 983.2395830308087, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0054_5508", "pubmed23n0049_10101", "pubmed23n0022_10489", "pubmed23n0056_1432", "pubmed23n0047_5343", "pubmed23n0062_650", "pubmed23n0016_11256"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 100.0, "verification": 200.0, "llm": 1095.961042039562}}}
{"topic": "Acne", "competency": "Diagnosis", "stem": "What is usually not necessary to diagnose acne?", "options": {"A": "Laboratory studies and imaging studies", "B": "Topical keratolytic agents and topical antibiotics", "C": "Screening tests for hyperandrogenism", "D": "Creams, lotions, gels, foams, and solutions"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The diagnosis of acne is usually not difficult because of the characteristic and chronic lesions, and laboratory studies and imaging studies are usually not necessary.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Pediatrics_Nelson_4024", "title": "Pediatrics_Nelson", "span_text": "Laboratory studies and imaging studies are usually not necessary to diagnose acne."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"method": "keyword search", "doc_ids": ["Pediatrics_Nelson_4024"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Pediatrics_Nelson_4024"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "expert review", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Acne Diagnosis diagnosis differential diagnosis signs symptoms screening clinical features presentation diagnostic criteria workup evaluation diagnostic test laboratory findings imaging", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 5152.763207966927, "llm_ms": 978.803624981083, "verification_ms": 919.4635419989936, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Pediatrics_Nelson_4024"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 10.0, "verification": 20.0, "llm": 978.803624981083}}}
{"topic": "Urinary tract infection", "competency": "Therapy", "stem": "What is the recommended duration of antibiotic therapy for acute uncomplicated urinary tract infections?", "options": {"A": "3 days", "B": "7 to 14 days", "C": "Single shot therapy", "D": "Long-term suppressive therapy"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Acute uncomplicated urinary tract infections can be cured with antibiotics for 3 days.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0060_11281", "title": "Therapy of urinary tract infection", "span_text": "Acute uncomplicated urinary tract infections which do not respond to general measures as increased fluid intake will be cured with antibiotics for 3 days."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"source": "pubmed", "query": "urinary tract infection therapy", "doc_ids": ["Gynecology_Novak_3795", "InternalMed_Harrison_25125", "pubmed23n0008_15301", "pubmed23n0043_7574", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5209", "Surgery_Schwartz_11808", "pubmed23n0043_21016", "pubmed23n0024_6678", "pubmed23n0036_8618", "pubmed23n0060_11281", "pubmed23n0043_7579", "InternalMed_Harrison_25535", "Gynecology_Novak_2474", "pubmed23n0058_3919", "pubmed23n0027_706", "pubmed23n0013_5376", "InternalMed_Harrison_10179", "InternalMed_Harrison_10479", "Surgery_Schwartz_4220", "InternalMed_Harrison_7480", "pubmed23n0050_12565", "pubmed23n0061_3486", "pubmed23n0024_5796", "InternalMed_Harrison_532", "pubmed23n0056_13656", "pubmed23n0030_10409", "pubmed23n0059_20806", "pubmed23n0039_6486", "pubmed23n0023_1035", "pubmed23n0038_9136", "pubmed23n0054_2462", "pubmed23n0023_10207", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3511", "pubmed23n0037_1822", "pubmed23n0047_5576", "pubmed23n0032_5758", "pubmed23n0044_9236", "pubmed23n0013_8070", "pubmed23n0016_10524", "pubmed23n0002_11704", "pubmed23n0041_11363", "Surgery_Schwartz_11662", "Surgery_Schwartz_5014", "pubmed23n0060_2581", "pubmed23n0050_9647", "pubmed23n0049_13820", "pubmed23n0058_8896", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5731", "pubmed23n0014_4173", "pubmed23