{"topic": "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease", "competency": "Pathophysiology", "stem": "What is the primary mechanism of salt and water accumulation in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?", "options": {"A": "Increased cardiac output", "B": "Decreased pulmonary vascular resistance", "C": "Increased right atrial and mean pulmonary arterial pressures", "D": "Decreased systemic vascular resistance"}, "answer_key": "C", "explanation": "The pathogenesis of salt and water accumulation in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is unclear and may differ from that in patients with congestive heart failure due to myocardial disease. Studies have shown that patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease have increased right atrial and mean pulmonary arterial pressures, which may contribute to the accumulation of salt and water.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0054_3031", "title": "Pathogenesis of congestive state in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease", "span_text": "The pathogenesis of salt and water accumulation in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is unclear and may differ from that in patients with congestive heart failure due to myocardial disease. This study was undertaken to investigate some of the mechanisms involved. Hemodynamics, water and electrolyte spaces, renal function, and plasma hormone concentrations were measured in nine patients with edema due to chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and in six patients after recovery. Mean cardiac output (3.8 +/- 0.26 l/min.m2) was normal, but right atrial (11 +/- 1 mm Hg) and mean pulmonary arterial (41 +/- 3 mm Hg) pressures were increased."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"time": 100, "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0054_3031", "Physiology_Levy_2804", "pubmed23n0060_1503", "pubmed23n0060_17161", "First_Aid_Step1_845", "InternalMed_Harrison_3815", "pubmed23n0016_5489", "InternalMed_Harrison_27371", "Pathology_Robbins_3324", "pubmed23n0060_