{"topic": "Hepatitis C", "competency": "Monitoring", "stem": "What should be monitored in patients with hepatitis C during treatment?", "options": {"A": "Liver function and signs of drug-induced hepatitis", "B": "Cardiovascular events and lymphomas", "C": "Mucocutaneous reactions and cytopenias", "D": "Interstitial pneumonitis and gastrointestinal events"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Patients with hepatitis C should be monitored for drug toxicity, signs and symptoms of drug-induced hepatitis, and liver function during treatment.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "InternalMed_Harrison_13413", "title": "InternalMed_Harrison", "span_text": "During treatment, patients should be monitored for drug toxicity. The most common adverse reaction of significance is hepatitis. Patients should be carefully educated about the signs and symptoms of drug-induced hepatitis (e.g., dark urine, loss of appetite) and should be instructed to discontinue treatment promptly and see their health care provider should these symptoms occur."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"source": "textbook", "query": "hepatitis C monitoring", "doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_13413", "InternalMed_Harrison_13590", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4007", "pubmed23n0052_10942", "pubmed23n0045_20591", "pubmed23n0033_2900", "pubmed23n0052_8817"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_13413", "InternalMed_Harrison_13590", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4007", "pubmed23n0052_10942", "pubmed23n0045_20591", "pubmed23n0033_2900", "pubmed23n0052_8817"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "manual review", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Hepatitis C Monitoring monitoring follow-up treatment response labs imaging vital signs disease control therapeutic monitoring adverse effects dose adjustment clinical assessment outcome tracking", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_i