{"topic": "Migraine", "competency": "Pathophysiology", "stem": "What is the primary mechanism underlying the pathophysiology of migraine?", "options": {"A": "Vasoconstriction followed by reactive vasodilation", "B": "Central mechanisms involving neurogenic inflammation", "C": "Excess muscle contraction", "D": "Hypothalamic activation alone"}, "answer_key": "B", "explanation": "Recent evidence suggests that migraine may not be due to vasoconstriction followed by reactive vasodilation, and the pain of migraine may be generated centrally or enhanced by neurogenic inflammation.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0052_2782", "title": "Advances in understanding the pathophysiology of headache", "span_text": "Recent evidence suggests that migraine may not be due to vasoconstriction followed by reactive vasodilation, and tension-type headache may not be due to excess muscle contraction. The prodromes of migraine may have a hypothalamic origin, and the aura and changes in cognition may have a cortical neuronal origin. The pain of migraine and tension-type headache may be generated centrally or enhanced or generated by neurogenic inflammation."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"source": "pubmed", "query": "migraine pathophysiology", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0062_3283", "pubmed23n0044_6073", "pubmed23n0052_2782", "InternalMed_Harrison_32366", "Neurology_Adams_1525", "pubmed23n0052_2778", "InternalMed_Harrison_30152", "pubmed23n0061_5231", "pubmed23n0050_1590", "InternalMed_Harrison_30121", "Neurology_Adams_6131", "pubmed23n0046_16862", "Neurology_Adams_1348", "Psichiatry_DSM-5_1624", "pubmed23n0039_8450", "pubmed23n0057_19597", "pubmed23n0046_5403", "pubmed23n0050_1589", "pubmed23n0054_7302", "pubmed23n0063_4421", "pubmed23n0052_16569", "pubmed23n0034_4324", "pubmed23n0044_20762", "pubmed23n0050_1592", "pubmed23n0002_9407", "pubmed23n0045_6951", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1710", "pubmed23n0050_4253", "pubmed23n0050_1595", "pubmed23n0058_6321"], "evidence_