{"topic": "Tuberculosis", "competency": "Complications", "stem": "What is a major cause of right iliac fossa pathology, sometimes associated with malabsorption, in tropical countries?", "options": {"A": "Ileocaecal tuberculosis", "B": "Amoeboma", "C": "Enteric fever", "D": "Peptic ulcer disease"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Ileocaecal tuberculosis is a major cause of right iliac fossa pathology, sometimes associated with malabsorption, in tropical countries.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0059_757", "title": "Gastroenterological emergencies in the tropics.", "span_text": "Ileocaecal tuberculosis is a major cause of right iliac fossa pathology--sometimes associated with malabsorption; amoeboma is an important clinical differential diagnosis."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"time": 100, "doc_ids": ["Obstentrics_Williams_7116", "pubmed23n0059_757", "pubmed23n0018_492", "pubmed23n0063_19914", "Obstentrics_Williams_7112", "pubmed23n0039_8091", "pubmed23n0041_8487"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Obstentrics_Williams_7116", "pubmed23n0059_757", "pubmed23n0018_492", "pubmed23n0063_19914", "Obstentrics_Williams_7112", "pubmed23n0039_8091", "pubmed23n0041_8487"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"time": 50, "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Tuberculosis Complications complications adverse outcomes sequelae comorbidity disease progression risk factors mortality morbidity hospitalization long-term effects severity clinical deterioration", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 81694.03258402599, "llm_ms": 1900.6980420090258, "verification_ms": 1017.6816250314005, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "fa