{"topic": "Rheumatoid arthritis", "competency": "Pathophysiology", "stem": "What is the primary mechanism of tissue destruction in rheumatoid arthritis?", "options": {"A": "Production of metalloproteinases and their inhibitors in synovium", "B": "Activation of complement by the classical pathway", "C": "Release of chemotactic factors after activation of the complement cascade by the alternate pathway", "D": "Autocrine secretion of peptide growth factors"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Rheumatoid arthritis is a chronic inflammatory disease characterized by synovial inflammation and destruction of articular extracellular matrix. The production of metalloproteinases and their inhibitors in synovium is a key factor in the pathogenesis of tissue destruction.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0053_11667", "title": "Mechanisms of tissue destruction and cellular activation in rheumatoid arthritis", "span_text": "Rheumatoid arthritis is a chronic inflammatory disease of unknown etiology that is marked by synovial inflammation and destruction of articular extracellular matrix. Several studies of the pathogenesis of tissue destruction have focused on the production of metalloproteinases and their inhibitors in synovium as determinants of joint preservation."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"time": 100, "doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_3815", "pubmed23n0006_3694", "pubmed23n0051_2389", "pubmed23n0053_11667", "Pharmacology_Katzung_2149", "pubmed23n0036_3596", "pubmed23n0057_15261", "pubmed23n0025_9583", "pubmed23n0050_6566", "pubmed23n0040_7158", "Immunology_Janeway_3819", "pubmed23n0052_20677", "Pathology_Robbins_4914", "pubmed23n0023_901", "pubmed23n0051_19702", "pubmed23n0055_22754", "pubmed23n0054_172", "pubmed23n0002_12372", "pubmed23n0036_1594", "pubmed23n0058_16371", "pubmed23n0062_5844", "Pharmacology_Katzung_7182", "pubmed23n0028_8845", "pubmed23n0041_2231", "pubmed23n0002_11", "pubmed23n0051_12222", "pubmed23n0012_4495", "pubmed23