{"topic": "Diabetes mellitus", "competency": "Pharmacology", "stem": "What is the primary mechanism of action of colesevelam hydrochloride in the treatment of type 2 diabetes?", "options": {"A": "Interruption of the enterohepatic circulation and decrease in farnesoid X receptor activation", "B": "Competitive inhibition of HMG-CoA reductase", "C": "Stimulation of insulin release", "D": "Decrease in glucagon levels"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Colesevelam hydrochloride is a bile acid sequestrant that is approved as an antihyperglycemic therapy for persons with type 2 diabetes. Its exact mechanism of action is unknown but presumed to involve an interruption of the enterohepatic circulation and a decrease in farnesoid X receptor activation.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Pharmacology_Katzung_4747", "title": "Pharmacology_Katzung", "span_text": "Colesevelam hydrochloride, the bile acid sequestrant and cholesterol-lowering drug, is approved as an antihyperglycemic therapy for persons with type 2 diabetes who are taking other medications or have not achieved adequate control with diet and exercise. The exact mechanism of action is unknown but presumed to involve an interruption of the enterohepatic circulation and a decrease in farnesoid X receptor (FXR) activation."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"time": 100, "doc_ids": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_3608", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3212", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4762", "pubmed23n0050_12703", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4747", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4404", "pubmed23n0031_9064"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_3608", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3212", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4762", "pubmed23n0050_12703", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4747", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4404", "pubmed23n0031_9064"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"time": 50, "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Diabetes mellitus Pharmacology pharmacology drug mechanism indication contraindication adverse effects drug interaction dosage pharmacokinetics pharmacodynamics monitoring toxicity therapeutic target", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 39122.87349998951, "llm_ms": 935.8788329991512, "verification_ms": 827.8160000336356, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_3608", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3212", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4762", "pubmed23n0050_12703", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4747", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4404", "pubmed23n0031_9064"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"total": 150.0, "retrieval": 39122.87349998951, "llm": 935.8788329991512, "verification": 827.8160000336356}}}
{"topic": "Pregnancy", "competency": "Monitoring", "stem": "What is the primary tool for population-based surveillance of the condition of infants at birth and maternal status during pregnancy?", "options": {"A": "Pregnancy Risk Assessment Monitoring System", "B": "Birth certificate", "C": "Electronic fetal heart rate monitoring", "D": "Ultrasound monitoring"}, "answer_key": "B", "explanation": "The birth certificate is the primary tool for population-based surveillance of the condition of infants at birth and maternal status during pregnancy.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0062_19816", "title": "The Pregnancy Risk Assessment Monitoring System: design, questionnaire, data collection and response rates", "span_text": "The birth certificate, the primary tool for population-based surveillance of the condition of infants at birth and maternal status during pregnancy, provides little data about maternal behaviour during pregnancy."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"time": 100, "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0062_19816", "Obstentrics_Williams_6959", "InternalMed_Harrison_576", "pubmed23n0018_2", "pubmed23n0060_8769", "pubmed23n0017_5426", "pubmed23n0003_6323", "First_Aid_Step2_725", "Obstentrics_Williams_7184", "pubmed23n0048_18176", "pubmed23n0043_1774", "pubmed23n0003_10673", "pubmed23n0062_10388", "Obstentrics_Williams_6715", "pubmed23n0044_24498", "pubmed23n0048_20060", "Obstentrics_Williams_4438", "pubmed23n0060_10986", "InternalMed_Harrison_9387", "Obstentrics_Williams_2298", "First_Aid_Step1_472", "pubmed23n0001_2951", "Obstentrics_Williams_1069", "pubmed23n0058_21374", "pubmed23n0046_24008"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0062_19816", "Obstentrics_Williams_6959", "InternalMed_Harrison_576", "pubmed23n0018_2", "pubmed23n0060_8769", "pubmed23n0017_5426", "pubmed23n0003_6323", "First_Aid_Step2_725", "Obstentrics_Williams_7184", "pubmed23n0048_18176", "pubmed23n0043_1774", "pubmed23n0003_10673", "pubmed23n0062_10388", "Obstentrics_Williams_67