{"topic": "Depression", "competency": "Pathophysiology", "stem": "What role do sex steroids play in the pathophysiology of depression?", "options": {"A": "They have no effect on depression", "B": "Estrogen deficiency states are thought to play a role in the etiology of depression in some women", "C": "Testosterone deficiency in men is never associated with depressive symptoms", "D": "Hormone replacement therapy has no effect on mood and depressive symptoms"}, "answer_key": "B", "explanation": "Sex steroids, such as estrogen and testosterone, are implicated in the pathophysiology of depression. Estrogen deficiency states, which occur in the postpartum and postmenopausal periods, are thought to play a role in the etiology of depression in some women. Likewise, severe testosterone deficiency in men is sometimes associated with depressive symptoms.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Pharmacology_Katzung_3259", "title": "Pharmacology_Katzung", "span_text": "Finally, sex steroids are also implicated in the pathophysiology of depression. Estrogen deficiency states, which occur in the postpartum and postmenopausal periods, are thought to play a role in the etiology of depression in some women. Likewise, severe testosterone deficiency in men is sometimes associated with depressive symptoms."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"source": "textbook", "id": "Pharmacology_Katzung_3259", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0045_9755", "pubmed23n0054_21134", "pubmed23n0052_6086", "InternalMed_Harrison_20981", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3240", "pubmed23n0050_18868", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3259", "pubmed23n0050_14868", "pubmed23n0020_9384", "pubmed23n0047_4583", "pubmed23n0055_6038"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0045_9755", "pubmed23n0054_21134", "pubmed23n0052_6086", "InternalMed_Harrison_20981", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3240", "pubmed23n0050_18868", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3259", "pubmed23n0050_14868", "pubmed23n0020_9384", "pubmed23n0047_4583", "pubmed23n0055_6038"], "retrieval_mod