{"topic": "Rheumatoid arthritis", "competency": "Prognosis", "stem": "What is the long-term outcome for most patients with rheumatoid arthritis in clinical settings?", "options": {"A": "Most patients have no evidence of disease 3 to 5 years later", "B": "More than 90% of patients have evidence of disease 3 to 5 years later, generally with progression", "C": "The disease remains static with no progression", "D": "The disease goes into remission"}, "answer_key": "B", "explanation": "In clinical settings, more than 90% of patients with rheumatoid arthritis have evidence of disease 3 to 5 years later, generally with progression, unlike in epidemiological studies where 75% of individuals who meet the 1958 American Rheumatism Association criteria for RA have no evidence of disease 3 to 5 years later.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0050_6011", "title": "The paradox of effective therapies but poor long-term outcomes in rheumatoid arthritis", "span_text": "In epidemiological studies of large populations, 75% of individuals who meet the 1958 American Rheumatism Association (ARA) criteria for RA have no evidence of disease 3 to 5 years later; in contrast, in clinical settings more than 90% of patients with RA have evidence of disease 3 to 5 years later, generally with progression."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"time": 100, "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0050_6011", "pubmed23n0053_11668", "pubmed23n0061_9530", "pubmed23n0043_10192", "pubmed23n0050_6783", "pubmed23n0060_6096", "pubmed23n0058_17523", "pubmed23n0052_6909", "pubmed23n0058_18345", "pubmed23n0049_8622", "pubmed23n0045_1659", "pubmed23n0049_8621", "pubmed23n0062_6891", "pubmed23n0025_6262", "pubmed23n0054_9", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4093", "pubmed23n0062_13029", "pubmed23n0055_11722", "Pediatrics_Nelson_2551", "Obstentrics_Williams_8302", "pubmed23n0007_7707", "pubmed23n0054_3126", "pubmed23n0058_11370", "Pathology_Robbins_4853", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1955", "pubmed23n0050_899", "pubmed23n0027_