{"topic": "GERD", "competency": "Therapy", "stem": "A patient with GERD symptoms that are not adequately controlled by medication may require which of the following treatment options?", "options": {"A": "Endoscopic examination and possible antireflux surgery", "B": "Increased dosage of antacids", "C": "Immediate surgical intervention to prevent rupture of the distal gastric staple line", "D": "Percutaneous decompression of the distal stomach"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "For patients with GERD symptoms that are not adequately controlled by medication, endoscopic examination is recommended to assess the degree of mucosal injury and presence of Barrett's esophagus, and treatment options may include antireflux surgery.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Surgery_Schwartz_6929", "title": "Surgery_Schwartz", "span_text": "Failure of medication to adequately control GERD symptoms suggests either that the patient may have relatively severe dis-ease or a non-GERD cause for his or her symptoms. Endoscopic examination at this stage of the patient\u2019s evaluation is recom-mended and will provide the opportunity to assess the degree of mucosal injury and presence of BE."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"time": 100, "doc_ids": ["Surgery_Schwartz_6929", "Gynecology_Novak_944", "Surgery_Schwartz_7803", "pubmed23n0048_14236"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Surgery_Schwartz_6929", "Gynecology_Novak_944", "Surgery_Schwartz_7803", "pubmed23n0048_14236"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"time": 200, "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "GERD Therapy therapy treatment management drug dosage medication pharmacotherapy first-line second-line guideline intervention acute management long-term management", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 50523.42983300332, "llm_ms": 880.5354160140269, "verification_ms": 912.586499