{"topic": "Pneumonia", "competency": "Monitoring", "stem": "How should the therapeutic response of a patient with pneumonia be monitored?", "options": {"A": "Microbiologically or clinically, including follow-up cultures and assessment of systemic manifestations of infection", "B": "Only through clinical findings, such as clearing of radiographic infiltrates", "C": "Only through microbiological methods, such as cultures of specimens taken from infected sites", "D": "Through patient self-reporting of symptoms"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The therapeutic response of a patient with pneumonia can be monitored microbiologically or clinically. Microbiological monitoring involves taking cultures of specimens from infected sites to document eradication of the pathogen, while clinical monitoring involves assessing the patient's systemic manifestations of infection, such as malaise, fever, and leukocytosis, as well as clinical findings like radiographic infiltrates.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Pharmacology_Katzung_5618", "title": "Pharmacology_Katzung", "span_text": "The therapeutic response may be monitored microbiologically or clinically. Cultures of specimens taken from infected sites should eventually become sterile or demonstrate eradication of the pathogen and are useful for documenting recurrence or relapse. Follow-up cultures may also be useful for detecting superinfections or the development of resistance. Clinically, the patient\u2019s systemic manifestations of infection (malaise, fever, leukocytosis) should abate, and the clinical findings should improve (eg, as shown by clearing of radiographic infiltrates or lessening hypoxemia in pneumonia)."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"method": "keyword search", "doc_ids": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_5618", "InternalMed_Harrison_22965", "pubmed23n0058_20553", "pubmed23n0055_17990", "pubmed23n0051_2023"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_5618", "InternalMed_Harrison_22965", "pubmed23n0058_20553", "pubmed23n0055_17990", "pubmed23n0051_2023"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "expert review", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Pneumonia Monitoring monitoring follow-up treatment response labs imaging vital signs disease control therapeutic monitoring adverse effects dose adjustment clinical assessment outcome tracking", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 8218.348791997414, "llm_ms": 943.5249579837546, "verification_ms": 865.3573329793289, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_5618", "InternalMed_Harrison_22965", "pubmed23n0058_20553", "pubmed23n0055_17990", "pubmed23n0051_2023"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 100.0, "verification": 200.0, "llm": 943.5249579837546}}}
{"topic": "Dermatitis", "competency": "Prevention", "stem": "What is a potential preventive strategy against pyoderma?", "options": {"A": "Using bentonite", "B": "Applying topical corticosteroids", "C": "Taking anticoagulant agents", "D": "Using antifungal medications"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Bentonite has been shown to have a strong binding affinity for staphylococci, leading to significant reductions in viable colony counts, and pre-treatment with bentonite prevented lesion development in a murine pyoderma model.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed_web", "doc_id": "pubmed_web_41576001", "title": "Preventive effect of bentonite against pyoderma via direct binding capability of staphylococci", "span_text": "Bentonite, a naturally occurring clay mineral, is widely used for detoxifying mycotoxins and chemical contaminants, yet its potential interactions with microorganisms remain poorly characterized. Staphylococci, including Staphylococcus aureus in humans and Staphylococcus pseudintermedius in dogs, are major contributors to impetigo or pyoderma and other skin diseases, with rising antibiotic resistance underscoring the need for novel preventive strategies."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"time": 100, "doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41576050", "pubmed_web_41576001", "pubmed_web_41569421", "pubmed_web_41567565", "pubmed_web_41563775", "pubmed_web_41562057", "pubmed_web_41561204", "pubmed_web_41560442", "pubmed_web_41555934", "pubmed_web_41552868", "pubmed_web_41552255", "pubmed_web_41552152", "pubmed_web_41551010", "pubmed_web_41543192", "pubmed_web_41538092", "pubmed_web_41537297", "pubmed_web_41532096", "pubmed_web_41532048", "pubmed_web_41526643", "pubmed_web_41524634"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41576050", "pubmed_web_41576001", "pubmed_web_41569421", "pubmed_web_41567565", "pubmed_web_41563775", "pubmed_web_41562057", "pubmed_web_41561204", "pubmed_web_41560442", "pubmed_web_41555934", "pubmed_web_41552868", "pubmed_web_41552255", "pubmed_we