{"topic": "Hyperlipidemia", "competency": "Treatment", "stem": "What is the primary approach to managing hyperlipidemia in patients with familial dysbetalipoproteinemia?", "options": {"A": "Pharmacologic therapy with statins as the first line of treatment", "B": "Dietary modification with a low-cholesterol, low-fat diet", "C": "Lifestyle changes including weight reduction and exercise", "D": "Combination therapy with cholesterol absorption inhibitors and fibrates"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Patients with familial dysbetalipoproteinemia are typically diet-responsive, but pharmacologic therapy is often required, with statins being the first line of treatment.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "InternalMed_Harrison_28216", "title": "InternalMed_Harrison", "span_text": "Because FDBL is associated with increased risk of premature ASCVD, it should be treated aggressively. Other metabolic conditions that can worsen the hyperlipidemia (see above) should be managed. Patients with FDBL are typically diet-responsive and can respond favorably to weight reduction and to low-cholesterol, low-fat diets. Alcohol intake should be curtailed. Pharmacologic therapy is often required, and statins are the first line in management."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"method": "keyword search", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0058_16219", "InternalMed_Harrison_28216", "pubmed23n0062_12872", "pubmed23n0012_1749", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1218"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0058_16219", "InternalMed_Harrison_28216", "pubmed23n0062_12872", "pubmed23n0012_1749", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1218"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "expert review", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Hyperlipidemia Treatment treatment therapy management drug dosage medication pharmacotherapy first-line second-line guideline intervention acute management long-term management", "evi