{"topic": "Atrial fibrillation", "competency": "Pharmacology", "stem": "What is the primary mechanism of action of amiodarone in the treatment of atrial fibrillation?", "options": {"A": "Blocks IKr, INa, ICa-L channels, \u03b2-adrenoceptors", "B": "Directly inhibits activity of free and clot-associated thrombin", "C": "Activates antithrombin, which inhibits action of IIa and factor Xa", "D": "Decreases conduction velocity, ERP, and PR interval"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Amiodarone is a class III antiarrhythmic agent that works by blocking multiple ion channels, including IKr, INa, and ICa-L, as well as \u03b2-adrenoceptors, which helps to prolong the action potential duration and QT interval, slowing heart rate and AV node conduction.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Pharmacology_Katzung_1479", "title": "Pharmacology_Katzung", "span_text": "CLASS 3 \u2022AmiodaroneBlocks IKr, INa, ICa-L channels, \u03b2adrenoceptors Prolongs action potential duration andQTinterval\u2022slowsheartrateandAVnodeconduction\u2022lowincidence of torsades de pointes"}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"time": 100, "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0013_7562", "Pharmacology_Katzung_287", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3801", "First_Aid_Step1_339", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1422", "First_Aid_Step1_472", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1457", "pubmed23n0047_9542", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1479", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1438", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1403", "Pharmacology_Katzung_332", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1455", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1488", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1432", "pubmed23n0002_11420", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3800", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3793", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4316", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1460", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1164", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1315", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1434", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1284", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1442", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1480", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1456"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0013_7562", "Pharmacology_Katzung_287", "Pharmacology_Katzung_380