{"topic": "Schizophrenia", "competency": "Prognosis", "stem": "A 24-year-old man is diagnosed with schizophrenia after a first psychotic episode. He had good premorbid functioning, acute onset of symptoms, and predominantly positive symptoms with few negative symptoms. Which of the following is most strongly associated with a better long-term prognosis in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Presence of few negative symptoms", "B": "History of suicidal behavior", "C": "Family history of schizophrenia", "D": "Early onset of delusions"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The presence of few negative symptoms is strongly associated with a better long-term prognosis in schizophrenia. Patients with fewer negative symptoms tend to have good premorbid functioning, acute onset, and intermittent early course, all of which are linked to improved outcomes. In contrast, prominent negative symptoms such as anhedonia and affective flattening are more predictive of poor long-term functional outcome.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0058_9445", "title": "Natural history of schizophrenia subtypes. II. Positive and negative symptoms and long-term course.", "span_text": "Schizophrenia with few negative symptoms was associated with good premorbid functioning, acute onset, intermittent early course, and a better prognosis. Positive symptoms predicted future hospitalizations but were less powerful and specific as indicators of differential illness history, course, and long-term functional incapacity. As predictors of long-term outcome, negative symptoms were of greater value measured at index admission several years after illness onset than at first hospital admission."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "prognostic factors in schizophrenia long-term outcome", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0002_1370", "pubmed23n0021_3311", "pubmed23n0026_3405", "pubmed23n0026_2302", "pubmed23n0058_9445", "Psichiatry_DSM-5_541", "pubmed23n0060_3460", "pubmed23n0054_21135", "pubmed23n0024_9197", "pubmed23n0001_5400"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0002_1370", "pubmed23n0021_3311", "pubmed23n0026_3405", "pubmed23n0026_2302", "pubmed23n0058_9445", "Psichiatry_DSM-5_541", "pubmed23n0060_3460", "pubmed23n0054_21135", "pubmed23n0024_9197", "pubmed23n0001_5400"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "evidence_match", "score": "high", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Schizophrenia Prognosis prognosis outcomes mortality survival complications disease course recurrence risk stratification severity long-term outcomes quality of life predictors", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 11820.084250008222, "llm_ms": 1241.886916977819, "verification_ms": 3392.7060840069316, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0002_1370", "pubmed23n0021_3311", "pubmed23n0026_3405", "pubmed23n0026_2302", "pubmed23n0058_9445", "Psichiatry_DSM-5_541", "pubmed23n0060_3460", "pubmed23n0054_21135", "pubmed23n0024_9197", "pubmed23n0001_5400"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 65.0, "total": 185.0, "llm": 1241.886916977819, "verification": 3392.7060840069316}}}
{"topic": "Prostate cancer", "competency": "Prognosis", "stem": "A 68-year-old man is diagnosed with metastatic prostate cancer involving multiple bones. Bone scintigraphy shows extensive lesions, and his serum alkaline phosphatase is elevated. Which factor is most strongly associated with improved cancer-specific survival in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Normalization of alkaline phosphatase levels in conjunction with bone scintigraphy findings", "B": "Younger age at diagnosis", "C": "Initial response to hormonal therapy alone", "D": "Low erythrocyte sedimentation rate"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The combination of bone scintigraphy and alkaline phosphatase levels, referred to as the EOD score, has been shown to be superior in predicting cancer-specific survival compared to other factors such as age, histologic grade, or individual lab values. This score improves accuracy by distinguishing active from nonactive bone metastases.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0053_5927", "title": "Significance of a new stratification of alkaline phosphatase and extent of disease in patients with prostate carcinoma with bone metastasis.", "span_text": "Multivariate analysis revealed that the EOD score was better than the new EOD grade. This improvement was due to the elimination of false-positive or nonactive metastatic bone lesions on bone scintigraphy through the alkaline phosphatase evaluation."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "prognostic factors in metastatic prostate cancer bone involvement", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0063_10697", "pubmed23n0063_15760", "pubmed23n0053_5927", "pubmed23n0046_16684", "pubmed23n0056_20847", "pubmed23n0045_18891", "pubmed23n0061_1040", "pubmed23n0063_8505", "pubmed23n0015_666", "pubmed23n0063_12841", "pubmed23n0054_16380", "pubmed23n0056_20844", "pubmed23n0046_3326", "Surgery_Schwartz_9248", "pubmed23n0061_732", "InternalMed_Harrison_534", "pubmed23n0062_13842", "pubmed23n0054_10173", "pubmed23n00