{"topic": "Myocardial infarction", "competency": "Treatment", "stem": "A 68-year-old man presents to the emergency department with chest pain and is diagnosed with acute myocardial infarction. Which of the following interventions is most effective in limiting myocardial damage when administered early?", "options": {"A": "Thrombolytic therapy initiated within 45 minutes of arrival", "B": "Long-term beta-blockade started one week after discharge", "C": "NSAIDs for pain relief and inflammation control", "D": "Elective angiography after stabilization"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Thrombolytic therapy, when delivered rapidly to eligible patients with acute myocardial infarction, can limit myocardial damage and reduce mortality. Early administration, ideally within 45 minutes of arrival at the emergency department, is critical to achieving these benefits.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0063_7762", "title": "Clinical management of patients receiving thrombolytic therapy.", "span_text": "Rapid delivery of thrombolytic therapy to suitable patients with acute myocardial infarction can limit myocardial damage and reduce the risk of death. This requires an emergency department team approach following a written protocol to initiate thrombolytic therapy within 45 minutes of the patient's arrival at the emergency department."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "thrombolytic therapy timing myocardial infarction", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0056_6322", "pubmed23n0051_17464", "pubmed23n0001_15136", "Obstentrics_Williams_8258", "pubmed23n0046_4888", "pubmed23n0051_14381", "pubmed23n0045_6108", "pubmed23n0001_15133", "pubmed23n0004_8662", "pubmed23n0063_7762", "pubmed23n0062_10201", "pubmed23n0041_6415", "pubmed23n0010_7886", "Obstentrics_Williams_6828", "pubmed23n0059_14951", "pubmed23n0024_3841", "pubmed23n0045_19921", "pubmed23n0058_19542", "pubmed23n0010_7602", "pubmed23n0027_12549", "pubmed23n0057_20631", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1218", "pubmed23n0063_8187", "pubmed23n0043_573", "pubmed23n0036_4448", "pubmed23n0032_7128", "pubmed23n0058_714", "pubmed23n0017_6495", "Obstentrics_Williams_6870", "pubmed23n0057_6744", "InternalMed_Harrison_18128", "pubmed23n0012_5617", "pubmed23n0061_3103", "pubmed23n0004_3650", "pubmed23n0060_8916", "pubmed23n0045_8654", "pubmed23n0045_6274", "pubmed23n0028_5397", "pubmed23n0050_983", "pubmed23n0058_19787", "pubmed23n0055_22386", "pubmed23n0024_10", "pubmed23n0038_6531", "pubmed23n0049_8519", "pubmed23n0063_13553", "pubmed23n0028_9168", "pubmed23n0028_7937", "pubmed23n0057_17817", "pubmed23n0002_6986", "pubmed23n0026_11532", "pubmed23n0004_7675", "pubmed23n0047_7671", "pubmed23n0060_7785", "pubmed23n0063_15049", "pubmed23n0013_9374", "pubmed23n0032_3687", "pubmed23n0037_4350", "pubmed23n0001_13048", "pubmed23n0060_12634", "pubmed23n0036_4418", "pubmed23n0051_3411", "pubmed23n0057_21914", "pubmed23n0053_5833", "pubmed23n0044_24329", "pubmed23n0054_8460", "pubmed23n0013_9375", "pubmed23n0049_1925", "pubmed23n0012_1368", "pubmed23n0024_8258", "pubmed23n0042_8891", "pubmed23n0004_7917", "pubmed23n0036_2602"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0056_6322", "pubmed23n0051_17464", "pubmed23n0001_15136", "Obstentrics_Williams_8258", "pubmed23n0046_4888", "pubmed23n0051_14381", "pubmed23n0045_6108", "pubmed23n0001_15133", "pubmed23n0004_8662", "pubmed23n0063_7762", "pubmed23n0062_10201", "pubmed23n0041_6415", "pubmed23n0010_7886", "Obstentrics_Williams_6828", "pubmed23n0059_14951", "pubmed23n0024_3841", "pubmed23n0045_19921", "pubmed23n0058_19542", "pubmed23n0010_7602", "pubmed23n0027_12549"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Myocardial infarction Treatment treatment therapy management drug dosage medication pharmacotherapy first-line second-line guideline intervention acute management long-term management", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 12837.179666967131, "llm_ms": 3119.717124965973, "verification_ms": 896.7717500054277, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0056_6322", "pubmed23n0051_17464", "pubmed23n0001_15136", "Obstentrics_Williams_8258", "pubmed23n0046_4888", "pubmed23n0051_14381", "pubmed23n0045_6108", "pubmed23n0001_15133", "pubmed23n0004_8662", "pubmed23n0063_7762", "pubmed23n0062_10201", "pubmed23n0041_6415", "pubmed23n0010_7886", "Obstentrics_Williams_6828", "pubmed23n0059_14951", "pubmed23n0024_3841", "pubmed23n0045_19921", "pubmed23n0058_19542", "pubmed23n0010_7602", "pubmed23n0027_12549"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "verification": 45.0, "llm": 3119.717124965973}}}
{"topic": "Hypothyroidism", "competency": "Monitoring", "stem": "A 68-year-old woman with newly diagnosed hypothyroidism and a history of coronary artery disease is being started on levothyroxine. What is the most appropriate initial dose and monitoring strategy for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Start with 12.5 to 25 micrograms daily and increase by 25 micrograms every 2 to 3 weeks with close monitoring of cardiac symptoms.", "B": "Start with 100 micrograms daily and adjust based on TSH levels after 6 weeks.", "C": "Start with 150 micrograms daily and reduce if symptoms of hyperthyroidism develop.", "D": "Start with 50 micrograms twice daily and titrate based on free T4 levels every 2 weeks."}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "In patients with hypothyroidism and preexisting coronary artery disease, rapid thyroid hormone replacement can precipitate angina, arrhythmias, or myocardial infarction. Therefore, a low initial dose of levothyroxine (12.5\u201325 micrograms daily) with gradual dose escalation every 2 to 3 weeks and close monitoring of cardiac symptoms is recommended to minimize cardiovascular risks.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0045_10555", "title": "Hypothyroidism complicated by angina pectoris: therapeutic approaches.", "span_text": "The precipitation of angina pectoris, cardiac arrhythmia, and even myocardial infarction may occur in patients when initiating rapid replacement therapy for hypothyroidism. This is particularly true when replacement therapy is instituted in elderly persons or in patients with preexisting coronary artery disease. A starting daily dose of 12.5 to 25 micrograms and increments of 25 micrograms every 2 to 3 weeks is recommended. Close monitoring of cardiac symptoms is essential to avoid side effects."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "initial levothyroxine dose in hypothyroid patients with coronary artery disease", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0045_1270", "pubmed23n0045_10555", "pubmed23n0047_9261", "pubmed23n0048_8037", "pubmed23n0043_11799", "pubmed23n0002_10201"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0045_1270", "pubmed23n0045_10555", "pubmed23n0047_9261", "pubmed23n0048_8037", "pubmed23n0043_11799", "pubmed23n0002_10201"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "score": "1.0", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Hypothyroidism Monitoring monitoring follow-up treatment response labs imaging vital signs disease control therapeutic monitoring adverse effects dose adjustment clinical assessment outcome tracking", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 8672.971666033845, "llm_ms": 1312.136459047906, "verification_ms": 857.8882499714382, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0045_1270", "pubmed23n0045_10555", "pubmed23n0047_9261", "pubmed23n0048_8037", "pubmed23n0043_11799", "pubmed23n0002_10201"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "verification": 30.0, "llm": 1312.136459047906}}}
{"topic": "Hepatitis C", "competency": "Complications", "stem": "A 55-year-old man with chronic hepatitis C is found to have cirrhosis on liver biopsy. Which of the following is a well-recognized long-term complication of his condition, even after successful antiviral treatment?", "options": {"A": "Hepatocellular carcinoma", "B": "Acute liver failure requiring emergency transplantation", "C": "Autoimmune hepatitis", "D": "Pancreatic adenocarcinoma"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Patients with chronic hepatitis C who develop cirrhosis remain at increased risk for hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC), even after achieving a sustained virologic response (SVR) with antiviral therapy. Although successful treatment reduces the risk of liver-related death and HCC, the risk persists at a lower level, necessitating ongoing surveillance.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "InternalMed_Harrison_23825", "title": "InternalMed_Harrison", "span_text": "Although successful treatment reduces mortality in cirrhotic patients (and those with advanced fibrosis) and reduces the likelihood of HCC, the risk of liver-related death and HCC persists, albeit at a much reduced level, necessitating continued clinical monitoring and cancer surveillance after SVR in cirrhotics."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "complications of chronic hepatitis C after treatment", "doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_23783", "pubmed23n0059_757", "pubmed23n0052_16488", "Pathology_Robbins_3910", "InternalMed_Harrison_23555", "pubmed23n0052_12953", "pubmed23n0045_10019", "pubmed23n0061_5869", "Obstentrics_Williams_7600", "InternalMed_Harrison_23825", "InternalMed_Harrison_23780", "Gynecology_Novak_3845", "pubmed23n0061_16962", "InternalMed_Harrison_23784", "InternalMed_Harrison_9900", "InternalMed_Harrison_23774", "InternalMed_Harrison_23934", "pubmed23n0039_4516", "Pharmacology_Katzung_7125", "Surgery_Schwartz_9022", "Gynecology_Novak_3844"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_23783", "pubmed23n0059_757", "pubmed23n0052_16488", "Pathology_Robbins_3910", "InternalMed_Harrison_23555", "pubmed23n0052_12953", "pubmed23n0045_10019", "pubmed23n0061_5869", "Obstentrics_Williams_7600", "InternalMed_Harrison_23825", "InternalMed_Harrison_23780", "Gynecology_Novak_3845", "pubmed23n0061_16962", "InternalMed_Harrison_23784", "InternalMed_Harrison_9900", "InternalMed_Harrison_23774", "InternalMed_Harrison_23934", "pubmed23n0039_4516", "Pharmacology_Katzung_7125", "Surgery_Schwartz_9022"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Hepatitis C Complications complications adverse outcomes sequelae comorbidity disease progression risk factors mortality morbidity hospitalization long-term effects severity clinical deterioration", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 28243.413583026268, "llm_ms": 1327.00087496778, "verification_ms": 833.1440829788335, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["InternalMed_Harrison_23783", "pubmed23n0059_757", "pubmed23n0052_16488", "Pathology_Robbins_3910", "InternalMed_Harrison_23555", "pubmed23n0052_12953", "pubmed23n0045_10019", "pubmed23n0061_5869", "Obstentrics_Williams_7600", "InternalMed_Harrison_23825", "InternalMed_Harrison_23780", "Gynecology_Novak_3845", "pubmed23n0061_16962", "InternalMed_Harrison_23784", "InternalMed_Harrison_9900", "InternalMed_Harrison_23774", "InternalMed_Harrison_23934", "pubmed23n0039_4516", "Pharmacology_Katzung_7125", "Surgery_Schwartz_9022"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "verification": 45.0, "llm": 1327.00087496778}}}
{"topic": "Osteoporosis", "competency": "Therapy", "stem": "A 68-year-old postmenopausal woman is scheduled to begin long-term glucocorticoid therapy for polymyalgia rheumatica. She has no history of fractures but is at increased risk for osteoporosis due to her age and planned steroid use. Which of the following is the most appropriate pharmacologic intervention to prevent steroid-induced osteoporosis in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Initiate treatment with denosumab or bisphosphonates.", "B": "Begin daily salmon calcitonin nasal spray.", "C": "Start transdermal estrogen therapy.", "D": "Administer high-dose calcium and vitamin D supplementation alone."}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "For postmenopausal women who are likely to require long-term glucocorticoid therapy, bisphosphonates or denosumab are recommended to prevent steroid-induced osteoporosis. These agents have been shown to protect against bone loss and reduce fracture risk in this population.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "InternalMed_Harrison_25125", "title": "InternalMed_Harrison", "span_text": "Strategiesto prevent osteoporosis should be initiated in most patients likely to require long-term glucocorticoid therapy and/or with other predisposing factors. Postmenopausal women can be protected from steroid-induced osteoporosis with either bisphosphonates or denosumab."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "osteoporosis therapy glucocorticoid-induced prevention", "doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_25125", "InternalMed_Harrison_25237", "pubmed23n0054_10168", "pubmed23n0053_15984", "InternalMed_Harrison_7925"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_25125", "InternalMed_Harrison_25237", "pubmed23n0054_10168", "pubmed23n0053_15984", "InternalMed_Harrison_7925"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match_in_evidence", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Osteoporosis Therapy therapy treatment management drug dosage medication pharmacotherapy first-line second-line guideline intervention acute management long-term management", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 51553.079375007655, "llm_ms": 1677.285458019469, "verification_ms": 1015.129082952626, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["InternalMed_Harrison_25125", "InternalMed_Harrison_25237", "pubmed23n0054_10168", "pubmed23n0053_15984", "InternalMed_Harrison_7925"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "verification": 45.0, "total": 165.0, "llm": 1677.285458019469}}}
{"topic": "Neonatal sepsis", "competency": "Complications", "stem": "Which neonatal condition is associated with an increased risk of neonatal sepsis in term infants with birth weights below the 5th percentile?", "options": {"A": "Fetal growth restriction", "B": "Polycythemia", "C": "Hyperbilirubinemia", "D": "Congenital malformations"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Infants with fetal growth restriction, particularly those with birth weights below the 5th percentile, are at increased risk for neonatal sepsis. This association is supported by evidence showing higher rates of adverse neonatal outcomes, including sepsis, in growth-restricted newborns compared to appropriately grown infants.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Obstentrics_Williams_5951", "title": "Obstentrics_Williams Text", "span_text": "In another analysis of more than 91,000 uncomplicated pregnancies, newborns with weights < 5th percentile had a higher risk of low 5-minute Apgar score, respiratory distress, necrotizing enterocolitis, and neonatal sepsis than appropriate-weight neonates."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "neonatal sepsis risk factors fetal growth restriction", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0015_9297", "pubmed23n0053_17697", "pubmed23n0054_5962", "pubmed23n0047_21103", "Obstentrics_Williams_5951", "pubmed23n0024_12109", "pubmed23n0016_5043", "pubmed23n0043_1775", "Pathology_Robbins_1684", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1175", "Obstentrics_Williams_2354", "Obstentrics_Williams_5624", "pubmed23n0042_12454", "pubmed23n0014_6129", "pubmed23n0048_19624", "pubmed23n0015_6402", "pubmed23n0054_12552", "pubmed23n0052_1066", "pubmed23n0027_952", "pubmed23n0060_10639", "Obstentrics_Williams_7545", "pubmed23n0053_14192", "pubmed23n0047_6071", "InternalMed_Harrison_11709", "pubmed23n0038_8905", "pubmed23n0053_14517", "Surgery_Schwartz_13390", "Obstentrics_Williams_7067", "Obstentrics_Williams_3002", "Obstentrics_Williams_3189", "pubmed23n0059_164", "pubmed23n0048_164", "Pediatrics_Nelson_4100", "pubmed23n0052_8663", "Obstentrics_Williams_5623", "pubmed23n0018_2121", "pubmed23n0045_6812", "Gynecology_Novak_3843", "Obstentrics_Williams_6672", "pubmed23n0049_14995", "Pediatrics_Nelson_741", "pubmed23n0016_1976", "Obstentrics_Williams_5304", "Obstentrics_Williams_3655", "pubmed23n0044_22800", "pubmed23n0049_4462", "pubmed23n0062_2161", "Pediatrics_Nelson_889", "pubmed23n0061_3998", "pubmed23n0043_1765", "InternalMed_Harrison_23086", "pubmed23n0053_4238", "pubmed23n0053_1183", "Surgery_Schwartz_5235", "pubmed23n0001_2108", "Obstentrics_Williams_3813", "Obstentrics_Williams_7747", "pubmed23n0023_2883", "Obstentrics_Williams_1325", "pubmed23n0052_16103", "pubmed23n0061_15739", "Surgery_Schwartz_2607", "pubmed23n0058_21152", "Obstentrics_Williams_4370", "pubmed23n0042_6102", "Obstentrics_Williams_4223", "pubmed23n0062_15331", "Obstentrics_Williams_3401", "pubmed23n0061_15536", "pubmed23n0056_7057", "Obstentrics_Williams_801", "Obstentrics_Williams_3656"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0015_9297", "pubmed23n0053_17697", "pubmed23n0054_5962", "pubmed23n0047_21103", "Obstentrics_Williams_5951", "pubmed23n0024_12109", "pubmed23n0016_5043", "pubmed23n0043_1775", "Pathology_Robbins_1684", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1175", "Obstentrics_Williams_2354", "Obstentrics_Williams_5624", "pubmed23n0042_12454", "pubmed23n0014_6129", "pubmed23n0048_19624", "pubmed23n0015_6402", "pubmed23n0054_12552", "pubmed23n0052_1066", "pubmed23n0027_952", "pubmed23n0060_10639"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Neonatal sepsis Complications complications adverse outcomes sequelae comorbidity disease progression risk factors mortality morbidity hospitalization long-term effects severity clinical deterioration", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 46039.4232920371, "llm_ms": 1445.207874989137, "verification_ms": 930.3217499982566, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0015_9297", "pubmed23n0053_17697", "pubmed23n0054_5962", "pubmed23n0047_21103", "Obstentrics_Williams_5951", "pubmed23n0024_12109", "pubmed23n0016_5043", "pubmed23n0043_1775", "Pathology_Robbins_1684", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1175", "Obstentrics_Williams_2354", "Obstentrics_Williams_5624", "pubmed23n0042_12454", "pubmed23n0014_6129", "pubmed23n0048_19624", "pubmed23n0015_6402", "pubmed23n0054_12552", "pubmed23n0052_1066", "pubmed23n0027_952", "pubmed23n0060_10639"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "verification": 45.0, "llm": 1445.207874989137}}}
{"topic": "Dehydration", "competency": "Prognosis", "stem": "A 32-year-old woman presents with high-grade fever, gastrointestinal symptoms, and severe dehydration. She is diagnosed with dengue hemorrhagic fever. Despite initial supportive therapy, her condition worsens with persistent fever, severe bicytopenia, and liver impairment. Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH) is suspected. What is the most important factor influencing her prognosis at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Prompt initiation of immunosuppressive treatment", "B": "Aggressive fluid resuscitation alone", "C": "Empirical antibiotic therapy", "D": "Early mechanical ventilation"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "In patients with dengue-associated hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH), the prognosis is poor unless immunosuppressive treatment is promptly initiated. Delay in starting therapy such as dexamethasone or tocilizumab is associated with continued hyperinflammation and high mortality. While supportive care is essential, specific immunosuppressive therapy is critical to altering the disease course and improving outcomes.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed_web", "doc_id": "pubmed_web_41416341", "title": "A Case of Severe Dengue With Hemophagocytic Lymphohistiocytosis (HLH) Successfully Managed With Tocilizumab and Dexamethasone", "span_text": "Unless immunosuppressive treatment is promptly initiated, the prognosis of dengue-associated HLH remains poor. A previously healthy 32-year-old female was admitted to a tertiary care hospital in Sri Lanka with high-grade intermittent fever, gastrointestinal symptoms, and severe dehydration. Initial blood investigations and a point-of-care ultrasound scan confirmed dengue hemorrhagic fever. Despite supportive therapy, the patient's condition deteriorated with persistent fever, severe bicytopenia, and liver impairment."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "prognosis of dehydration in severe dengue with HLH", "doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41496135", "pubmed_web_41485792", "pubmed_web_41459887", "pubmed_web_41416341", "pubmed_web_41379184", "pubmed_web_41373998", "pubmed_web_41354320", "pubmed_web_41297274", "pubmed_web_41290010", "pubmed_web_41273141", "pubmed_web_41251011", "pubmed_web_41205150", "pubmed_web_41160386", "pubmed_web_41112953", "pubmed_web_41104280", "pubmed_web_41077187", "pubmed_web_41071303", "pubmed_web_41071134", "pubmed_web_40982160", "pubmed_web_40960368"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41496135", "pubmed_web_41485792", "pubmed_web_41459887", "pubmed_web_41416341", "pubmed_web_41379184", "pubmed_web_41373998", "pubmed_web_41354320", "pubmed_web_41297274", "pubmed_web_41290010", "pubmed_web_41273141", "pubmed_web_41251011", "pubmed_web_41205150", "pubmed_web_41160386", "pubmed_web_41112953", "pubmed_web_41104280", "pubmed_web_41077187", "pubmed_web_41071303", "pubmed_web_41071134", "pubmed_web_40982160", "pubmed_web_40960368"], "retrieval_mode": "pubmed_web"}, "verification": {"method": "evidence_match", "score": "high", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Dehydration Prognosis prognosis outcomes mortality survival complications disease course recurrence risk stratification severity long-term outcomes quality of life predictors", "evidence_top_k": 20, "retrieval_ms": 70305.10191601934, "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": true, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 42392.53795798868}, "status_counts": {"INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 1}, "failed_checks": ["insufficient_evidence"]}, {"attempt": 2, "reason": "pubmed_web_fallback", "query": "Dehydration Prognosis prognosis outcomes mortality survival complications disease course recurrence risk stratification severity long-term outcomes quality of life predictors Prognosis", "evidence_top_k": 60, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 12122.387290990446, "llm_ms": 1578.7064580363221, "verification_ms": 881.7039160057902, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "pubmed_web", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": true, "docs": 20, "runtime_ms": 4296.633082965855}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed_web_41496135", "pubmed_web_41485792", "pubmed_web_41459887", "pubmed_web_41416341", "pubmed_web_41379184", "pubmed_web_41373998", "pubmed_web_41354320", "pubmed_web_41297274", "pubmed_web_41290010", "pubmed_web_41273141", "pubmed_web_41251011", "pubmed_web_41205150", "pubmed_web_41160386", "pubmed_web_41112953", "pubmed_web_41104280", "pubmed_web_41077187", "pubmed_web_41071303", "pubmed_web_41071134", "pubmed_web_40982160", "pubmed_web_40960368"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 65.0, "total": 185.0, "llm": 1578.7064580363221, "verification": 881.7039160057902}}}
{"topic": "Hepatitis C", "competency": "Monitoring", "stem": "A 55-year-old patient with chronic hepatitis C begins antiviral therapy. The patient has a history of alcohol use and elevated baseline liver enzymes. Which monitoring strategy is most appropriate during the initial phase of treatment?", "options": {"A": "Monthly assessment of symptoms and serum aminotransferases", "B": "Clinical monitoring only if symptoms develop", "C": "Quarterly liver ultrasound and AFP levels", "D": "Routine liver biopsy every 6 months"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Patients with hepatitis C who have risk factors such as a history of hepatic disease, alcohol use, or elevated baseline liver enzymes should be monitored closely during the initial phase of treatment. Monthly monitoring of symptoms and serum aminotransferases is recommended to detect early signs of hepatotoxicity and ensure timely intervention.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "InternalMed_Harrison_13413", "title": "InternalMed_Harrison", "span_text": "Older patients, those with concomitant diseases, those with a history of hepatic disease (especially hepatitis C), and those using alcohol daily should be monitored especially closely (i.e., monthly), with repeated measurements of aminotransferases, during the initial phase of treatment."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "monitoring in hepatitis C treatment", "doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_13413", "InternalMed_Harrison_13590", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4007", "pubmed23n0052_10942", "pubmed23n0045_20591", "pubmed23n0033_2900", "pubmed23n0052_8817"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_13413", "InternalMed_Harrison_13590", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4007", "pubmed23n0052_10942", "pubmed23n0045_20591", "pubmed23n0033_2900", "pubmed23n0052_8817"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "evidence_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Hepatitis C Monitoring monitoring follow-up treatment response labs imaging vital signs disease control therapeutic monitoring adverse effects dose adjustment clinical assessment outcome tracking", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 15116.825332981534, "llm_ms": 1040.0677500292659, "verification_ms": 1649.7607079800218, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["InternalMed_Harrison_13413", "InternalMed_Harrison_13590", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4007", "pubmed23n0052_10942", "pubmed23n0045_20591", "pubmed23n0033_2900", "pubmed23n0052_8817"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "verification": 30.0, "llm": 1040.0677500292659}}}
{"topic": "Migraine", "competency": "Etiology", "stem": "Which of the following best describes the current understanding of the underlying cause of migraine headaches?", "options": {"A": "A unifying theory for the cause of migraine has not yet been established.", "B": "Migraine is primarily caused by distention of branches of the external carotid artery.", "C": "Migraine results from consistent emotional or psychological triggers.", "D": "Migraine is exclusively inherited in a Mendelian pattern."}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The exact cause of migraine remains unclear, and no single unifying theory has been established despite extensive research. While vascular theories and genetic factors have been implicated, none fully explain the pathogenesis. Genetic predisposition plays a role but is only clearly Mendelian in rare families. Emotional triggers may aggravate but not consistently cause attacks.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Neurology_Adams_1365", "title": "Neurology_Adams", "span_text": "So far, it has not been possible to determine from the many clinical observations and investigations, a unifying theory as to the cause of migraine. Tension and other emotional states, which are claimed by some migraineurs to precede their attacks, are so inconsistent as to be no more than potential aggravating factors. Clearly, an underlying genetic factor is implicated, although it is expressed in a recognizable mendelian pattern in only a small number of families (see above). How this genetic predisposition is translated periodically into a regional neurologic deficit, unilateral headache, or both is unknown."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "etiology of migraine pathogenesis cause", "results": ["Neurology_Adams_1365", "Neurology_Adams_1360"], "doc_ids": ["Neurology_Adams_1360", "Neurology_Adams_1365"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Neurology_Adams_1360", "Neurology_Adams_1365"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "direct_quote_match", "confirmed": "true", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Migraine Etiology etiology cause risk factors pathogenesis mechanism underlying cause genetic factors environmental factors predisposing factors associated conditions epidemiology incidence", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 12843.891166965477, "llm_ms": 1061.2924580345862, "verification_ms": 1296.86662502354, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Neurology_Adams_1360", "Neurology_Adams_1365"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 65.0, "verification": 40.0, "llm": 1061.2924580345862}}}
{"topic": "Systemic lupus erythematosus", "competency": "Complications", "stem": "A 32-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus is being evaluated for risk factors associated with increased mortality. Which of the following has been shown to be a significant risk factor for decreased survival in patients with SLE?", "options": {"A": "Elevated systolic blood pressure", "B": "Positive anti-Smith antibody", "C": "Presence of malar rash", "D": "History of photosensitivity"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Elevated systolic blood pressure is a significant risk factor for decreased survival in patients with systemic lupus erythematosus. Each millimeter of mercury increase in systolic blood pressure corresponds to a 2% increase in mortality risk, and this association remains statistically significant after correction for multiple comparisons.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0058_11470", "title": "Variables associated with decreased survival in systemic lupus erythematosus.", "span_text": "Fifty-one deaths occurred among 310 patients with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) observed for 1,234 patient-years. Twenty-one of 97 entry variables at first clinic visit were associated with an increased risk of mortality. When corrected for multiple comparisons, the only risk factor that retained statistical significance was systolic blood pressure. Each millimeter unit increase in systolic blood pressure corresponded to a 2% increase in mortality risk."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "risk factors for mortality in systemic lupus erythematosus", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0043_1775", "pubmed23n0064_212", "pubmed23n0049_1007", "pubmed23n0058_14994", "pubmed23n0058_11470", "pubmed23n0052_12309", "pubmed23n0016_10698", "pubmed23n0047_11191", "pubmed23n0051_12218", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1179", "pubmed23n0043_1776", "pubmed23n0060_4175", "pubmed23n0037_11086", "pubmed23n0042_10487", "pubmed23n0047_9330", "pubmed23n0058_12469", "pubmed23n0041_10807", "pubmed23n0016_11527", "pubmed23n0041_10455", "pubmed23n0054_1941", "pubmed23n0023_11652", "pubmed23n0014_9910", "pubmed23n0052_1357", "pubmed23n0018_5562", "pubmed23n0058_12477", "Obstentrics_Williams_8334", "pubmed23n0054_13105", "pubmed23n0058_12882", "pubmed23n0023_10013", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1979", "pubmed23n0051_8", "Obstentrics_Williams_8210", "pubmed23n0009_1586", "Obstentrics_Williams_4882", "pubmed23n0059_2533", "pubmed23n0050_11251", "pubmed23n0043_1767", "pubmed23n0037_4606", "pubmed23n0012_37", "pubmed23n0043_15729", "Obstentrics_Williams_8321", "pubmed23n0058_14986", "pubmed23n0002_7034", "pubmed23n0028_14157", "pubmed23n0056_17067", "pubmed23n0050_13706", "pubmed23n0029_8497", "pubmed23n0015_728", "pubmed23n0062_3061", "pubmed23n0027_983", "pubmed23n0058_14991", "Obstentrics_Williams_8344", "pubmed23n0017_2924", "pubmed23n0016_7460", "pubmed23n0007_13475", "pubmed23n0052_18327", "pubmed23n0022_11025", "pubmed23n0037_1336", "Obstentrics_Williams_8343", "pubmed23n0025_7484", "pubmed23n0043_12749", "pubmed23n0047_21699", "pubmed23n0051_19692", "pubmed23n0057_21910", "Obstentrics_Williams_8301", "pubmed23n0059_18009", "pubmed23n0037_4788", "pubmed23n0012_8643", "pubmed23n0043_12755", "pubmed23n0058_9076", "pubmed23n0016_5862", "pubmed23n0058_9184", "pubmed23n0015_4598", "pubmed23n0054_13257", "pubmed23n0038_1270", "pubmed23n0052_8063", "Obstentrics_Williams_8205", "pubmed23n0012_5833", "pubmed23n0019_5332", "pubmed23n0016_2005", "Obstentrics_Williams_8312", "pubmed23n0054_21755", "pubmed23n0012_5855", "Gynecology_Novak_6280", "pubmed23n0063_16376", "pubmed23n0043_4855", "pubmed23n0001_13853", "Surgery_Schwartz_10061", "pubmed23n0043_1772", "pubmed23n0059_19420", "pubmed23n0051_6213", "pubmed23n0047_541", "pubmed23n0047_12929", "pubmed23n0048_18176", "pubmed23n0052_15590", "pubmed23n0062_16812", "pubmed23n0037_1337", "pubmed23n0006_11638", "pubmed23n0006_4116"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0043_1775", "pubmed23n0064_212", "pubmed23n0049_1007", "pubmed23n0058_14994", "pubmed23n0058_11470", "pubmed23n0052_12309", "pubmed23n0016_10698", "pubmed23n0047_11191", "pubmed23n0051_12218", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1179", "pubmed23n0043_1776", "pubmed23n0060_4175", "pubmed23n0037_11086", "pubmed23n0042_10487", "pubmed23n0047_9330", "pubmed23n0058_12469", "pubmed23n0041_10807", "pubmed23n0016_11527", "pubmed23n0041_10455", "pubmed23n0054_1941"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"source_confirmed": "true", "accuracy_rating": "high", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Systemic lupus erythematosus Complications complications adverse outcomes sequelae comorbidity disease progression risk factors mortality morbidity hospitalization long-term effects severity clinical deterioration", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 17720.22299998207, "llm_ms": 1337.664999999106, "verification_ms": 878.6765830009244, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0043_1775", "pubmed23n0064_212", "pubmed23n0049_1007", "pubmed23n0058_14994", "pubmed23n0058_11470", "pubmed23n0052_12309", "pubmed23n0016_10698", "pubmed23n0047_11191", "pubmed23n0051_12218", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1179", "pubmed23n0043_1776", "pubmed23n0060_4175", "pubmed23n0037_11086", "pubmed23n0042_10487", "pubmed23n0047_9330", "pubmed23n0058_12469", "pubmed23n0041_10807", "pubmed23n0016_11527", "pubmed23n0041_10455", "pubmed23n0054_1941"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 65.0, "total": 185.0, "llm": 1337.664999999106, "verification": 878.6765830009244}}}
{"topic": "Pancreatitis", "competency": "Prognosis", "stem": "A 55-year-old man with acute necrotizing pancreatitis is being evaluated using Ranson's criteria. Which of the following best predicts a 100% mortality rate in this condition?", "options": {"A": "Presence of more than 7 Ranson's criteria", "B": "Presence of fewer than 3 Ranson's criteria", "C": "Development of mild pancreatic edema on CT imaging", "D": "Resolution of symptoms within 48 hours of admission"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "In patients with acute necrotizing pancreatitis, the presence of more than 7 Ranson's criteria is associated with a 100% mortality rate, indicating a very poor prognosis.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0043_16585", "title": "[High risk factors in the prognosis of acute necrotizing pancreatitis]", "span_text": "In this study, Ranson's risk factors were evaluated on 36 cases of acute necrotizing pancreatitis. It was found that all 13 cases with more than 7 factors each died, the mortality rate was 100%, and 2 of 5 cases with 5-7 factors each succumbed with the mortality rate of 40%."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "prognosis acute necrotizing pancreatitis Ranson criteria mortality", "doc_ids": ["Surgery_Schwartz_9951", "Surgery_Schwartz_9248", "Surgery_Schwartz_9670", "pubmed23n0027_15167", "Surgery_Schwartz_9923", "Obstentrics_Williams_7644", "Surgery_Schwartz_9672", "First_Aid_Step2_354", "Surgery_Schwartz_9964", "Surgery_Schwartz_9726", "pubmed23n0043_16585", "pubmed23n0014_6129", "pubmed23n0023_1646", "pubmed23n0025_11221", "Surgery_Schwartz_9970"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Surgery_Schwartz_9951", "Surgery_Schwartz_9248", "Surgery_Schwartz_9670", "pubmed23n0027_15167", "Surgery_Schwartz_9923", "Obstentrics_Williams_7644", "Surgery_Schwartz_9672", "First_Aid_Step2_354", "Surgery_Schwartz_9964", "Surgery_Schwartz_9726", "pubmed23n0043_16585", "pubmed23n0014_6129", "pubmed23n0023_1646", "pubmed23n0025_11221", "Surgery_Schwartz_9970"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Pancreatitis Prognosis prognosis outcomes mortality survival complications disease course recurrence risk stratification severity long-term outcomes quality of life predictors", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 48756.48533401545, "llm_ms": 3632.170707976911, "verification_ms": 810.4009170201607, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Surgery_Schwartz_9951", "Surgery_Schwartz_9248", "Surgery_Schwartz_9670", "pubmed23n0027_15167", "Surgery_Schwartz_9923", "Obstentrics_Williams_7644", "Surgery_Schwartz_9672", "First_Aid_Step2_354", "Surgery_Schwartz_9964", "Surgery_Schwartz_9726", "pubmed23n0043_16585", "pubmed23n0014_6129", "pubmed23n0023_1646", "pubmed23n0025_11221", "Surgery_Schwartz_9970"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "verification": 45.0, "llm": 3632.170707976911}}}
{"topic": "HIV", "competency": "Treatment", "stem": "A 35-year-old man with HIV infection presents with chronic refractory diarrhea. Extensive workup has not identified a specific infectious pathogen. Which of the following medications has been shown to be effective in managing chronic diarrhea in patients with AIDS when no specific agent can be identified?", "options": {"A": "Octreotide", "B": "Metronidazole", "C": "Ciprofloxacin", "D": "Albendazole"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Octreotide, a somatostatin analogue, has been shown to be useful in the management of chronic diarrhea in AIDS patients, particularly in cases of refractory diarrhea without an identifiable pathogen. Studies have demonstrated both complete and partial responses to octreotide in HIV-infected patients with chronic diarrhea, including those with HIV enteropathy or cryptosporidial infection.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0060_14369", "title": "Efficacy of octreotide in the management of chronic diarrhoea in AIDS.", "span_text": "In the absence of specific agents for cryptosporidial enteritis and HIV enteropathy, octreotide was found to be useful in the management of chronic diarrhoea in AIDS patients."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "treatment of chronic diarrhea in HIV patients", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0043_21016", "pubmed23n0056_2790", "InternalMed_Harrison_25535", "pubmed23n0056_13656", "pubmed23n0055_16262", "InternalMed_Harrison_13433", "pubmed23n0060_14369", "pubmed23n0057_16351", "pubmed23n0044_9236", "pubmed23n0058_5104", "pubmed23n0060_10061", "pubmed23n0055_14580", "Surgery_Schwartz_8795", "pubmed23n0053_8310", "pubmed23n0057_19552"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0043_21016", "pubmed23n0056_2790", "InternalMed_Harrison_25535", "pubmed23n0056_13656", "pubmed23n0055_16262", "InternalMed_Harrison_13433", "pubmed23n0060_14369", "pubmed23n0057_16351", "pubmed23n0044_9236", "pubmed23n0058_5104", "pubmed23n0060_10061", "pubmed23n0055_14580", "Surgery_Schwartz_8795", "pubmed23n0053_8310", "pubmed23n0057_19552"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "HIV Treatment treatment therapy management drug dosage medication pharmacotherapy first-line second-line guideline intervention acute management long-term management", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 7280.304290994536, "llm_ms": 1205.0184160470963, "verification_ms": 924.1525420220569, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0043_21016", "pubmed23n0056_2790", "InternalMed_Harrison_25535", "pubmed23n0056_13656", "pubmed23n0055_16262", "InternalMed_Harrison_13433", "pubmed23n0060_14369", "pubmed23n0057_16351", "pubmed23n0044_9236", "pubmed23n0058_5104", "pubmed23n0060_10061", "pubmed23n0055_14580", "Surgery_Schwartz_8795", "pubmed23n0053_8310", "pubmed23n0057_19552"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "verification": 45.0, "llm": 1205.0184160470963}}}
{"topic": "Pregnancy", "competency": "Pharmacology", "stem": "A 28-year-old pregnant woman with a history of bipolar disorder is being evaluated for medication management. She has been stable on lithium, but concerns are raised about potential risks during pregnancy. Which of the following is a key pharmacokinetic change affecting lithium during pregnancy that requires close monitoring?", "options": {"A": "Renal clearance of lithium increases during pregnancy, increasing the risk of subtherapeutic levels.", "B": "Lithium is metabolized more rapidly by hepatic enzymes in pregnancy, requiring dose reduction.", "C": "Lithium crosses the placenta only in the third trimester, so first-trimester exposure is minimal.", "D": "Lithium levels remain stable throughout pregnancy, so no dose adjustments are needed."}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Renal clearance of lithium increases during pregnancy, which can lead to subtherapeutic serum concentrations despite previously stable dosing. After delivery, renal clearance decreases, increasing the risk of lithium toxicity. Therefore, serum lithium levels must be closely monitored during pregnancy and the postpartum period to avoid therapeutic failure or toxicity.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Pharmacology_Katzung_3214", "title": "Pharmacology_Katzung Text", "span_text": "Renal clearance of lithium increases during pregnancy and reverts to lower levels immediately after delivery. A patient whose serum lithium concentration is in a good therapeutic range during pregnancy may develop toxic levels after delivery. Special care in monitoring lithium levels is needed at these times."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "lithium use in pregnancy pharmacokinetics", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0054_10758", "Pharmacology_Katzung_2596", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3214", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6568", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5784", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6617", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5740", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3959", "pubmed23n0045_14808", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5735", "Pharmacology_Katzung_2935", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3997", "Pharmacology_Katzung_105", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6708", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6565", "Pharmacology_Katzung_7053", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4002"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0054_10758", "Pharmacology_Katzung_2596", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3214", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6568", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5784", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6617", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5740", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3959", "pubmed23n0045_14808", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5735", "Pharmacology_Katzung_2935", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3997", "Pharmacology_Katzung_105", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6708", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6565", "Pharmacology_Katzung_7053", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4002"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"source_consistency": "Confirmed across multiple textbook entries", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Pregnancy Pharmacology pharmacology drug mechanism indication contraindication adverse effects drug interaction dosage pharmacokinetics pharmacodynamics monitoring toxicity therapeutic target", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 7822.403582977131, "llm_ms": 3233.7926250183955, "verification_ms": 1986.7710419930518, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0054_10758", "Pharmacology_Katzung_2596", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3214", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6568", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5784", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6617", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5740", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3959", "pubmed23n0045_14808", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5735", "Pharmacology_Katzung_2935", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3997", "Pharmacology_Katzung_105", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6708", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6565", "Pharmacology_Katzung_7053", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4002"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 65.0, "total": 185.0, "llm": 3233.7926250183955, "verification": 1986.7710419930518}}}
{"topic": "Osteoporosis", "competency": "Pharmacology", "stem": "A 68-year-old postmenopausal woman with a history of breast cancer is being evaluated for osteoporosis prevention. Which of the following medications is most likely to prevent bone loss while also serving as an antagonist in breast tissue?", "options": {"A": "Tamoxifen", "B": "Heparin", "C": "Prednisone", "D": "Alendronate"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Tamoxifen acts as a selective estrogen receptor modulator, functioning as an antagonist in breast tissue, which is beneficial in breast cancer, and as an agonist in bone tissue, helping to increase bone density and prevent osteoporosis.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Pharmacology_Katzung_244", "title": "Pharmacology_Katzung", "span_text": "Tamoxifen is a drug that binds to steroid receptors naturally activated by estrogen. Tamoxifen acts as an antagonist on estrogen receptors expressed in mammary tissue but as an agonist on estrogen receptors in bone. Consequently, tamoxifen may be useful not only in the treatment of breast cancer but also in the prevention of osteoporosis by increasing bone density (see Chapters 40 and 42)."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "tamoxifen osteoporosis pharmacology", "results": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_244"], "doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_385", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4839", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3761", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4404", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4842", "Pharmacology_Katzung_244"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_385", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4839", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3761", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4404", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4842", "Pharmacology_Katzung_244"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"accuracy": "confirmed", "source_consistency": "high", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Osteoporosis Pharmacology pharmacology drug mechanism indication contraindication adverse effects drug interaction dosage pharmacokinetics pharmacodynamics monitoring toxicity therapeutic target", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 5353.446708002593, "llm_ms": 3650.235250010155, "verification_ms": 1023.8317080074921, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["InternalMed_Harrison_385", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4839", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3761", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4404", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4842", "Pharmacology_Katzung_244"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "verification": 80.0, "llm": 3650.235250010155}}}
{"topic": "Dermatitis", "competency": "Treatment", "stem": "A 28-year-old woman presents with an acute exacerbation of atopic dermatitis. She is concerned about the long-term use of topical corticosteroids due to potential side effects. Which of the following treatments has been shown to significantly improve atopic dermatitis and reduce serum eosinophil cationic protein levels in patients with acute exacerbations?", "options": {"A": "High-dose UVA1 irradiation", "B": "Levamisole hydrochloride", "C": "PUVA therapy", "D": "Topical pimecrolimus alone"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "High-dose UVA1 irradiation has been shown to induce significant clinical improvement in patients with acute exacerbation of atopic dermatitis and significantly reduces elevated serum levels of eosinophil cationic protein, a marker of disease activity. This makes it a promising phototherapeutic modality for acute cases, especially when corticosteroid-sparing treatment is desired.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0051_20999", "title": "High-dose UVA1 therapy in the treatment of patients with atopic dermatitis.", "span_text": "High-dose UVA1 irradiation was found to induce a significant clinical improvement of atopic dermatitis (p less than 0.001). In comparison with UVA-UVB therapy, significant differences in favor of high-dose UVA1 were observed (p less than 0.01). High-dose UVA1, but not UVA-UVB treatment, significantly reduced the elevated serum level of eosinophil cationic protein in patients with atopic dermatitis (p less than 0.003). These studies indicate that high-dose UVA1 irradiation may represent a novel phototherapeutic modality for the treatment of patients with an acute exacerbation of atopic dermatitis."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "treatment for acute atopic dermatitis alternatives to corticosteroids", "doc_ids": ["Pediatrics_Nelson_4043", "InternalMed_Harrison_16952", "pubmed23n0042_14476", "pubmed23n0013_9304", "pubmed23n0051_20999", "pubmed23n0012_7561", "pubmed23n0062_14665"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Pediatrics_Nelson_4043", "InternalMed_Harrison_16952", "pubmed23n0042_14476", "pubmed23n0013_9304", "pubmed23n0051_20999", "pubmed23n0012_7561", "pubmed23n0062_14665"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "direct_match", "confidence": "high", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Dermatitis Treatment treatment therapy management drug dosage medication pharmacotherapy first-line second-line guideline intervention acute management long-term management", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 6878.9259170298465, "llm_ms": 1534.3508339719847, "verification_ms": 1114.4329590024427, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Pediatrics_Nelson_4043", "InternalMed_Harrison_16952", "pubmed23n0042_14476", "pubmed23n0013_9304", "pubmed23n0051_20999", "pubmed23n0012_7561", "pubmed23n0062_14665"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "verification": 30.0, "llm": 1534.3508339719847}}}
{"topic": "Deep vein thrombosis", "competency": "Prevention", "stem": "A 72-year-old man is admitted with acute ischemic stroke resulting in lower-limb paresis. Which pharmacologic agent has been shown to be more effective than unfractionated heparin for the prevention of deep vein thrombosis in this patient population?", "options": {"A": "Low-molecular-weight heparinoid (ORG 10172)", "B": "Warfarin adjusted to INR 2\u20133", "C": "Aspirin 300 mg daily", "D": "Intravenous unfractionated heparin"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "In patients with acute ischemic stroke and lower-limb paresis, a randomized double-blind study demonstrated that low-molecular-weight heparinoid (ORG 10172), given subcutaneously at 750 anti-factor Xa units twice daily, was more effective than subcutaneous unfractionated heparin (5000 units twice daily) in preventing deep vein thrombosis, with a 71% relative risk reduction. The incidence of hemorrhage was similar in both groups.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0050_6562", "title": "A low-molecular-weight heparinoid compared with unfractionated heparin in the prevention of deep vein thrombosis in patients with acute ischemic stroke. A randomized, double-blind study.", "span_text": "To compare the relative safety and efficacy of a low-molecular-weight heparinoid (ORG 10172) with unfractionated heparin in the prevention of deep vein thrombosis in patients with acute ischemic stroke. Double-blind randomized trial. Seven Canadian university-affiliated hospitals. Eighty-seven patients with acute ischemic stroke resulting in lower-limb paresis. Patients received either low-molecular-weight heparinoid, 750 anti-factor Xa units twice daily, or unfractionated heparin, 5000 units subcutaneously twice daily. Treatment was continued for 14 days or until hospital discharge if sooner. Deep vein thrombosis was diagnosed using 125I-labeled fibrinogen leg scanning and impedance plethysmography. Venography was indicated if either test was positive. Overt hemorrhage, major or minor, was assessed clinically. Venous thrombosis occurred in four patients (9%) given low-molecular-weight heparinoid and in 13 patients (31%) given heparin (relative risk reduction, 71%; 95% CI, 16% to 93%. The corresponding rates for proximal vein thrombosis were 4% and 12%, respectively (relative risk reduction, 63%; P greater than 0.2). The incidence of hemorrhage was 2% in both groups. Low-molecular-weight heparinoid, given in a fixed dose of 750 anti-factor Xa units subcutaneously twice daily, is more effective than subcutaneous low-dose heparin for the prevention of deep vein thrombosis in patients with acute ischemic stroke."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "prevention of DVT in acute ischemic stroke patients", "results": ["pubmed23n0050_6562", "pubmed23n0003_4295", "pubmed23n0052_20026"], "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0052_20026", "pubmed23n0057_19176", "pubmed23n0044_2780", "pubmed23n0033_10472", "pubmed23n0050_6562", "pubmed23n0051_21653", "pubmed23n0003_4295", "pubmed23n0054_1348", "pubmed23n0044_510", "pubmed23n0044_2413", "pubmed23n0054_5613", "pubmed23n0021_7630", "InternalMed_Harrison_5958", "pubmed23n0021_4544", "Gynecology_Novak_3882", "pubmed23n0028_12060", "pubmed23n0059_8009", "pubmed23n0028_6269", "pubmed23n0044_2412", "pubmed23n0026_10853", "Surgery_Schwartz_3790", "InternalMed_Harrison_29881", "pubmed23n0013_10878", "pubmed23n0019_3503", "pubmed23n0033_8625", "pubmed23n0026_10399", "pubmed23n0046_5629", "pubmed23n0030_10338", "pubmed23n0016_5510", "InternalMed_Harrison_11260", "pubmed23n0055_16040", "pubmed23n0033_6488", "pubmed23n0050_21397", "pubmed23n0036_6820", "InternalMed_Harrison_29933", "pubmed23n0013_6526", "pubmed23n0062_3064", "pubmed23n0039_7404", "pubmed23n0011_6983", "pubmed23n0030_10570", "pubmed23n0057_23800", "pubmed23n0044_19835", "pubmed23n0039_1225", "Neurology_Adams_6409", "InternalMed_Harrison_24032", "Anatomy_Gray_938", "pubmed23n0054_14896", "pubmed23n0038_1673", "pubmed23n0055_13823", "pubmed23n0029_3364", "pubmed23n0021_8937"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0052_20026", "pubmed23n0057_19176", "pubmed23n0044_2780", "pubmed23n0033_10472", "pubmed23n0050_6562", "pubmed23n0051_21653", "pubmed23n0003_4295", "pubmed23n0054_1348", "pubmed23n0044_510", "pubmed23n0044_2413", "pubmed23n0054_5613", "pubmed23n0021_7630", "InternalMed_Harrison_5958", "pubmed23n0021_4544", "Gynecology_Novak_3882", "pubmed23n0028_12060", "pubmed23n0059_8009", "pubmed23n0028_6269", "pubmed23n0044_2412", "pubmed23n0026_10853"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"accuracy": "confirmed", "consistency": "high", "conflicting_evidence": "none", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Deep vein thrombosis Prevention prevention prophylaxis risk reduction screening vaccination primary prevention secondary prevention lifestyle modification counseling public health early detection follow-up", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 9813.197374984156, "llm_ms": 1653.730666032061, "verification_ms": 2549.0552919800393, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0052_20026", "pubmed23n0057_19176", "pubmed23n0044_2780", "pubmed23n0033_10472", "pubmed23n0050_6562", "pubmed23n0051_21653", "pubmed23n0003_4295", "pubmed23n0054_1348", "pubmed23n0044_510", "pubmed23n0044_2413", "pubmed23n0054_5613", "pubmed23n0021_7630", "InternalMed_Harrison_5958", "pubmed23n0021_4544", "Gynecology_Novak_3882", "pubmed23n0028_12060", "pubmed23n0059_8009", "pubmed23n0028_6269", "pubmed23n0044_2412", "pubmed23n0026_10853"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 85.0, "verification": 67.0, "llm": 1653.730666032061}}}
{"topic": "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease", "competency": "Diagnosis", "stem": "A 65-year-old male with a history of smoking presents with exertional dyspnea. Pulmonary function testing reveals a reduced transfer factor (diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide) at rest. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in evaluating for exertional hypoxemia?", "options": {"A": "Measure arterial oxygen tension during exercise testing.", "B": "Perform high-resolution CT of the chest.", "C": "Initiate long-term oxygen therapy.", "D": "Order a serum alpha-1 antitrypsin level."}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "In patients with exertional dyspnea and a reduced transfer factor at rest, there is a strong correlation between the transfer factor and arterial oxygen tension during maximal exercise. Measurement of the transfer factor can serve as a screening tool, and exercise blood gas analysis is recommended for further evaluation in patients with a transfer factor between 55% and 80% of predicted. Therefore, measuring arterial oxygen tension during exercise is the most appropriate next step to assess for exertional hypoxemia.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0048_14214", "title": "The relationship between the transfer factor obtained at rest, and arterial oxygen tension during exercise, in patients with miscellaneous pulmonary diseases.", "span_text": "Independent of spirometric findings and diagnosis a significant correlation (r = 0.80, P &lt; 0.001) was shown to exist between the transfer factor measured at rest and the arterial oxygen tension at maximal exercise level. We recommend measurement of the transfer factor as a screening test for exertional hypoxaemia and suggest that exercise blood gas analysis only seems necessary in patients with a transfer factor between 55% and 80% of the predicted value."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "diagnosis of COPD exertional dyspnea transfer factor exercise testing", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0026_2112", "InternalMed_Harrison_23994", "pubmed23n0010_4736", "pubmed23n0048_14214", "pubmed23n0033_2730", "pubmed23n0059_19498", "pubmed23n0040_7035", "Pediatrics_Nelson_443", "pubmed23n0060_18841", "pubmed23n0054_20199", "pubmed23n0053_21069", "pubmed23n0028_11243", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1599", "pubmed23n0062_1477", "pubmed23n0021_891", "pubmed23n0028_5127", "InternalMed_Harrison_26171", "pubmed23n0050_15742", "pubmed23n0051_16909", "pubmed23n0052_17360", "pubmed23n0021_11110", "pubmed23n0031_1734", "pubmed23n0054_1438", "pubmed23n0018_10703", "pubmed23n0044_5060", "pubmed23n0062_11087", "pubmed23n0051_9435", "Gynecology_Novak_3379", "pubmed23n0061_8494", "pubmed23n0029_6485", "pubmed23n0060_4832", "pubmed23n0045_2021", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1924", "pubmed23n0030_1621", "pubmed23n0017_4708", "pubmed23n0044_10139", "Gynecology_Novak_5476", "pubmed23n0036_7426", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3004", "pubmed23n0029_360", "pubmed23n0043_15626", "InternalMed_Harrison_26020", "pubmed23n0028_2093", "pubmed23n0058_15056", "pubmed23n0054_9863", "pubmed23n0049_6708", "pubmed23n0062_11242", "pubmed23n0062_8258", "pubmed23n0053_16472", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3292", "InternalMed_Harrison_2580", "pubmed23n0015_4995", "pubmed23n0016_5876", "pubmed23n0026_2893", "pubmed23n0024_5709", "pubmed23n0012_40", "InternalMed_Harrison_19881", "InternalMed_Harrison_23259", "InternalMed_Harrison_19673", "InternalMed_Harrison_31359", "InternalMed_Harrison_25595", "pubmed23n0017_629", "pubmed23n0029_54", "pubmed23n0057_24290", "pubmed23n0062_8891", "pubmed23n0020_1117", "pubmed23n0054_13137", "pubmed23n0041_7284", "pubmed23n0027_1630", "pubmed23n0046_18097", "pubmed23n0010_491", "pubmed23n0025_2237", "pubmed23n0053_1050", "pubmed23n0057_23367", "pubmed23n0017_6764", "pubmed23n0050_19193", "pubmed23n0063_2568", "pubmed23n0013_10792", "pubmed23n0016_11334", "pubmed23n0050_2691", "pubmed23n0051_4745", "pubmed23n0060_816", "pubmed23n0035_217", "pubmed23n0056_11044", "pubmed23n0048_12294", "pubmed23n00