{"topic": "Anemia", "competency": "Diagnosis", "stem": "A 6-year-old child presents with fatigue, pallor, and mild jaundice. Laboratory results show anemia, reticulocytosis, and elevated indirect bilirubin. The direct Coombs test is positive. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Autoimmune hemolytic anemia", "B": "Iron deficiency anemia", "C": "Aplastic anemia", "D": "Sickle cell anemia"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The presence of anemia, reticulocytosis, and elevated indirect bilirubin indicates hemolysis. A positive direct Coombs test confirms the diagnosis of autoimmune hemolytic anemia by demonstrating antibody-mediated red blood cell destruction, distinguishing it from other forms of hemolytic anemia.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Surgery_Schwartz_10044", "title": "Surgery_Schwartz Text: Surgery_Schwartz", "span_text": "The diagnosis relies on demonstrat-ing hemolysis as indicated by anemia, reticulocytosis, and/or products of red blood cell destruction, including bilirubin, in the blood, urine, and stool. A positive result on direct Coombs\u2019 test confirms the AIHA diagnosis by distinguishing autoimmune from other forms of hemolytic anemia."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "diagnosis of autoimmune hemolytic anemia laboratory findings", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0015_9331", "pubmed23n0015_4995", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1924", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3308", "Surgery_Schwartz_10044", "pubmed23n0010_6579", "pubmed23n0058_17636", "InternalMed_Harrison_8213", "pubmed23n0020_5155", "pubmed23n0062_17803"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0015_9331", "pubmed23n0015_4995", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1924", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3308", "Surgery_Schwartz_10044", "pubmed23n0010_6579", "pubmed23n0058_17636", "InternalMed_Harrison_8213", "pubmed23n0020_5155", "pubmed23n0062_17803"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "score": "0.98", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Anemia Diagnosis diagnosis differential diagnosis signs symptoms screening clinical features presentation diagnostic criteria workup evaluation diagnostic test laboratory findings imaging", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 5801.888708025217, "llm_ms": 1163.5259579634294, "verification_ms": 1043.8116249861196, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0015_9331", "pubmed23n0015_4995", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1924", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3308", "Surgery_Schwartz_10044", "pubmed23n0010_6579", "pubmed23n0058_17636", "InternalMed_Harrison_8213", "pubmed23n0020_5155", "pubmed23n0062_17803"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "verification": 30.0, "llm": 1163.5259579634294}}}
{"topic": "Deep vein thrombosis", "competency": "Pathophysiology", "stem": "Which of the following mechanisms is most directly implicated in the pathogenesis of venous thrombosis due to increased platelet activity?", "options": {"A": "Platelet coagulant activities that catalyze intrinsic coagulation reactions on the platelet surface", "B": "Increased release of tissue-type plasminogen activator in response to endothelial injury", "C": "Enhanced neutrophil phagocytosis of platelet-derived microparticles", "D": "Direct activation of anticoagulant pathways by platelet factor 3"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Increased platelet coagulant activities\u2014such as contact product forming activity, collagen-induced coagulant activity, intrinsic factor Xa forming activity, and platelet factor 3 activity\u2014have been found in patients with deep vein thrombosis. These activities promote fibrin formation by catalyzing intrinsic coagulation reactions on the platelet surface, contributing directly to thrombus formation.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0038_8200", "title": "The possible role of platelet coagulant activities in the pathogenesis of venous thrombosis.", "span_text": "Recent studies of the role of platelets in blood coagulation have shown that platelets can trigger intrinsic coagulation by two alternative pathways, protect platelet-associated active clotting factors from inactivation by plasma inhibitors and catalyze intrinsic coagulation reactions on the platelet surface to form fibrin. These platelet coagulant activities (i.e., contact product forming activity, collagen-induced coagulant activity, intrinsic factor Xa forming activity and platelet factor 3 activity) were found to be increased in patients with deep vein thrombosis developing after hip surgery, in patients with established retinal vein thrombosis and in patients with established deep vein thrombosis. It is suggested that increases in platelet coagulant activities concerned with triggering and catalyzing intrinsic coagulation reactions may play a role in the pathogenesis of venous thrombosis."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "platelet role in deep vein thrombosis pathophysiology", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0061_13507", "InternalMed_Harrison_19653", "pubmed23n0026_7298", "pubmed23n0063_1900", "InternalMed_Harrison_9290", "InternalMed_Harrison_19849", "pubmed23n0048_6008", "pubmed23n0046_3715", "pubmed23n0060_1744", "pubmed23n0038_8200", "Pathology_Robbins_717", "pubmed23n0029_6845", "Pathology_Robbins_2401", "pubmed23n0018_1347", "pubmed23n0050_7742", "pubmed23n0050_6562", "InternalMed_Harrison_19658", "InternalMed_Harrison_9296", "pubmed23n0048_19489", "pubmed23n0037_2479", "pubmed23n0017_3970", "pubmed23n0055_9827", "pubmed23n0056_22812", "pubmed23n0051_1768", "pubmed23n0036_3438", "pubmed23n0058_13213", "pubmed23n0058_18274", "pubmed23n0058_11857", "InternalMed_Harrison_19048", "Surgery_Schwartz_6756", "pubmed23n0016_12071", "pubmed23n0059_18076", "pubmed23n0015_1536", "pubmed23n0061_1163", "Obstentrics_Williams_7242", "pubmed23n0042_5550", "Surgery_Schwartz_6762", "InternalMed_Harrison_25140", "pubmed23n0044_2412", "pubmed23n0033_10472", "pubmed23n0046_16951", "pubmed23n0027_15365", "pubmed23n0050_21776", "pubmed23n0032_9937", "pubmed23n0051_19759", "pubmed23n0026_7053", "pubmed23n0054_1348", "pubmed23n0020_4299", "pubmed23n0015_5132", "pubmed23n0046_11817", "pubmed23n0043_21437", "pubmed23n0021_10771", "pubmed23n0043_10403", "pubmed23n0044_2413", "pubmed23n0054_13010", "pubmed23n0029_11456", "Pathology_Robbins_2359", "pubmed23n0042_1771", "InternalMed_Harrison_19836", "Obstentrics_Williams_7121", "pubmed23n0063_6055", "pubmed23n0046_5629", "pubmed23n0041_10764", "pubmed23n0062_5157", "pubmed23n0054_5613", "pubmed23n0003_4295", "pubmed23n0057_3308", "pubmed23n0057_21160", "pubmed23n0025_6795", "pubmed23n0053_13619", "pubmed23n0042_7913", "pubmed23n0063_192", "pubmed23n0049_8776", "pubmed23n0046_18890", "pubmed23n00