{"topic": "Breast cancer", "competency": "Diagnosis", "stem": "A 52-year-old woman presents with a palpable breast mass. Mammography reveals a suspicious lesion, and ultrasound is performed to further characterize the finding. The ultrasound shows a well-circumscribed, anechoic lesion with posterior acoustic enhancement. What is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Diagnose a simple cyst and discharge the patient without further intervention", "B": "Proceed with core needle biopsy to rule out malignancy", "C": "Perform fine-needle aspiration as the definitive diagnostic test", "D": "Recommend surgical excision due to the risk of occult cancer"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Ultrasound is highly accurate in distinguishing cystic from solid breast masses. A simple cyst on ultrasound\u2014defined as a well-circumscribed, anechoic lesion with posterior acoustic enhancement\u2014has a nearly 100% diagnostic accuracy for benignity when strict criteria are met. In such cases, no further workup, including aspiration, biopsy, or follow-up, is required. Therefore, the appropriate management is to diagnose a simple cyst and discharge the patient without additional intervention.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0063_11624", "title": "Breast sonography.", "span_text": "If strict sonographic criteria are used for a simple cyst, the diagnostic accuracy approaches 100%. Sonographic diagnosis of a simple cyst precludes the need for further workup, including aspiration, biopsy, or followup."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "ultrasound diagnosis of breast cysts and management", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0060_18841", "pubmed23n0054_20199", "pubmed23n0017_1983", "pubmed23n0014_9733", "pubmed23n0036_7308", "pubmed23n0054_1438", "Gynecology_Novak_3379", "pubmed23n0063_11624", "pubmed23n0018_4903", "pubmed23n0041_8955", "InternalMed_Harrison_28583", "pubmed23n0030_1016", "pubmed23n0053_4263", "pubmed23n0016_5680", "pu