{"topic": "GERD", "competency": "Prevention", "stem": "Which of the following is a potential consequence of the widespread absence of Helicobacter pylori in a population?", "options": {"A": "Increased risk of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) complications", "B": "Reduced incidence of peptic ulcer disease", "C": "Lower rates of childhood-onset asthma", "D": "Decreased risk of gastric adenocarcinoma"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Lifelong absence of H. pylori is a risk factor for GERD complications, including esophageal adenocarcinoma. The disappearance of H. pylori may contribute to the rising incidence of GERD and its associated complications in populations where H. pylori colonization has declined.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "InternalMed_Harrison_12603", "title": "InternalMed_Harrison", "span_text": "For example, lifelong absence of H. pylori is a risk factor for GERD complications, including esophageal adenocarcinoma."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "GERD prevention H. pylori", "results": ["InternalMed_Harrison_12603"], "doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_12603"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_12603"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "confirmed": true, "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "GERD Prevention prevention prophylaxis risk reduction screening vaccination primary prevention secondary prevention lifestyle modification counseling public health early detection follow-up", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 5108.141375007108, "llm_ms": 967.5143749918789, "verification_ms": 909.2073329957202, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "fa