{"topic": "Peptic ulcer disease", "competency": "Prevention", "stem": "Which of the following is considered a primary prevention strategy for peptic ulcer disease in populations?", "options": {"A": "Eradication of Helicobacter pylori in asymptomatic individuals", "B": "Long-term use of proton pump inhibitors in patients with a history of ulcers", "C": "Surgical intervention for refractory peptic ulcers", "D": "Screening for gastric adenocarcinoma in patients with chronic gastritis"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Primary prevention of peptic ulcer disease involves preventing the initial occurrence of ulcers. Since Helicobacter pylori is a major cause of peptic ulcers, eradicating the organism in asymptomatic carriers can prevent the development of ulcers, making this a primary prevention strategy.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "InternalMed_Harrison_12603", "title": "InternalMed_Harrison Text", "span_text": "Carriage of H. pylori has considerable public health significance in developed countries, where it is associated with peptic ulcer disease and gastric adenocarcinoma, and in developing countries, where gastric adenocarcinoma may be an even more common cause of cancer death late in life. If mass prevention were contemplated, vaccination would be the most obvious method, and experimental immunization of animals has given promising results."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "primary prevention peptic ulcer disease H. pylori", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0014_766", "pubmed23n0059_18944", "InternalMed_Harrison_12603", "InternalMed_Harrison_19033", "pubmed23n0055_13775", "pubmed23n0049_8703", "pubmed23n0061_20690", "InternalMed_Harrison_294", "pubmed23n0060_4515", "InternalMed_Harrison_10756", "pubmed23n0055_13768", "pubmed23n0063_11753", "pubmed23n0048_17336", "pubmed23n0049_3300", "InternalMed_Harrison_25306", "pubmed23n0024_2414", "Gynecology_Novak_839", "pubmed23n0057_19176", "pubmed23n0050_7903", "pubmed23n0022_6533", "pubmed23n0