{"topic": "Allergic rhinitis", "competency": "Monitoring", "stem": "A 10-year follow-up study of children diagnosed with allergic rhinitis found that a significant proportion continued to experience symptoms into adulthood. Which of the following outcomes was most commonly observed in this cohort?", "options": {"A": "Complete resolution of all allergic rhinitis symptoms in over half of the patients.", "B": "Development of asthma or wheezing in 19% of patients, more commonly among those with perennial allergic rhinitis.", "C": "Universal requirement for ongoing medication to control nasal symptoms.", "D": "Conversion from perennial to seasonal allergic rhinitis in the majority of patients."}, "answer_key": "B", "explanation": "The 10-year prognosis study showed that asthma or wheezing developed in 29 cases (19%), and this was significantly more common among those with perennial allergic rhinitis compared to seasonal. This indicates that perennial allergic rhinitis is a risk factor for the development of asthma.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0050_15827", "title": "A 10-year prognosis for childhood allergic rhinitis.", "span_text": "Asthma or wheezing had developed in 29 cases (19%) and was more common (p less than 0.01) among those with perennial allergic rhinitis (15 of 44) than among those with seasonal allergic rhinitis (14 of 110)."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "prognosis and long-term outcomes in children with allergic rhinitis", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0050_15827", "pubmed23n0063_5717", "pubmed23n0062_9571", "pubmed23n0054_19861", "InternalMed_Harrison_16077", "pubmed23n0049_15933", "pubmed23n0027_6483", "pubmed23n0046_4884", "pubmed23n0048_19386", "pubmed23n0001_5978", "pubmed23n0061_13085", "pubmed23n0039_11511", "pubmed23n0057_9966"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0050_15827", "pubmed23n0063_5717", "pubmed23n0062_9571", "pubmed23n0054_19861", "InternalMed_Harrison_16077", "pubmed23n0049_15933", "pubmed23n0027_6483", "pubmed23n0046_4884", "pubmed23n0048_19386", "pubmed23n0001_5978", "pubmed23n0061_13085", "pubmed23n0039_11511", "pubmed23n0057_9966"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "direct_quote_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Allergic rhinitis Monitoring monitoring follow-up treatment response labs imaging vital signs disease control therapeutic monitoring adverse effects dose adjustment clinical assessment outcome tracking", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 7705.67808294436, "llm_ms": 1598.597583011724, "verification_ms": 1662.275165959727, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0050_15827", "pubmed23n0063_5717", "pubmed23n0062_9571", "pubmed23n0054_19861", "InternalMed_Harrison_16077", "pubmed23n0049_15933", "pubmed23n0027_6483", "pubmed23n0046_4884", "pubmed23n0048_19386", "pubmed23n0001_5978", "pubmed23n0061_13085", "pubmed23n0039_11511", "pubmed23n0057_9966"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "verification": 30.0, "llm": 1598.597583011724}}}
{"topic": "Neonatal sepsis", "competency": "Pharmacology", "stem": "A preterm neonate with jaundice is prescribed an antibiotic for suspected sepsis. Which of the following antibiotics is most likely to increase the risk of kernicterus in this infant?", "options": {"A": "Sulfonamide", "B": "Penicillin", "C": "Gentamicin", "D": "Vancomycin"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Sulfonamide antibiotics can displace bilirubin from albumin binding sites in neonates, increasing the amount of free bilirubin. Due to the increased permeability of the blood-brain barrier in neonates, this free bilirubin can enter the brain and cause kernicterus. This risk is particularly significant in preterm infants with jaundice.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Pharmacology_Katzung_6588", "title": "Pharmacology_Katzung", "span_text": "This was in fact observed when sulfonamide antibiotics were given to preterm neonates as prophylaxis against sepsis."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "sulfonamide neonatal sepsis kernicterus", "doc_ids": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_6588", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6618", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6568", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6566", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4042"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_6588", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6618", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6568", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6566", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4042"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Neonatal sepsis Pharmacology pharmacology drug mechanism indication contraindication adverse effects drug interaction dosage pharmacokinetics pharmacodynamics monitoring toxicity therapeutic target", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 7114.581832953263, "llm_ms": 2281.0174580081366, "verification_ms": 2387.9638749640435, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_6588", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6618", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6568", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6566", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4042"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "verification": 45.0, "llm": 2281.0174580081366}}}
{"topic": "HIV", "competency": "Pharmacology", "stem": "Which of the following antiretroviral agents directly inhibits HIV reverse transcriptase without requiring intracellular phosphorylation?", "options": {"A": "Foscarnet", "B": "Zidovudine", "C": "Emtricitabine", "D": "Lamivudine"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Foscarnet is an inorganic pyrophosphate analog that directly inhibits HIV reverse transcriptase by binding to the pyrophosphate binding site of the enzyme, without the need for activation via phosphorylation. This distinguishes it from nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors like zidovudine, emtricitabine, and lamivudine, which must be phosphorylated intracellularly to become active.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Pharmacology_Katzung_5364", "title": "Pharmacology_Katzung", "span_text": "Foscarnet (phosphonoformic acid) is an inorganic pyrophosphate analog that inhibits herpesvirus DNA polymerase, RNA polymerase, and HIV reverse transcriptase directly without requiring activation by phosphorylation. Foscarnet blocks the pyrophosphate binding site of these enzymes and inhibits cleavage of pyrophosphate from deoxynucleotide triphosphates."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "HIV reverse transcriptase inhibitor not requiring phosphorylation", "doc_ids": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_5464", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5883", "pubmed23n0057_16351", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5397", "InternalMed_Harrison_338", "pubmed23n0048_2651", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5641", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5364"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_5464", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5883", "pubmed23n0057_16351", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5397", "InternalMed_Harrison_338", "pubmed23n0048_2651", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5641", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5364"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "HIV Pharmacology pharmacology drug mechanism indication contraindication adverse effects drug interaction dosage pharmacokinetics pharmacodynamics monitoring toxicity therapeutic target", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 9908.668916032184, "llm_ms": 1101.6043749987148, "verification_ms": 808.1207919749431, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_5464", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5883", "pubmed23n0057_16351", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5397", "InternalMed_Harrison_338", "pubmed23n0048_2651", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5641", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5364"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "verification": 30.0, "llm": 1101.6043749987148}}}
{"topic": "Hyperlipidemia", "competency": "Pathophysiology", "stem": "A 45-year-old man presents with elevated serum triglycerides and cholesterol. Evaluation suggests a defect in lipoprotein metabolism. Which of the following is a primary physiologic regulatory site commonly affected in hyperlipidemia?", "options": {"A": "Lipoprotein lipase-mediated triglyceride catabolism", "B": "Intestinal absorption of dietary fats", "C": "Bile acid synthesis in the liver", "D": "Renal excretion of lipoproteins"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Hyperlipidemia often results from defects in one of four key regulatory sites in lipoprotein metabolism, including lipoprotein lipase-mediated triglyceride catabolism. Impairment in this process leads to accumulation of triglyceride-rich lipoproteins, contributing to hyperlipidemia.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0007_14259", "title": "Pathophysiology of lipoprotein transport.", "span_text": "Most hyperlipidemia can be accounted for by defects in one of four sites of physiologic regulation: (1) triglyceride-rich lipoprotein production, (2) lipoprotein lipase-mediated triglyceride catabolism, (3) remnant lipoprotein catabolism, and (4) extrahepatic cholesterol-rich lipoprotein catabolism."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "pathophysiology of hyperlipidemia regulatory sites", "results_returned": 1, "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0007_14259"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0007_14259"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match_in_evidence", "confirmed": "true", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Hyperlipidemia Pathophysiology pathophysiology pathogenesis mechanism molecular pathway cellular response inflammation hemodynamics neurohormonal physiology biomarkers dysfunction disease process", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 9281.785042025149, "llm_ms": 1636.2081660190597, "verification_ms": 770.8187910029665, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0007_14259"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "processing": 45.0, "llm": 1636.2081660190597, "verification": 770.8187910029665}}}
{"topic": "Myocardial infarction", "competency": "Screening", "stem": "Which of the following screening approaches has been shown to provide significant additional information to classical risk factors for predicting future myocardial infarction?", "options": {"A": "Automated serial ECG analysis", "B": "Thallium-201 myocardial imaging in asymptomatic individuals", "C": "Routine signal-averaged ECG in all adults over 40", "D": "Programmed electrical stimulation in low-risk populations"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Automated serial ECG analysis contributes significant additional information to risk estimates obtained from classical risk factors and yields stronger risk indicators than single measurements. This approach has been shown to be useful in cardiovascular screening programs for predicting future myocardial infarction.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0043_9816", "title": "Risk assessment of future myocardial infarction from automated serial ECG analysis.", "span_text": "Multivariate logistic regression analysis revealed that automated ECG measurements contribute significant additional information to risk estimates obtained from classical risk factors. Serial ECG analysis yields stronger risk indicators than single measurements. Results are represented via receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curves and regression coefficients, which can be used in independent applications. The results of this study indicate that it is useful to include automated serial ECG analysis in cardiovascular screening programs."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "screening for myocardial infarction risk prediction methods", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0018_7002", "pubmed23n0043_9816", "pubmed23n0058_2120", "pubmed23n0010_8834"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0018_7002", "pubmed23n0043_9816", "pubmed23n0058_2120", "pubmed23n0010_8834"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "score": "0.98", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Myocardial infarction Screening screening early detection risk assessment screening test sensitivity specificity guideline population screening high-risk preventive care follow-up diagnostic threshold", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 11950.950625003316, "llm_ms": 6824.860458029434, "verification_ms": 0.47679204726591706, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 0, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 1}, "failed_checks": ["evidence_span_mismatch"], "failed_checks_all": ["evidence_span_mismatch"], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0018_7002", "pubmed23n0043_9816", "pubmed23n0058_2120", "pubmed23n0010_8834"], "evidence_reused": false}, {"attempt": 2, "reason": "v2_requote", "query": "Myocardial infarction Screening screening early detection risk assessment screening test sensitivity specificity guideline population screening high-risk preventive care follow-up diagnostic threshold", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 5756.742500001565, "llm_ms": 1016.2695000180975, "verification_ms": 860.0637079798616, "extra_instructions": "re-quote exact evidence span", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0018_7002", "pubmed23n0043_9816", "pubmed23n0058_2120", "pubmed23n0010_8834"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "verification": 67.0, "llm": 1016.2695000180975}}}
{"topic": "Deep vein thrombosis", "competency": "Etiology", "stem": "A 35-year-old woman with no prior medical history presents with acute left leg swelling. She is not pregnant, has not undergone recent surgery, and is not currently hospitalized. However, she has been taking oral contraceptives for the past 6 months. Which of the following is the most significant acquired risk factor for her condition?", "options": {"A": "Use of oral contraceptives", "B": "Prolonged bedrest", "C": "Undiagnosed malignancy", "D": "Genetic deficiency of protein C"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The use of oral contraceptives is a well-established acquired risk factor for deep vein thrombosis. It increases the risk of venous thromboembolic events approximately threefold, particularly during the first year of use. In a young woman without other obvious risk factors, hormonal contraception is the most significant acquired contributor to thrombotic risk.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Pharmacology_Katzung_4538", "title": "Pharmacology_Katzung Text", "span_text": "The overall incidence of these disorders in patients taking low-dose oral contraceptives is about threefold higher. The risk for this disorder is increased during the first month of contraceptive use and remains constant for several years or more."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "risk factors for deep vein thrombosis oral contraceptives", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0017_10503", "pubmed23n0011_8448", "InternalMed_Harrison_5005", "pubmed23n0042_6838", "InternalMed_Harrison_4539", "pubmed23n0025_1441", "pubmed23n0016_6364", "pubmed23n0062_9005", "Surgery_Schwartz_6628", "InternalMed_Harrison_15241", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4538", "pubmed23n0047_9049", "pubmed23n0043_11188", "pubmed23n0050_396", "Anatomy_Gray_937", "InternalMed_Harrison_5284", "InternalMed_Harrison_19654", "pubmed23n0054_13010", "pubmed23n0051_9881", "pubmed23n0024_10535", "Anatomy_Gray_1669", "pubmed23n0049_15697", "Obstentrics_Williams_7242", "pubmed23n0011_11271", "pubmed23n0033_10592", "Gynecology_Novak_3658", "pubmed23n0026_8097", "pubmed23n0049_15690", "pubmed23n0052_3118", "pubmed23n0038_8200", "pubmed23n0048_9150", "pubmed23n0053_17230", "pubmed23n0018_10447", "pubmed23n0053_9303", "InternalMed_Harrison_2573", "pubmed23n0042_11586", "pubmed23n0057_20095", "pubmed23n0035_6346", "Gynecology_Novak_3657", "pubmed23n0040_8890", "InternalMed_Harrison_21922", "pubmed23n0047_6931", "pubmed23n0005_10960", "pubmed23n0048_14965", "pubmed23n0018_5231"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0017_10503", "pubmed23n0011_8448", "InternalMed_Harrison_5005", "pubmed23n0042_6838", "InternalMed_Harrison_4539", "pubmed23n0025_1441", "pubmed23n0016_6364", "pubmed23n0062_9005", "Surgery_Schwartz_6628", "InternalMed_Harrison_15241", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4538", "pubmed23n0047_9049", "pubmed23n0043_11188", "pubmed23n0050_396", "Anatomy_Gray_937", "InternalMed_Harrison_5284", "InternalMed_Harrison_19654", "pubmed23n0054_13010", "pubmed23n0051_9881", "pubmed23n0024_10535"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "score": "0.98", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Deep vein thrombosis Etiology etiology cause risk factors pathogenesis mechanism underlying cause genetic factors environmental factors predisposing factors associated conditions epidemiology incidence", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 13379.159958974924, "llm_ms": 1320.2117499895394, "verification_ms": 973.1381669989787, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0017_10503", "pubmed23n0011_8448", "InternalMed_Harrison_5005", "pubmed23n0042_6838", "InternalMed_Harrison_4539", "pubmed23n0025_1441", "pubmed23n0016_6364", "pubmed23n0062_9005", "Surgery_Schwartz_6628", "InternalMed_Harrison_15241", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4538", "pubmed23n0047_9049", "pubmed23n0043_11188", "pubmed23n0050_396", "Anatomy_Gray_937", "InternalMed_Harrison_5284", "InternalMed_Harrison_19654", "pubmed23n0054_13010", "pubmed23n0051_9881", "pubmed23n0024_10535"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 65.0, "verification": 45.0, "llm": 1320.2117499895394}}}
{"topic": "Postpartum hemorrhage", "competency": "Pharmacology", "stem": "A 28-year-old woman presents with significant bleeding following vaginal delivery. Which of the following medications is most appropriate for the management of postpartum hemorrhage due to uterine atony?", "options": {"A": "Methylergonovine", "B": "Bromocriptine", "C": "Epoprostenol", "D": "Aprotinin"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Methylergonovine is a uteroselective ergot alkaloid that causes sustained contraction of uterine smooth muscle and is used specifically for the prevention and treatment of postpartum hemorrhage due to uterine atony. It acts as a mixed partial agonist at 5-HT2 and \u03b1-adrenergic receptors with selectivity for uterine tissue. Bromocriptine is used to suppress lactation but is associated with cardiovascular toxicity and is not indicated for hemorrhage. Aprotinin has been removed from the market due to safety concerns, and epoprostenol is not used in this context.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Pharmacology_Katzung_1752", "title": "Pharmacology_Katzung", "span_text": "Uteroselective: \u2022ErgonovineMixed partial agonist effects at 5-HT2 and \u03b1 adrenoceptors Sameasergotamine\u2022someselectivity for uterine smooth muscle Postpartum bleeding \u2022migraineheadacheOral,parenteral(methylergonovine)\u2022duration2\u20134h\u2022Toxicity: Same as ergotamine"}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "drugs for postpartum hemorrhage pharmacology", "doc_ids": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_1752", "Pharmacology_Katzung_276", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3843", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1744", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5453", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3761", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3817"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_1752", "Pharmacology_Katzung_276", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3843", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1744", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5453", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3761", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3817"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "score": "0.95", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Postpartum hemorrhage Pharmacology pharmacology drug mechanism indication contraindication adverse effects drug interaction dosage pharmacokinetics pharmacodynamics monitoring toxicity therapeutic target", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 5646.776457957458, "llm_ms": 1741.29333300516, "verification_ms": 1031.8090000073425, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_1752", "Pharmacology_Katzung_276", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3843", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1744", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5453", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3761", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3817"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "verification": 30.0, "llm": 1741.29333300516}}}
{"topic": "Acute kidney injury", "competency": "Complications", "stem": "A 68-year-old man with a history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension is admitted with sepsis and develops acute kidney injury (AKI) during his hospitalization. He recovers renal function before discharge. Which of the following is the most significant long-term complication associated with AKI in this patient population?", "options": {"A": "Increased risk of cardiovascular disease", "B": "Development of chronic liver disease", "C": "Higher likelihood of recurrent pneumonia", "D": "Progression to type 1 diabetes mellitus"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Patients who experience acute kidney injury, especially those with underlying conditions such as diabetes and hypertension, are at significantly increased risk for long-term cardiovascular complications. AKI is recognized as a risk factor for the development or progression of cardiovascular disease, which is the leading cause of morbidity and mortality in patients with kidney dysfunction. This risk persists even after recovery of renal function.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "InternalMed_Harrison_21641", "title": "InternalMed_Harrison", "span_text": "Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of morbidity and mortality in patients at every stage of CKD. The incremental risk of cardiovascular disease in those with CKD compared to the ageand sex-matched general population ranges from 10-to 200-fold, depending on the stage of CKD."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "long-term complications of acute kidney injury", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0053_17698", "pubmed23n0061_16963", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1934", "Pathology_Robbins_1082", "pubmed23n0047_5691", "pubmed23n0048_7202", "InternalMed_Harrison_21641", "pubmed23n0052_9476", "pubmed23n0030_8211", "pubmed23n0028_9430", "InternalMed_Harrison_21487", "pubmed23n0016_1630", "pubmed23n0063_622", "pubmed23n0047_7719", "InternalMed_Harrison_8755", "InternalMed_Harrison_21649", "pubmed23n0062_3110", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6415", "Surgery_Schwartz_13389", "pubmed23n0062_6784", "pubmed23n0047_9618", "pubmed23n0049_19365", "pubmed23n0012_7416", "pubmed23n0051_11328", "pubmed23n0014_6129", "pubmed23n0030_11307", "pubmed23n0058_12689", "pubmed23n0047_3304", "Obstentrics_Williams_4942", "pubmed23n0049_1042", "pubmed23n0056_19136", "Surgery_Schwartz_13806", "pubmed23n0032_3863", "pubmed23n0061_13025", "pubmed23n0059_10495", "Pathology_Robbins_1083", "pubmed23n0056_8997", "pubmed23n0047_7496", "pubmed23n0053_11906", "pubmed23n0050_14166", "pubmed23n0059_757", "pubmed23n0026_2920", "pubmed23n0062_21051", "Obstentrics_Williams_6524", "InternalMed_Harrison_11332", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3447", "InternalMed_Harrison_25601", "Pediatrics_Nelson_889", "pubmed23n0051_5336", "pubmed23n0057_19210", "pubmed23n0063_8972", "pubmed23n0048_4150", "pubmed23n0058_5181", "pubmed23n0052_16488", "pubmed23n0063_20280", "pubmed23n0055_6430", "Surgery_Schwartz_13814", "pubmed23n0048_17520", "pubmed23n0031_2227", "InternalMed_Harrison_17723", "InternalMed_Harrison_17243", "Surgery_Schwartz_5235", "pubmed23n0015_2500", "pubmed23n0059_5397", "InternalMed_Harrison_26060", "Surgery_Schwartz_9248", "pubmed23n0049_4462", "InternalMed_Harrison_19633", "pubmed23n0050_16311", "pubmed23n0043_17277", "pubmed23n0050_610", "pubmed23n0046_12100", "pubmed23n0022_5581", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3452", "pubmed23n0054_963", "pubmed23n0051_11867", "pubmed23n0048_5468"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0053_17698", "pubmed23n0061_16963", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1934", "Pathology_Robbins_1082", "pubmed23n0047_5691", "pubmed23n0048_7202", "InternalMed_Harrison_21641", "pubmed23n0052_9476", "pubmed23n0030_8211", "pubmed23n0028_9430", "InternalMed_Harrison_21487", "pubmed23n0016_1630", "pubmed23n0063_622", "pubmed23n0047_7719", "InternalMed_Harrison_8755", "InternalMed_Harrison_21649", "pubmed23n0062_3110", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6415", "Surgery_Schwartz_13389", "pubmed23n0062_6784"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "evidence_match", "result": "Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of morbidity and mortality in patients with kidney disease, including those with a history of AKI.", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Acute kidney injury Complications complications adverse outcomes sequelae comorbidity disease progression risk factors mortality morbidity hospitalization long-term effects severity clinical deterioration", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 16080.654499994125, "llm_ms": 1658.1427079509012, "verification_ms": 1323.089582961984, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0053_17698", "pubmed23n0061_16963", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1934", "Pathology_Robbins_1082", "pubmed23n0047_5691", "pubmed23n0048_7202", "InternalMed_Harrison_21641", "pubmed23n0052_9476", "pubmed23n0030_8211", "pubmed23n0028_9430", "InternalMed_Harrison_21487", "pubmed23n0016_1630", "pubmed23n0063_622", "pubmed23n0047_7719", "InternalMed_Harrison_8755", "InternalMed_Harrison_21649", "pubmed23n0062_3110", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6415", "Surgery_Schwartz_13389", "pubmed23n0062_6784"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "verification": 85.0, "total": 205.0, "llm": 1658.1427079509012}}}
{"topic": "Epilepsy", "competency": "Diagnosis", "stem": "A 65-year-old patient presents with new-onset seizures. Clinical evaluation and EEG show focal abnormalities. A CT scan is normal. Which imaging modality is most likely to identify the underlying cause of seizures in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)", "B": "Positron emission tomography (PET)", "C": "Single-photon emission computed tomography (SPECT)", "D": "Skull X-ray"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "MRI is particularly useful in patients with late-onset epilepsy who have focal seizures or focal EEG findings, especially when CT scans are normal or nondiagnostic. It has been shown to identify the cause of seizures in a significant proportion of such patients.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0063_15199", "title": "Magnetic resonance imaging and late-onset epilepsy.", "span_text": "MRI was diagnostic in 32% of the patients with partial seizures and/or focal EEG findings, as compared with 0% of patients without focal features (p less than 0.01). We conclude that MRI is useful in investigation of patients with late-onset epilepsy with focal features."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "diagnostic imaging in late-onset epilepsy with focal features", "doc_ids": ["Pediatrics_Nelson_443", "pubmed23n0017_6049", "pubmed23n0063_15199", "pubmed23n0032_11082", "pubmed23n0043_12470", "pubmed23n0047_13302", "pubmed23n0007_4758", "pubmed23n0062_6490"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Pediatrics_Nelson_443", "pubmed23n0017_6049", "pubmed23n0063_15199", "pubmed23n0032_11082", "pubmed23n0043_12470", "pubmed23n0047_13302", "pubmed23n0007_4758", "pubmed23n0062_6490"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "direct_quote_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Epilepsy Diagnosis diagnosis differential diagnosis signs symptoms screening clinical features presentation diagnostic criteria workup evaluation diagnostic test laboratory findings imaging", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 5869.528667011764, "llm_ms": 3480.2923750248738, "verification_ms": 2965.6912499922328, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Pediatrics_Nelson_443", "pubmed23n0017_6049", "pubmed23n0063_15199", "pubmed23n0032_11082", "pubmed23n0043_12470", "pubmed23n0047_13302", "pubmed23n0007_4758", "pubmed23n0062_6490"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "verification": 30.0, "llm": 3480.2923750248738}}}
{"topic": "Neonatal sepsis", "competency": "Investigation", "stem": "A term neonate is evaluated for suspected sepsis due to poor feeding and temperature instability. Which approach is most appropriate for the initial diagnostic evaluation given the limitations of definitive testing?", "options": {"A": "Use a combination of clinical, historical, and laboratory findings to guide management due to the low sensitivity of blood cultures.", "B": "Delay treatment until blood culture results are available to confirm infection.", "C": "Rely solely on maternal history of fever during labor to diagnose neonatal sepsis.", "D": "Perform an ultrasound as the first-line diagnostic test to confirm sepsis."}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The diagnosis of neonatal sepsis is challenging due to the lack of a definitive diagnostic test. Blood cultures, though used, have low sensitivity. Therefore, clinicians must use a combination of historical, clinical, and laboratory data to initiate timely treatment while minimizing unnecessary therapy in uninfected infants.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0062_17702", "title": "Clinicopathologic approach to the diagnosis of neonatal sepsis.", "span_text": "Perinatally acquired bacterial neonatal sepsis is a low-incidence, high-risk disease with a relatively benign treatment. Accurate diagnosis is difficult because there is no definitive diagnostic test; even blood cultures have an unacceptably low sensitivity. Therefore, the clinician must accept that a number of neonates will have treatment initiated for sepsis who do not have the disease. In order to treat rapidly all infants with sepsis and to minimize therapy for those without infection, historical, clinical, and laboratory data can be used together in a management approach to achieve optimal results."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "diagnostic approach to neonatal sepsis", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0062_17702", "pubmed23n0037_1777", "pubmed23n0043_188", "pubmed23n0002_8348", "pubmed23n0018_2369", "pubmed23n0022_8342", "pubmed23n0062_20157", "pubmed23n0056_12592", "Obstentrics_Williams_9063", "pubmed23n0052_12052", "pubmed23n0032_2513", "pubmed23n0016_5454"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0062_17702", "pubmed23n0037_1777", "pubmed23n0043_188", "pubmed23n0002_8348", "pubmed23n0018_2369", "pubmed23n0022_8342", "pubmed23n0062_20157", "pubmed23n0056_12592", "Obstentrics_Williams_9063", "pubmed23n0052_12052", "pubmed23n0032_2513", "pubmed23n0016_5454"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "score": "1.0", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Neonatal sepsis Investigation investigation diagnostic test laboratory findings imaging workup evaluation differential diagnosis clinical criteria biomarkers screening confirmatory test diagnostic accuracy", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 7393.856083000002, "llm_ms": 1738.9342920000104, "verification_ms": 931.5797919999795, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0062_17702", "pubmed23n0037_1777", "pubmed23n0043_188", "pubmed23n0002_8348", "pubmed23n0018_2369", "pubmed23n0022_8342", "pubmed23n0062_20157", "pubmed23n0056_12592", "Obstentrics_Williams_9063", "pubmed23n0052_12052", "pubmed23n0032_2513", "pubmed23n0016_5454"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "verification": 30.0, "llm": 1738.9342920000104}}}
{"topic": "Hypothyroidism", "competency": "Prevention", "stem": "Which of the following preventive strategies is most effective in preventing long-term complications of congenital hypothyroidism?", "options": {"A": "Newborn screening programs that include measurement of thyroid function", "B": "Maternal thyroid hormone supplementation during pregnancy", "C": "Routine thyroid ultrasound in infants with family history", "D": "Early childhood developmental assessments starting at 6 months"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Neonatal screening for hypothyroidism is a proven preventive mechanism that allows for early detection and treatment, preventing complications such as mental retardation.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Gynecology_Novak_695", "title": "Gynecology_Novak", "span_text": "Neonatal screening for phenylketonuria (PKU) and hypothyroidism are examples of effective mechanisms for prevention of mental retardation."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "prevention of hypothyroidism complications", "results": ["Gynecology_Novak_695"], "doc_ids": ["Gynecology_Novak_695"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Gynecology_Novak_695"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "confirmed": "true", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Hypothyroidism Prevention prevention prophylaxis risk reduction screening vaccination primary prevention secondary prevention lifestyle modification counseling public health early detection follow-up", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 6052.395458999968, "llm_ms": 918.632333000005, "verification_ms": 565.6039159999864, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 0, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 1}, "failed_checks": ["reviewer_error"], "failed_checks_all": ["reviewer_error"], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Gynecology_Novak_695"], "evidence_reused": false}, {"attempt": 2, "reason": "v6_retry", "query": "Hypothyroidism Prevention prevention prophylaxis risk reduction screening vaccination primary prevention secondary prevention lifestyle modification counseling public health early detection follow-up", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 6052.418750000015, "llm_ms": 1039.5860419999963, "verification_ms": 711.5474159999735, "extra_instructions": "Ensure the correct answer is directly supported by the evidence and quote the exact supporting span.", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Gynecology_Novak_695"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "total": 165.0, "llm": 1039.5860419999963, "verification": 711.5474159999735}}}
{"topic": "Allergic rhinitis", "competency": "Pathophysiology", "stem": "A 28-year-old patient with allergic rhinitis undergoes segmental antigen challenge as part of a research study. Bronchoalveolar lavage is performed 48 hours later. Which of the following findings would most likely be elevated in the lavage fluid during this late-phase response?", "options": {"A": "Eosinophils and eosinophil-derived granular proteins", "B": "Neutrophils and IL-8", "C": "Mast cells and histamine", "D": "Lymphocytes and IFN-\u03b3"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "In the late-phase allergic response, which occurs approximately 48 hours after antigen exposure, there is a marked increase in eosinophils and their granular proteins such as major basic protein, eosinophil-derived neurotoxin, eosinophil cationic protein, and eosinophil peroxidase. This is supported by findings from bronchoalveolar lavage in allergic rhinitis patients after segmental antigen challenge.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0058_5394", "title": "Immediate and late airway response of allergic rhinitis patients to segmental antigen challenge. Characterization of eosinophil and mast cell mediators.", "span_text": "Repeat segmental lavage 48 h later (late response) showed marked and significant increases in both low and normal density eosinophils as well as striking elevations of eosinophil granular protein levels (major basic protein, eosinophil-derived neurotoxin, eosinophil cationic protein, and eosinophil peroxidase). Leukotriene C4, but not tryptase, concentrations were also consistently elevated in late lavage samples. Further, the late lavage samples showed a significant increase in interleukin-5 concentrations that correlated with the presence of eosinophils and eosinophil granular proteins."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "eosinophils late phase allergic rhinitis pathophysiology", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0057_13963", "Immunology_Janeway_3573", "pubmed23n0051_18950", "pubmed23n0058_5394", "pubmed23n0044_2521", "Physiology_Levy_2802", "Pharmacology_Katzung_2163", "pubmed23n0046_22758", "InternalMed_Harrison_3951", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1831", "pubmed23n0021_10401", "pubmed23n0057_7922", "pubmed23n0057_16518", "pubmed23n0025_8566", "pubmed23n0056_20035", "pubmed23n0058_5836", "pubmed23n0026_7175", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1752", "pubmed23n0046_3830", "pubmed23n0055_19387", "pubmed23n0001_5220", "pubmed23n0048_20003", "pubmed23n0022_8121", "pubmed23n0057_1462", "pubmed23n0058_4812", "Physiology_Levy_2799", "pubmed23n0063_3406", "pubmed23n0046_396", "pubmed23n0057_4504", "pubmed23n0005_4961", "pubmed23n0051_1909", "pubmed23n0056_20344", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1811", "InternalMed_Harrison_24980", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1751", "pubmed23n0051_9471", "pubmed23n0052_2641", "pubmed23n0044_17723", "pubmed23n0048_19386", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1617", "pubmed23n0050_15827", "pubmed23n0042_4218", "pubmed23n0048_5859", "pubmed23n0056_21886", "pubmed23n0041_5439", "pubmed23n0051_17043", "pubmed23n0050_19134", "pubmed23n0044_24751"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0057_13963", "Immunology_Janeway_3573", "pubmed23n0051_18950", "pubmed23n0058_5394", "pubmed23n0044_2521", "Physiology_Levy_2802", "Pharmacology_Katzung_2163", "pubmed23n0046_22758", "InternalMed_Harrison_3951", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1831", "pubmed23n0021_10401", "pubmed23n0057_7922", "pubmed23n0057_16518", "pubmed23n0025_8566", "pubmed23n0056_20035", "pubmed23n0058_5836", "pubmed23n0026_7175", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1752", "pubmed23n0046_3830", "pubmed23n0055_19387"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"confirmed_by": "pubmed23n0058_5394", "consistency": "high", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Allergic rhinitis Pathophysiology pathophysiology pathogenesis mechanism molecular pathway cellular response inflammation hemodynamics neurohormonal physiology biomarkers dysfunction disease process", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 9480.861624999989, "llm_ms": 1703.538999999978, "verification_ms": 747.8129579999973, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0057_13963", "Immunology_Janeway_3573", "pubmed23n0051_18950", "pubmed23n0058_5394", "pubmed23n0044_2521", "Physiology_Levy_2802", "Pharmacology_Katzung_2163", "pubmed23n0046_22758", "InternalMed_Harrison_3951", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1831", "pubmed23n0021_10401", "pubmed23n0057_7922", "pubmed23n0057_16518", "pubmed23n0025_8566", "pubmed23n0056_20035", "pubmed23n0058_5836", "pubmed23n0026_7175", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1752", "pubmed23n0046_3830", "pubmed23n0055_19387"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 65.0, "verification": 43.0, "llm": 1703.538999999978}}}
{"topic": "Asthma", "competency": "Complications", "stem": "A 28-year-old patient with acute severe asthma is admitted to the ICU and requires mechanical ventilation. Which of the following complications is most likely to occur in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Hypotension", "B": "Diabetic ketoacidosis", "C": "Acute renal failure", "D": "Hepatic encephalopathy"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Patients with acute severe asthma who require mechanical ventilation are at high risk for complications such as hypotension, pulmonary barotrauma, and cardiac arrhythmias. These complications are almost exclusively seen in those who are mechanically ventilated. Hypotension occurs in up to 41% of such patients and is associated with dynamic hyperinflation and reduced venous return.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0050_19009", "title": "Risk factors for morbidity in mechanically ventilated patients with acute severe asthma.", "span_text": "Hypotension (18/88, 20%), pulmonary barotrauma (12/88, 14%), and arrhythmias (9/88, 10%) were entirely confined to patients who had been mechanically ventilated. There were no significant differences in ventilatory parameters, airway pressures, or blood gases between mechanically ventilated patients with and without complications."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "complications of mechanical ventilation in acute severe asthma", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0018_6146", "pubmed23n0053_6687", "Obstentrics_Williams_7116", "Surgery_Schwartz_13390", "pubmed23n0050_19009", "pubmed23n0048_20578", "pubmed23n0053_11329", "pubmed23n0045_2264", "pubmed23n0051_9670", "pubmed23n0001_10283", "pubmed23n0060_1502", "Obstentrics_Williams_6994", "pubmed23n0058_16140", "pubmed23n0052_17280", "Obstentrics_Williams_7112", "pubmed23n0059_18811", "pubmed23n0047_1145", "Obstentrics_Williams_6990", "InternalMed_Harrison_31956"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0018_6146", "pubmed23n0053_6687", "Obstentrics_Williams_7116", "Surgery_Schwartz_13390", "pubmed23n0050_19009", "pubmed23n0048_20578", "pubmed23n0053_11329", "pubmed23n0045_2264", "pubmed23n0051_9670", "pubmed23n0001_10283", "pubmed23n0060_1502", "Obstentrics_Williams_6994", "pubmed23n0058_16140", "pubmed23n0052_17280", "Obstentrics_Williams_7112", "pubmed23n0059_18811", "pubmed23n0047_1145", "Obstentrics_Williams_6990", "InternalMed_Harrison_31956"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "score": "0.95", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Asthma Complications complications adverse outcomes sequelae comorbidity disease progression risk factors mortality morbidity hospitalization long-term effects severity clinical deterioration", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 8070.731042000034, "llm_ms": 1558.2823749999761, "verification_ms": 809.8973749999914, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0018_6146", "pubmed23n0053_6687", "Obstentrics_Williams_7116", "Surgery_Schwartz_13390", "pubmed23n0050_19009", "pubmed23n0048_20578", "pubmed23n0053_11329", "pubmed23n0045_2264", "pubmed23n0051_9670", "pubmed23n0001_10283", "pubmed23n0060_1502", "Obstentrics_Williams_6994", "pubmed23n0058_16140", "pubmed23n0052_17280", "Obstentrics_Williams_7112", "pubmed23n0059_18811", "pubmed23n0047_1145", "Obstentrics_Williams_6990", "InternalMed_Harrison_31956"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 65.0, "total": 185.0, "llm": 1558.2823749999761, "verification": 809.8973749999914}}}
{"topic": "Stroke", "competency": "Prognosis", "stem": "A 35-year-old patient presents with an ischemic stroke and is found to have no significant underlying vascular risk factors. The patient recovers well neurologically over the first month. What is the most accurate statement regarding the long-term prognosis for this patient?", "options": {"A": "The long-term prognosis is favorable, with low annual recurrence rates and good recovery from neurological deficits.", "B": "The risk of recurrent stroke is high, exceeding 5% per year, requiring aggressive secondary prevention.", "C": "Most young stroke survivors die within five years due to cerebrovascular complications.", "D": "Long-term functional outcomes are poor, with fewer than 20% able to return to work."}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The long-term prognosis for ischemic stroke in young adults is favorable. Recovery from neurological deficits is usually good, the risk of recurrence is low (1.1\u20131.2% annually), and more than 80% of patients can resume work on a full or part-time basis. Recurrent events are more likely in those with pre-existing risk factors.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0049_12951", "title": "Long-term prognosis of ischemic stroke in young adults.", "span_text": "It is concluded that the long-term prognosis for ischemic stroke in the young adult is favourable. The recovery from neurological deficits is usually good (exceptions are occlusions within the internal carotid and middle cerebral arteries), the risk for recurrence is low (1.1-1.2% annually), and the social prognosis with respect to working capacity and family relation is fair."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "long-term prognosis ischemic stroke young adults", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0049_12951", "pubmed23n0022_11030", "pubmed23n0039_7037", "pubmed23n0028_4922", "pubmed23n0005_12236", "pubmed23n0054_778", "pubmed23n0047_2474", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3820", "pubmed23n0023_6061", "pubmed23