{"topic": "Iron deficiency anemia", "competency": "Etiology", "stem": "Which of the following is a well-established cause of iron deficiency anemia?", "options": {"A": "Chronic blood loss from gastrointestinal malignancy", "B": "Excessive dietary iron intake", "C": "Hyperferremia due to genetic overexpression of hepcidin", "D": "Increased erythropoietic drive from polycythemia vera"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Chronic blood loss, particularly from gastrointestinal malignancies, is a common cause of iron deficiency anemia due to sustained loss of iron-rich red blood cells. This is especially relevant in conditions like colorectal cancer, where occult bleeding from right-sided tumors can lead to iron deficiency anemia over time.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "First_Aid_Step1_412", "title": "First_Aid_Step1", "span_text": "Ascending\u2014exophytic mass, iron deficiency anemia, weight loss. Descending\u2014infiltrating mass, partial obstruction, colicky pain, hematochezia."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "etiology of iron deficiency anemia", "doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_1753", "InternalMed_Harrison_5626", "First_Aid_Step1_412", "pubmed23n0036_1511", "Psichiatry_DSM-5_2027", "InternalMed_Harrison_21456", "pubmed23n0033_2917", "pubmed23n0047_3214", "pubmed23n0062_17765", "InternalMed_Harrison_29123", "Surgery_Schwartz_7526", "InternalMed_Harrison_2212", "InternalMed_Harrison_5625", "pubmed23n0054_7101", "InternalMed_Harrison_6920", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3617", "Pathology_Robbins_2740", "Pathology_Robbins_3826", "pubmed23n0020_10213", "pubmed23n0040_57", "Psichiatry_DSM-5_3390", "pubmed23n0018_6728", "pubmed23n0049_16179", "pubmed23n0015_13100", "pubmed23n0049_4400", "pubmed23n0044_19452", "pubmed23n0013_1171", "pubmed23n0041_4957", "pubmed23n0017_10497", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3182", "pubmed23n0048_20804", "pubmed23n0052_9089", "pubmed23n0049_4405", "pubmed23n0008_10114", "InternalMed_Harrison_21457", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3464", "Pediatrics_Nelson_137", "pubmed23n0057_19616"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_1753", "InternalMed_Harrison_5626", "First_Aid_Step1_412", "pubmed23n0036_1511", "Psichiatry_DSM-5_2027", "InternalMed_Harrison_21456", "pubmed23n0033_2917", "pubmed23n0047_3214", "pubmed23n0062_17765", "InternalMed_Harrison_29123", "Surgery_Schwartz_7526", "InternalMed_Harrison_2212", "InternalMed_Harrison_5625", "pubmed23n0054_7101", "InternalMed_Harrison_6920", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3617", "Pathology_Robbins_2740", "Pathology_Robbins_3826", "pubmed23n0020_10213", "pubmed23n0040_57"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "evidence_match", "score": "high", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Iron deficiency anemia Etiology etiology cause risk factors pathogenesis mechanism underlying cause genetic factors environmental factors predisposing factors associated conditions epidemiology incidence", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 8463.698041000043, "llm_ms": 2725.7412500000555, "verification_ms": 839.5800830001008, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["InternalMed_Harrison_1753", "InternalMed_Harrison_5626", "First_Aid_Step1_412", "pubmed23n0036_1511", "Psichiatry_DSM-5_2027", "InternalMed_Harrison_21456", "pubmed23n0033_2917", "pubmed23n0047_3214", "pubmed23n0062_17765", "InternalMed_Harrison_29123", "Surgery_Schwartz_7526", "InternalMed_Harrison_2212", "InternalMed_Harrison_5625", "pubmed23n0054_7101", "InternalMed_Harrison_6920", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3617", "Pathology_Robbins_2740", "Pathology_Robbins_3826", "pubmed23n0020_10213", "pubmed23n0040_57"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "total": 165.0, "llm": 2725.7412500000555, "verification": 839.5800830001008}}}
{"topic": "Preeclampsia", "competency": "Diagnosis", "stem": "A 32-year-old pregnant woman at 36 weeks' gestation presents with new-onset hypertension and headache. Laboratory tests show thrombocytopenia and elevated liver enzymes. According to current diagnostic criteria, which of the following is no longer required for the diagnosis of preeclampsia?", "options": {"A": "Proteinuria", "B": "Thrombocytopenia", "C": "Elevated liver enzymes", "D": "Headache"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Proteinuria is no longer an absolute requirement for the diagnosis of preeclampsia. Current guidelines allow for the diagnosis based on new-onset hypertension and other systemic findings such as thrombocytopenia, elevated liver enzymes, or renal insufficiency, with or without proteinuria.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "InternalMed_Harrison_552", "title": "InternalMed_Harrison", "span_text": "In December, 2013 The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists issued a report summarizing the findings and recommendations of their Task Force on Hypertension in Pregnancy. With respect to preeclampsia several pertinent revisions to the diagnostic criteria were made including: proteinuria is no longer an absolute requirement for making the diagnosis; the terms mild and severe preeclampsia have been replaced, and the disease is now termed preeclampsia either with or without severe features; removal of fetal growth restriction as a defining criterion for severe preeclampsia."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "diagnostic criteria for preeclampsia proteinuria", "doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_552", "pubmed23n0043_10754"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_552", "pubmed23n0043_10754"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Preeclampsia Diagnosis diagnosis differential diagnosis signs symptoms screening clinical features presentation diagnostic criteria workup evaluation diagnostic test laboratory findings imaging", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 6312.859583999966, "llm_ms": 913.7920000000577, "verification_ms": 938.9939169999479, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["InternalMed_Harrison_552", "pubmed23n0043_10754"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "verification": 45.0, "llm": 913.7920000000577}}}
{"topic": "Atrial fibrillation", "competency": "Diagnosis", "stem": "A 68-year-old woman with a history of hypertension and chronic kidney disease presents with palpitations and fatigue. Electrocardiography reveals an irregularly irregular rhythm without discernible P waves, consistent with atrial fibrillation. Which diagnostic test is most appropriate to identify potential intracardiac sources of systemic embolism in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Transesophageal echocardiography", "B": "Exercise stress testing", "C": "Holter monitoring", "D": "Cardiac MRI"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Transesophageal echocardiography (TEE) is the preferred method for detecting intracardiac sources of systemic embolism, such as left atrial thrombi, in patients with atrial fibrillation. It provides superior visualization of the left atrium and left atrial appendage compared to transthoracic echocardiography.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0053_12075", "title": "Conversion of atrial fibrillation to sinus rhythm: a possible side effect of transesophageal echocardiography", "span_text": "Transesophageal echocardiography (TEE) is accepted as the procedure of choice for the diagnosis of intracardiac sources of systemic embolism."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "diagnostic test for intracardiac embolism source in atrial fibrillation", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0033_2730", "pubmed23n0024_5709", "pubmed23n0041_801", "pubmed23n0006_1403", "pubmed23n0051_19322", "pubmed23n0016_11334", "pubmed23n0018_4149", "pubmed23n0035_3486", "pubmed23n0052_14503", "pubmed23n0033_11419", "pubmed23n0015_4649", "pubmed23n0003_11513", "pubmed23n0032_6541", "pubmed23n0053_12075", "pubmed23n0056_2553", "pubmed23n0021_10079"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0033_2730", "pubmed23n0024_5709", "pubmed23n0041_801", "pubmed23n0006_1403", "pubmed23n0051_19322", "pubmed23n0016_11334", "pubmed23n0018_4149", "pubmed23n0035_3486", "pubmed23n0052_14503", "pubmed23n0033_11419", "pubmed23n0015_4649", "pubmed23n0003_11513", "pubmed23n0032_6541", "pubmed23n0053_12075", "pubmed23n0056_2553", "pubmed23n0021_10079"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "score": 1, "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Atrial fibrillation Diagnosis diagnosis differential diagnosis signs symptoms screening clinical features presentation diagnostic criteria workup evaluation diagnostic test laboratory findings imaging", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 7124.191708000012, "llm_ms": 1175.458375000062, "verification_ms": 834.0680420000126, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0033_2730", "pubmed23n0024_5709", "pubmed23n0041_801", "pubmed23n0006_1403", "pubmed23n0051_19322", "pubmed23n0016_11334", "pubmed23n0018_4149", "pubmed23n0035_3486", "pubmed23n0052_14503", "pubmed23n0033_11419", "pubmed23n0015_4649", "pubmed23n0003_11513", "pubmed23n0032_6541", "pubmed23n0053_12075", "pubmed23n0056_2553", "pubmed23n0021_10079"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "total": 165.0, "llm": 1175.458375000062, "verification": 834.0680420000126}}}
{"topic": "Osteoporosis", "competency": "Prognosis", "stem": "A 58-year-old woman underwent a liver transplant 18 months ago and is on long-term immunosuppressive therapy including corticosteroids. What is the most likely long-term complication associated with her medication regimen?", "options": {"A": "Osteoporosis", "B": "Hypertension", "C": "Diabetes mellitus", "D": "Peripheral neuropathy"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Corticosteroid use is a well-known risk factor for osteoporosis in patients following liver transplantation. Long-term immunosuppressive therapy, particularly with corticosteroids, contributes to bone mineral loss and increased fracture risk.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0059_9002", "title": "Long-term results after liver transplantation in adults.", "span_text": "The main side-effects of drugs concern osteoporosis (corticosteroids), deterioration of renal function (cyclosporine), and de novo malignancies."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "osteoporosis prognosis corticosteroids liver transplant", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0059_9002", "Surgery_Schwartz_11056", "pubmed23n0060_18038", "Gynecology_Novak_4331"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0059_9002", "Surgery_Schwartz_11056", "pubmed23n0060_18038", "Gynecology_Novak_4331"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "score": "0.95", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Osteoporosis Prognosis prognosis outcomes mortality survival complications disease course recurrence risk stratification severity long-term outcomes quality of life predictors", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 9551.446333000058, "llm_ms": 1889.3689169999561, "verification_ms": 700.9783329999664, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0059_9002", "Surgery_Schwartz_11056", "pubmed23n0060_18038", "Gynecology_Novak_4331"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "verification": 60.0, "llm": 1889.3689169999561}}}
{"topic": "Epilepsy", "competency": "Prevention", "stem": "A 32-year-old man sustains a severe traumatic brain injury involving frontotemporal regions and remains in a coma for 8 hours. He experiences seizures in the acute post-injury period. Which intervention has been shown to reduce the risk of secondary mental disorders in such patients with traumatic epilepsy?", "options": {"A": "Administration of nootropic and long-acting psychotropic drugs", "B": "Long-term prophylaxis with phenobarbital", "C": "Intermittent rectal diazepam during febrile episodes", "D": "Immediate surgical resection of the contused brain tissue"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "In patients with traumatic epilepsy, particularly after severe brain injury involving frontotemporal areas and prolonged coma, the use of nootropic and long-acting psychotropic drugs has been shown to reduce cerebral asthenic symptoms and stabilize emotional disturbances, thereby helping prevent secondary neurotic and mental disorders.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0055_17797", "title": "[Prevention of secondary neurotic and other mental disorders in traumatic epilepsy]", "span_text": "Administration of nootropic and long-acting drugs brought about a reduction of the cerebral asthenic symptomatology and stabilization of emotional disturbances which promoted prevention of secondary neurotic and other mental disorders in patients with traumatic epilepsy."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "prevention of mental disorders in traumatic epilepsy", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0055_17797", "pubmed23n0027_7896", "pubmed23n0042_4914", "pubmed23n0048_1400", "pubmed23n0053_250"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0055_17797", "pubmed23n0027_7896", "pubmed23n0042_4914", "pubmed23n0048_1400", "pubmed23n0053_250"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "score": "0.98", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Epilepsy Prevention prevention prophylaxis risk reduction screening vaccination primary prevention secondary prevention lifestyle modification counseling public health early detection follow-up", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 5507.602249999991, "llm_ms": 1095.8630000000085, "verification_ms": 819.5880419999639, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0055_17797", "pubmed23n0027_7896", "pubmed23n0042_4914", "pubmed23n0048_1400", "pubmed23n0053_250"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "verification": 67.0, "llm": 1095.8630000000085}}}
{"topic": "Malnutrition", "competency": "Screening", "stem": "Which of the following best describes the primary goal of nutritional screening in older adults?", "options": {"A": "To identify individuals at risk for malnutrition to allow for early intervention", "B": "To provide specialized dietary counseling for all elderly patients", "C": "To replace comprehensive dietary assessments in all clinical settings", "D": "To diagnose specific nutrient deficiencies using laboratory testing alone"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The primary goal of nutritional screening in older adults is to prevent nutrition-related problems or enable early intervention by identifying those at risk. This helps improve health outcomes and quality of life.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0058_8961", "title": "Risk factors for poor nutritional status in older Americans.", "span_text": "Nutrition screening should prevent nutrition-related problems or allow early intervention. The elements that contribute to nutritional problems in the elderly must be clearly identified and prioritized. The desired goal of nutrition screening is a more vigorous older population able to maintain optimal nutrition, resulting in improved health and quality of life."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "goal of nutritional screening in older adults", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0016_4428", "pubmed23n0047_555", "pubmed23n0058_8961", "InternalMed_Harrison_5630"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0016_4428", "pubmed23n0047_555", "pubmed23n0058_8961", "InternalMed_Harrison_5630"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Malnutrition Screening screening early detection risk assessment screening test sensitivity specificity guideline population screening high-risk preventive care follow-up diagnostic threshold", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 7688.703167000085, "llm_ms": 1149.611584000013, "verification_ms": 778.2793339999898, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0016_4428", "pubmed23n0047_555", "pubmed23n0058_8961", "InternalMed_Harrison_5630"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "verification": 45.0, "llm": 1149.611584000013}}}
{"topic": "Migraine", "competency": "Pathophysiology", "stem": "Which of the following mechanisms is most strongly implicated in the pathophysiology of migraine based on current understanding?", "options": {"A": "Neurogenic inflammation involving the trigeminovascular system", "B": "Excessive skeletal muscle contraction in the scalp and neck", "C": "Primary dysfunction of cardiac autonomic innervation", "D": "Chronic cerebral vasodilation due to mitochondrial dysfunction"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Neurogenic inflammation within the trigeminovascular system plays a key role in migraine pathophysiology. Activation of trigeminal sensory nerves leads to the release of vasoactive neuropeptides, contributing to dural inflammation and pain. This mechanism is supported by pharmacological evidence, including the action of triptans in inhibiting neuropeptide release via 5-HT1d receptors.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0044_6073", "title": "Clinical effects and mechanism of action of sumatriptan in migraine.", "span_text": "Important pharmacological actions of sumatriptan are (i) poor penetration of the blood-brain barrier suggesting a peripheral point of action; (ii) 5-HT1-like/5-HT1d receptor-mediated vasoconstriction of large cerebral arteries and dural vessels; and (iii) blockade of neurogenic dural inflammation via 5-HT1d autoreceptor-mediated inhibition of vasoactive neuropeptides within the trigeminovascular system."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "migraine pathophysiology neurogenic inflammation trigeminovascular", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0062_3283", "pubmed23n0044_6073", "pubmed23n0052_2782", "InternalMed_Harrison_32366", "Neurology_Adams_1525", "pubmed23n0052_2778", "InternalMed_Harrison_30152", "pubmed23n0061_5231", "pubmed23n0050_1590", "InternalMed_Harrison_30121", "Neurology_Adams_6131", "pubmed23n0046_16862", "Neurology_Adams_1348", "Psichiatry_DSM-5_1624", "pubmed23n0039_8450", "pubmed23n0057_19597", "pubmed23n0046_5403", "pubmed23n0050_1589", "pubmed23n0054_7302", "pubmed23n0063_4421", "pubmed23n0052_16569", "pubmed23n0034_4324", "pubmed23n0044_20762", "pubmed23n0050_1592", "pubmed23n0002_9407", "pubmed23n0045_6951", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1710", "pubmed23n0050_4253", "pubmed23n0050_1595", "pubmed23n0058_6321"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0062_3283", "pubmed23n0044_6073", "pubmed23n0052_2782", "InternalMed_Harrison_32366", "Neurology_Adams_1525", "pubmed23n0052_2778", "InternalMed_Harrison_30152", "pubmed23n0061_5231", "pubmed23n0050_1590", "InternalMed_Harrison_30121", "Neurology_Adams_6131", "pubmed23n0046_16862", "Neurology_Adams_1348", "Psichiatry_DSM-5_1624", "pubmed23n0039_8450", "pubmed23n0057_19597", "pubmed23n0046_5403", "pubmed23n0050_1589", "pubmed23n0054_7302", "pubmed23n0063_4421"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "evidence_match", "score": "high", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Migraine Pathophysiology pathophysiology pathogenesis mechanism molecular pathway cellular response inflammation hemodynamics neurohormonal physiology biomarkers dysfunction disease process", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 7471.918542000026, "llm_ms": 1424.66704200001, "verification_ms": 1113.0614589999368, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0062_3283", "pubmed23n0044_6073", "pubmed23n0052_2782", "InternalMed_Harrison_32366", "Neurology_Adams_1525", "pubmed23n0052_2778", "InternalMed_Harrison_30152", "pubmed23n0061_5231", "pubmed23n0050_1590", "InternalMed_Harrison_30121", "Neurology_Adams_6131", "pubmed23n0046_16862", "Neurology_Adams_1348", "Psichiatry_DSM-5_1624", "pubmed23n0039_8450", "pubmed23n0057_19597", "pubmed23n0046_5403", "pubmed23n0050_1589", "pubmed23n0054_7302", "pubmed23n0063_4421"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 65.0, "total": 185.0, "llm": 1424.66704200001, "verification": 1113.0614589999368}}}
{"topic": "Acute kidney injury", "competency": "Treatment", "stem": "Which of the following represents a novel therapeutic strategy for acute kidney injury that targets both oxidative stress and mitochondrial DNA-driven inflammation in renal tubular epithelial cells?", "options": {"A": "A cascade-targeted core-shell nanotherapeutic agent with kidney injury molecule 1-mediated delivery and mitochondrial localization", "B": "High-dose intravenous thiamin supplementation", "C": "Early initiation of continuous venovenous hemofiltration", "D": "Strict glycemic control using insulin infusion"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The cascade-targeted core-shell nanotherapeutic agent STMB enables precise delivery to damaged renal tubular epithelial cells via kidney injury molecule 1 and localizes to mitochondria, where it scavenges reactive oxygen species and prevents mitochondrial DNA leakage, thereby interrupting the cycle of oxidative damage and inflammatory signaling in acute kidney injury.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed_web", "doc_id": "pubmed_web_41580683", "title": "Renal-tubular-mitochondrial sequentially targeted nanoagent breaks the vicious cycle of oxidative stress and mtDNA-driven inflammation in acute kidney injury therapy.", "span_text": "The present study developed a cascade-targeted core-shell nanotherapeutic agent STMB with L-serine shell that enables precise kidney injury molecule 1-mediated delivery to damaged RTECs, while tannic acid core facilitates mitochondrial localization. Tannic acid continuously scavenges reactive oxygen species. By simultaneously clearing reactive oxygen species and preventing mitochondrial DNA leakage by STMB, this dual-channel intervention successfully terminates the self-amplifying cycle of oxidative damage and inflammatory signaling, resulting in therapeutic effects in both cisplatin-induced and ischemia-reperfusion AKI models."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "novel treatments for acute kidney injury targeting oxidative stress and inflammation", "doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41582053", "pubmed_web_41582010", "pubmed_web_41582009", "pubmed_web_41580843", "pubmed_web_41580683", "pubmed_web_41580574", "pubmed_web_41580347", "pubmed_web_41579307", "pubmed_web_41578941", "pubmed_web_41578542", "pubmed_web_41578395", "pubmed_web_41578027", "pubmed_web_41578009", "pubmed_web_41576499", "pubmed_web_41575963", "pubmed_web_41575788", "pubmed_web_41575776", "pubmed_web_41575591", "pubmed_web_41575219", "pubmed_web_41574613"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41582053", "pubmed_web_41582010", "pubmed_web_41582009", "pubmed_web_41580843", "pubmed_web_41580683", "pubmed_web_41580574", "pubmed_web_41580347", "pubmed_web_41579307", "pubmed_web_41578941", "pubmed_web_41578542", "pubmed_web_41578395", "pubmed_web_41578027", "pubmed_web_41578009", "pubmed_web_41576499", "pubmed_web_41575963", "pubmed_web_41575788", "pubmed_web_41575776", "pubmed_web_41575591", "pubmed_web_41575219", "pubmed_web_41574613"], "retrieval_mode": "pubmed_web"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Acute kidney injury Treatment treatment therapy management drug dosage medication pharmacotherapy first-line second-line guideline intervention acute management long-term management", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 11640.696791999972, "llm_ms": 3943.5573750000685, "verification_ms": 0.4069170000775557, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 0, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 1}, "failed_checks": ["evidence_span_mismatch"], "failed_checks_all": ["evidence_span_mismatch"], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["InternalMed_Harrison_2200", "First_Aid_Step2_1063", "Gynecology_Novak_3795", "pubmed23n0047_956