{"topic": "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease", "competency": "Investigation", "stem": "A 55-year-old smoker with mild exertional dyspnea undergoes evaluation for early chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Which of the following diagnostic modalities is most sensitive for detecting early localized obstructive airway disease and identifying regional abnormalities in the lungs?", "options": {"A": "Spirometry with measurement of FEV1/FVC ratio", "B": "Perfusion lung imaging", "C": "Xenon or aerosol lung-imaging procedures", "D": "Arterial blood gas analysis"}, "answer_key": "C", "explanation": "Xenon and aerosol lung-imaging procedures are more sensitive than spirometry, perfusion imaging, or blood gas analysis for detecting early localized obstructive airway disease. These radionuclide imaging techniques can reveal abnormalities more frequently than standard pulmonary function tests and are particularly useful for identifying regional airway defects in patients with respiratory symptoms.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0028_11243", "title": "Early detection of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease using radionuclide lung-imaging procedures.", "span_text": "The aerosol and xenon lung-imaging procedures revealed abnormalities with approximately the same frequency as each other, but more often than any one group of pulmonary function tests, including spirometric data, maximal expiratory flow-volume curves, alveolararterial oxygen gradient, or indices derived from single-breath nitrogen washout. We concluded that xenon and aerosol lung-imaging studies are sensitive and useful screening procedures for detecting evidence of early localized obstructive airway disease and for locating regional abnormalities in the airways of patients with respiratory disease."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "diagnostic tests for early COPD detection", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0026_2112", "pubmed23n0028_11243", "pubmed23n0048_3420", "InternalMed_Harrison_32560", "pubmed23n0054_20199", "pubmed23n0015_9331", "pubmed23n0018_7002", "pubmed23n0042_7109", "pubmed23n0040_7035", "pubmed23n0010_4736", "pubmed23n0014_4992", "pubmed23n0048_14214", "pubmed23n0062_4194", "pubmed23n0012_40", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3004", "pubmed23n0051_16909", "InternalMed_Harrison_22254", "pubmed23n0020_9012", "pubmed23n0042_11058", "pubmed23n0051_3380", "pubmed23n0044_5060", "pubmed23n0015_8551", "pubmed23n0055_19211", "pubmed23n0015_6391", "pubmed23n0062_18963", "pubmed23n0048_14554", "pubmed23n0029_7233", "pubmed23n0026_5748", "pubmed23n0006_1007", "InternalMed_Harrison_19664", "pubmed23n0004_12662", "pubmed23n0030_1621", "pubmed23n0007_4725", "pubmed23n0026_2893", "pubmed23n0057_20630", "pubmed23n0045_2542", "InternalMed_Harrison_31321", "InternalMed_Harrison_24327", "pubmed23n0043_15638", "pubmed23n0039_7416", "pubmed23n0061_8494", "pubmed23n0017_6764", "InternalMed_Harrison_19673", "pubmed23n0023_3386", "pubmed23n0033_11122", "pubmed23n0046_2957", "pubmed23n0047_20303", "pubmed23n0054_16412", "pubmed23n0008_11327", "pubmed23n0050_11124", "pubmed23n0032_12239", "pubmed23n0048_11890", "Pediatrics_Nelson_443", "InternalMed_Harrison_23270", "pubmed23n0014_7109", "pubmed23n0017_629", "pubmed23n0038_3499", "pubmed23n0030_11306", "pubmed23n0033_12207", "pubmed23n0039_2746", "pubmed23n0026_885", "pubmed23n0054_13137", "pubmed23n0053_5112", "pubmed23n0051_6910", "pubmed23n0020_6791", "InternalMed_Harrison_28474", "pubmed23n0044_10139", "pubmed23n0050_19193", "pubmed23n0027_1630", "pubmed23n0052_17360", "pubmed23n0010_491", "pubmed23n0059_21389", "pubmed23n0041_6445", "pubmed23n0050_6411", "pubmed23n0061_8485", "pubmed23n0030_2916", "pubmed23n0059_14258", "pubmed23n0062_1477", "pubmed23n0035_5697", "pubmed23n0057_24290", "pubmed23n0018_7246", "pubmed23n0014_10831", "pubmed23n0063_506", "pubmed23n0060_17488", "pubmed23n0001_2893", "pubmed23n0040_10104", "pubmed23n0014_1537", "pubmed23n0045_15373", "pubmed23n0043_4478", "pubmed23n0043_17279", "pubm