{"topic": "Epilepsy", "competency": "Prognosis", "stem": "A 10-year-old child with epilepsy has been seizure-free for two years on antiepileptic medication. The parents are considering discontinuation of therapy. What is the most accurate statement regarding the long-term prognosis after drug withdrawal in such children?", "options": {"A": "Approximately 10% of children experience seizure recurrence after drug withdrawal, with recurrences typically occurring within three years.", "B": "Most children experience seizure recurrence within the first six months after drug withdrawal.", "C": "Seizure recurrence is common after three years of being seizure-free, even in those with normal EEGs.", "D": "EEG abnormalities are frequently observed more than three years after drug withdrawal."}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Approximately 10.9% of children experience seizure recurrence after antiepileptic drug withdrawal, and all recurrences occur within three years of discontinuation. No recurrences were observed beyond three years, and EEG abnormalities such as asymmetry or seizure discharges were not found after this period.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0025_7340", "title": "Long-term prognosis on recurrence of seizures among children with epilepsy after drug withdrawal-elimination.", "span_text": "The long-term prognosis of 110 children after withdrawal-elimination of the drugs on the basis of our rigid criteria was analyzed. The 12 cases (10.9%) of 110 patients experienced recurrences, which occurred between three months to three years after drug elimination, but there were no recurrences later than three years to 10 years after drug elimination."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "prognosis after antiepileptic drug withdrawal in children", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0016_10801", "pubmed23n0004_13213", "pubmed23n0055_404", "pubmed23n0025_7340", "pubmed23n0053_250", "Surgery_Schwartz_12423"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0016_10801", "pubmed23n0004_13213", "pubmed23n0055_404", "pubmed23n0025_7340", "pubmed23n0053_250", "Surgery_Schwartz_12423"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "score": "1", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Epilepsy Prognosis prognosis outcomes mortality survival complications disease course recurrence risk stratification severity long-term outcomes quality of life predictors", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 8292.925875000037, "llm_ms": 1115.4138750000584, "verification_ms": 800.170167000033, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0016_10801", "pubmed23n0004_13213", "pubmed23n0055_404", "pubmed23n0025_7340", "pubmed23n0053_250", "Surgery_Schwartz_12423"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "total": 165.0, "llm": 1115.4138750000584, "verification": 800.170167000033}}}
{"topic": "Acne", "competency": "Pharmacology", "stem": "A 24-year-old woman with moderate inflammatory acne has not responded adequately to topical benzoyl peroxide and topical retinoids. She is prescribed a medication that acts as an androgen antagonist. Which of the following drugs is most likely being used in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Spironolactone", "B": "Isotretinoin", "C": "Clindamycin", "D": "Danazol"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Spironolactone is an androgen antagonist used in the treatment of acne and hirsutism in women. It reduces sebaceous gland activity by blocking androgen receptors, making it effective for hormonal acne. Isotretinoin is a retinoid used for severe cystic acne, clindamycin is an antibiotic used topically for inflammatory acne, and danazol, although it has androgenic effects, is not used for acne treatment and may actually cause acne as a side effect.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Pharmacology_Katzung_4434", "title": "Pharmacology_Katzung", "span_text": "Spironolactone is also an androgen antagonist and as such is sometimes used in the treatment of hirsutism and acne in women. Dosages of 50\u2013200 mg/d cause a reduction in the density, diameter, and rate of growth of facial hair in patients with idiopathic hirsutism or hirsutism secondary to androgen excess."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "androgen antagonist acne treatment spironolactone", "doc_ids": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_4569", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6806", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6750", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4434", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6705"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_4569", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6806", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6750", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4434", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6705"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "score": "high", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Acne Pharmacology pharmacology drug mechanism indication contraindication adverse effects drug interaction dosage pharmacokinetics pharmacodynamics monitoring toxicity therapeutic target", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 7062.025041999959, "llm_ms": 1049.797999999896, "verification_ms": 802.674416000059, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_4569", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6806", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6750", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4434", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6705"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "verification": 60.0, "llm": 1049.797999999896}}}
{"topic": "Prostate cancer", "competency": "Pathophysiology", "stem": "Which of the following factors is most implicated in the multifactorial pathophysiology of sexual and physical dysfunction following radical prostatectomy?", "options": {"A": "Denervation and androgen deficiency", "B": "Hyperactivation of growth factor receptors", "C": "Persistent Ras p21-GTP binding", "D": "Microcirculatory ischemia"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Following radical prostatectomy, sexual and physical dysfunction are common and primarily result from denervation and androgen deficiency, which disrupt normal physiological processes necessary for erectile function and vitality.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "InternalMed_Harrison_534", "title": "InternalMed_Harrison", "span_text": "The pathophysiology of these symptoms after radical prostatectomy is multifactorial, but denervation and androgen deficiency are important contributors to these symptoms."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "pathophysiology of sexual dysfunction after radical prostatectomy", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0043_4325", "pubmed23n0049_4473", "pubmed23n0044_20761", "pubmed23n0055_20887", "pubmed23n0057_16261", "InternalMed_Harrison_534", "pubmed23n0057_1716", "pubmed23n0053_18083", "Pathology_Robbins_4253", "Histology_Ross_3674", "pubmed23n0049_11909", "pubmed23n0062_16314", "InternalMed_Harrison_7401", "pubmed23n0049_16863", "pubmed23n0043_4321", "pubmed23n0049_16862", "pubmed23n0062_1706", "pubmed23n0049_4467", "pubmed23n0050_6566", "Pathology_Robbins_4988", "InternalMed_Harrison_3816", "Surgery_Schwartz_3891", "Pathology_Robbins_4910", "pubmed23n0052_14395", "pubmed23n0050_19064", "Pathology_Robbins_4254", "InternalMed_Harrison_27371", "pubmed23n0007_11863", "pubmed23n0052_7450", "Surgery_Schwartz_2102", "Gynecology_Novak_560", "InternalMed_Harrison_536", "InternalMed_Harrison_7948", "pubmed23n0056_15306", "Pharmacology_Katzung_7182", "pubmed23n0047_3688", "pubmed23n0044_22220"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0043_4325", "pubmed23n0049_4473", "pubmed23n0044_20761", "pubmed23n0055_20887", "pubmed23n0057_16261", "InternalMed_Harrison_534", "pubmed23n0057_1716", "pubmed23n0053_18083", "Pathology_Robbins_4253", "Histology_Ross_3674", "pubmed23n0049_11909", "pubmed23n0062_16314", "InternalMed_Harrison_7401", "pubmed23n0049_16863", "pubmed23n0043_4321", "pubmed23n0049_16862", "pubmed23n0062_1706", "pubmed23n0049_4467", "pubmed23n0050_6566", "Pathology_Robbins_4988"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Prostate cancer Pathophysiology pathophysiology pathogenesis mechanism molecular pathway cellular response inflammation hemodynamics neurohormonal physiology biomarkers dysfunction disease process", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 8800.271167000006, "llm_ms": 1061.8355840000504, "verification_ms": 918.5703330000479, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0043_4325", "pubmed23n0049_4473", "pubmed23n0044_20761", "pubmed23n0055_20887", "pubmed23n0057_16261", "InternalMed_Harrison_534", "pubmed23n0057_1716", "pubmed23n0053_18083", "Pathology_Robbins_4253", "Histology_Ross_3674", "pubmed23n0049_11909", "pubmed23n0062_16314", "InternalMed_Harrison_7401", "pubmed23n0049_16863", "pubmed23n0043_4321", "pubmed23n0049_16862", "pubmed23n0062_1706", "pubmed23n0049_4467", "pubmed23n0050_6566", "Pathology_Robbins_4988"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "verification": 45.0, "llm": 1061.8355840000504}}}
{"topic": "Hepatitis B", "competency": "Pathophysiology", "stem": "Which immune mechanism is most strongly associated with hepatocellular injury in acute hepatitis B infection?", "options": {"A": "Cell-mediated immune response to hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg)", "B": "Humoral immune response to hepatitis B e antigen (HBeAg)", "C": "Passive transfer of maternal antibodies against HBsAg", "D": "Direct cytopathic effect of hepatitis B virus on hepatocytes"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The cell-mediated immune response to HBsAg is implicated in causing acute hepatic damage by attacking virus-infected hepatocytes. This immune-mediated injury, rather than direct viral cytopathy, is the primary mechanism of liver damage in acute hepatitis B.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0003_9861", "title": "Cellular and humoral immunity in viral hepatitis", "span_text": "Although antibody to HBsAg is found in virtually all following recovery from hepatitis B, a cell-mediated response to HBsAg can be detected in most patients during the acute phase, and it has been suggested that this may cause the acute hepatic damage by an attack on virus-infected cells."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "immune mechanism hepatocellular injury acute hepatitis B", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0002_8762", "pubmed23n0049_15263", "pubmed23n0003_9861", "pubmed23n0046_19718", "pubmed23n0055_20143", "pubmed23n0041_3305", "pubmed23n0008_9476", "pubmed23n0020_8865", "pubmed23n0026_11389", "pubmed23n0037_9165", "pubmed23n0044_8510", "pubmed23n0058_17561", "pubmed23n0056_13724", "pubmed23n0027_160", "pubmed23n0060_11259", "pubmed23n0037_2179", "Physiology_Levy_2885", "Pathology_Robbins_3866", "pubmed23n0057_2715", "pubmed23n0032_7986", "pubmed23n0053_10372", "pubmed23n0044_11637", "pubmed23n0005_5114", "pubmed23n0034_1435", "pubmed23n0010_7576", "pubmed23n0058_18602", "pubmed23n0059_8271", "pubmed23n0061_20316"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0002_8762", "pubmed23n0049_15263", "pubmed23n0003_9861", "pubmed23n0046_19718", "pubmed23n0055_20143", "pubmed23n0041_3305", "pubmed23n0008_9476", "pubmed23n0020_8865", "pubmed23n0026_11389", "pubmed23n0037_9165", "pubmed23n0044_8510", "pubmed23n0058_17561", "pubmed23n0056_13724", "pubmed23n0027_160", "pubmed23n0060_11259", "pubmed23n0037_2179", "Physiology_Levy_2885", "Pathology_Robbins_3866", "pubmed23n0057_2715", "pubmed23n0032_7986"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "evidence_match", "score": "high", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Hepatitis B Pathophysiology pathophysiology pathogenesis mechanism molecular pathway cellular response inflammation hemodynamics neurohormonal physiology biomarkers dysfunction disease process", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 6814.056040999958, "llm_ms": 1185.1259170000503, "verification_ms": 1380.2986670000337, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0002_8762", "pubmed23n0049_15263", "pubmed23n0003_9861", "pubmed23n0046_19718", "pubmed23n0055_20143", "pubmed23n0041_3305", "pubmed23n0008_9476", "pubmed23n0020_8865", "pubmed23n0026_11389", "pubmed23n0037_9165", "pubmed23n0044_8510", "pubmed23n0058_17561", "pubmed23n0056_13724", "pubmed23n0027_160", "pubmed23n0060_11259", "pubmed23n0037_2179", "Physiology_Levy_2885", "Pathology_Robbins_3866", "pubmed23n0057_2715", "pubmed23n0032_7986"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "total": 165.0, "llm": 1185.1259170000503, "verification": 1380.2986670000337}}}
{"topic": "Appendicitis", "competency": "Screening", "stem": "A 12-year-old boy presents to the emergency department with right lower quadrant abdominal pain, nausea, and low-grade fever. Physical examination reveals tenderness at McBurney's point. Which of the following diagnostic approaches is most appropriate as an initial screening strategy for acute appendicitis in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Measurement of white blood cell count with differential, C-reactive protein, and neutrophil-to-lymphocyte ratio, followed by clinical prediction tools and ultrasonography", "B": "Immediate diagnostic laparoscopy to confirm or rule out appendicitis", "C": "Empiric antibiotic therapy without further diagnostic testing", "D": "Plain abdominal X-ray to assess for bowel obstruction or perforation"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Initial screening for acute appendicitis should include laboratory testing such as white blood cell count with differential, C-reactive protein, and neutrophil-to-lymphocyte ratio, along with clinical prediction tools and ultrasonography as a first-line imaging modality. This approach balances diagnostic accuracy with safety, minimizing radiation exposure in pediatric patients.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed_web", "doc_id": "pubmed_web_41569907", "title": "Abdominal Pain Syndromes: Acute Appendicitis", "span_text": "Diagnosis should include the use of laboratory testing, including white blood cell count with differential, C-reactive protein level, and neutrophil-to-lymphocyte ratio, in addition to use of clinical prediction tools such as the Appendicitis Inflammatory Response score and diagnostic imaging. Although ultrasonography is a reasonable first-line imaging modality, its outcomes are user-dependent and studies are often nondiagnostic (ie, unable to rule out appendicitis)."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "initial screening approach for acute appendicitis in pediatric patients", "doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41573353", "pubmed_web_41570492", "pubmed_web_41569907", "pubmed_web_41567908", "pubmed_web_41564021", "pubmed_web_41563002", "pubmed_web_41561811", "pubmed_web_41558042", "pubmed_web_41552813", "pubmed_web_41552800", "pubmed_web_41552142", "pubmed_web_41546346", "pubmed_web_41546299", "pubmed_web_41546219", "pubmed_web_41546201", "pubmed_web_41545224", "pubmed_web_41541145", "pubmed_web_41537206", "pubmed_web_41531805", "pubmed_web_41525245"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41573353", "pubmed_web_41570492", "pubmed_web_41569907", "pubmed_web_41567908", "pubmed_web_41564021", "pubmed_web_41563002", "pubmed_web_41561811", "pubmed_web_41558042", "pubmed_web_41552813", "pubmed_web_41552800", "pubmed_web_41552142", "pubmed_web_41546346", "pubmed_web_41546299", "pubmed_web_41546219", "pubmed_web_41546201", "pubmed_web_41545224", "pubmed_web_41541145", "pubmed_web_41537206", "pubmed_web_41531805", "pubmed_web_41525245"], "retrieval_mode": "pubmed_web"}, "verification": {"method": "evidence_match", "score": "high", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "pubmed_web_fallback", "query": "Appendicitis Screening screening early detection risk assessment screening test sensitivity specificity guideline population screening high-risk preventive care follow-up diagnostic threshold", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 40662.965708, "llm_ms": 1542.4391249999871, "verification_ms": 1822.544500000049, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "pubmed_web", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": true, "docs": 20, "runtime_ms": 33009.37512500002}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed_web_41573353", "pubmed_web_41570492", "pubmed_web_41569907", "pubmed_web_41567908", "pubmed_web_41564021", "pubmed_web_41563002", "pubmed_web_41561811", "pubmed_web_41558042", "pubmed_web_41552813", "pubmed_web_41552800", "pubmed_web_41552142", "pubmed_web_41546346", "pubmed_web_41546299", "pubmed_web_41546219", "pubmed_web_41546201", "pubmed_web_41545224", "pubmed_web_41541145", "pubmed_web_41537206", "pubmed_web_41531805", "pubmed_web_41525245"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "total": 165.0, "llm": 1542.4391249999871, "verification": 1822.544500000049}}}
{"topic": "Diarrhea", "competency": "Etiology", "stem": "A 2-year-old child presents with acute watery diarrhea during the rainy season in Metro Manila. Laboratory analysis of stool samples identifies a viral pathogen as the most likely cause. Which of the following is the most common viral etiology of pediatric diarrhea in this setting?", "options": {"A": "Rotavirus", "B": "Enteric adenovirus type 40", "C": "Enteric adenovirus type 41", "D": "Cryptosporidium"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Rotavirus is the leading cause of pediatric diarrhea in Metro Manila, detected in 33.9% of diarrhea cases, with higher incidence during the rainy season when diarrheal diseases peak.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0055_13168", "title": "A comprehensive survey of pediatric diarrhea at a private hospital in Metro Manila.", "span_text": "Rotavirus was detected in 33.9% of the diarrhea stools examined and was the leading cause of diarrhea in the study population. Although proportionately more rotavirus was found during the cold season, most children became infected with rotavirus during the rainy season, when diarrheal disease was at its peak in Metropolitan Manila."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "etiology of pediatric diarrhea in Metro Manila rainy season", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0055_13168", "pubmed23n0052_12494", "pubmed23n0022_3184"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0055_13168", "pubmed23n0052_12494", "pubmed23n0022_3184"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Diarrhea Etiology etiology cause risk factors pathogenesis mechanism underlying cause genetic factors environmental factors predisposing factors associated conditions epidemiology incidence", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 6705.7917909999105, "llm_ms": 3766.74641600016, "verification_ms": 772.3738750000848, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0055_13168", "pubmed23n0052_12494", "pubmed23n0022_3184"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "verification": 45.0, "llm": 3766.74641600016}}}
{"topic": "Stroke", "competency": "Pharmacology", "stem": "A 72-year-old man with a history of nonvalvular atrial fibrillation and a creatinine clearance of 25 mL/min requires anticoagulation for stroke prevention. Which of the following is the most appropriate dosing regimen for dabigatran in this patient?", "options": {"A": "75 mg twice daily", "B": "100 mg once daily", "C": "150 mg twice daily", "D": "200 mg once daily"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "In patients with nonvalvular atrial fibrillation and reduced creatinine clearance (15\u201330 mL/min), the recommended dose of dabigatran is 75 mg twice daily. This adjustment accounts for decreased renal clearance and reduces the risk of drug accumulation and bleeding.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Pharmacology_Katzung_3801", "title": "Pharmacology_Katzung", "span_text": "For prevention of stroke and systemic embolism in nonvalvular atrial fibrillation, the dosage is 150 mg twice daily for patients with creatinine clearance greater than 30 mL/min. For decreased creatinine clearance of 15\u201330 mL/min, the dosage is 75 mg twice daily."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "dabigatran dosing in renal impairment for stroke prevention", "doc_ids": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_3801", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3817"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_3801", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3817"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match_in_evidence", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Stroke Pharmacology pharmacology drug mechanism indication contraindication adverse effects drug interaction dosage pharmacokinetics pharmacodynamics monitoring toxicity therapeutic target", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 5992.491707999989, "llm_ms": 7241.684500000019, "verification_ms": 1018.3110410000609, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_3801", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3817"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "verification": 45.0, "llm": 7241.684500000019}}}
{"topic": "Iron deficiency anemia", "competency": "Pharmacology", "stem": "A 45-year-old woman presents with fatigue, pallor, and exertional dyspnea. Laboratory tests reveal a low mean cell volume (MCV) and low mean cell hemoglobin concentration, consistent with microcytic hypochromic anemia. Further evaluation confirms iron deficiency anemia. What is the primary pharmacologic indication for iron supplementation in this patient?", "options": {"A": "To enhance oxygen delivery by supporting hemoglobin synthesis.", "B": "To chelate excess heavy metals contributing to anemia.", "C": "To stimulate erythropoietin production in the kidneys.", "D": "To inhibit hepcidin-mediated iron sequestration in macrophages."}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Iron is essential for the formation of heme, which combines with globin chains to form hemoglobin. Hemoglobin enables red blood cells to carry oxygen from the lungs to tissues. In iron deficiency anemia, insufficient iron leads to reduced hemoglobin synthesis, resulting in microcytic hypochromic red blood cells and impaired oxygen delivery. Iron supplementation directly addresses this deficiency by providing substrate for heme and hemoglobin production.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Pharmacology_Katzung_3619", "title": "Pharmacology_Katzung", "span_text": "Iron forms the nucleus of the iron-porphyrin heme ring, which together with globin chains forms hemoglobin. Hemoglobin reversibly binds oxygen and provides the critical mechanism for oxygen delivery from the lungs to other tissues. In the absence of adequate iron, small erythrocytes with insufficient hemoglobin are formed, giving rise to microcytic hypochromic anemia."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "pharmacology of iron supplementation in iron deficiency anemia", "doc_ids": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_6212", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4010", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3619", "pubmed23n0055_16874", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3084", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3634", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5305", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1043", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3635", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5232", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3648", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6173", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3618", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5928", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5368", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3617", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5765", "Pharmacology_Katzung_7085", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6954", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6459", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4002", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3761", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5231", "Pharmacology_Katzung_2547"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_6212", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4010", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3619", "pubmed23n0055_16874", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3084", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3634", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5305", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1043", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3635", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5232", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3648", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6173", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3618", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5928", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5368", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3617", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5765", "Pharmacology_Katzung_7085", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6954", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6459"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "evidence_match", "score": "high", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Iron deficiency anemia Pharmacology pharmacology drug mechanism indication contraindication adverse effects drug interaction dosage pharmacokinetics pharmacodynamics monitoring toxicity therapeutic target", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 8153.989832999969, "llm_ms": 1782.2018749998279, "verification_ms": 1428.3952079999835, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_6212", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4010", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3619", "pubmed23n0055_16874", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3084", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3634", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5305", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1043", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3635", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5232", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3648", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6173", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3618", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5928", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5368", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3617", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5765", "Pharmacology_Katzung_7085", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6954", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6459"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 65.0, "total": 185.0, "llm": 1782.2018749998279, "verification": 1428.3952079999835}}}
{"topic": "Epilepsy", "competency": "Screening", "stem": "A 65-year-old man presents with a history of ischemic stroke 6 months ago and no prior seizures. He has cortical involvement on neuroimaging and a history of a previous stroke. Which of the following is the most appropriate tool to assess his risk for developing unprovoked epileptic seizures?", "options": {"A": "SeLECT score incorporating NIHSS at discharge", "B": "Routine 24-hour ambulatory EEG monitoring", "C": "Serum autoantibody panel for neuronal surface antigens", "D": "Genetic testing for SCN1A mutation"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The SeLECT score is a validated prediction model for late poststroke seizures, and its performance improves when modified to include NIHSS at discharge. Cortical stroke location, higher NIHSS scores, and prior stroke history are significant predictors of vascular epilepsy, all of which are components of the enhanced SeLECT model.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed_web", "doc_id": "pubmed_web_41578597", "title": "Validation of SeLECT score and its modification for predicting unprovoked epileptic seizures in patients after ischemic stroke.", "span_text": "Vascular epilepsy was significantly associated with cortical localization of the ischemic lesion (hazard ratio [HR] 3.324; 95% confidence intervals [CI] 1.432-7.714; P = .005), higher NIHSS at discharge (HR 1.1; 95% CI 1.017-1.191; P = .018), and a history of prior stroke (HR 4.098; 95% CI 1.86-9.03; P < .001). Validation of the SeLECT score confirmed its predictive capabilities (area under the receiver operating characteristic curve 0.668; P = .005), and modifications incorporating NIHSS at discharge improved its performance (area under the receiver operating characteristic curve 0.715; P = .001)."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "prediction tool for post-stroke epilepsy screening", "doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41582482", "pubmed_web_41582304", "pubmed_web_41582223", "pubmed_web_41581458", "pubmed_web_41581268", "pubmed_web_41580946", "pubmed_web_41579792", "pubmed_web_41578597", "pubmed_web_41578547", "pubmed_web_41577840", "pubmed_web_41576843", "pubmed_web_41576839", "pubmed_web_41576697", "pubmed_web_41575783", "pubmed_web_41574838", "pubmed_web_41574403", "pubmed_web_41574142", "pubmed_web_41573391", "pubmed_web_41573381", "pubmed_web_41572956"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41582223", "pubmed_web_41581458", "pubmed_web_41581268", "pubmed_web_41580946", "pubmed_web_41579792", "pubmed_web_41578597", "pubmed_web_41578547", "pubmed_web_41577840", "pubmed_web_41576843", "pubmed_web_41576839", "pubmed_web_41576697", "pubmed_web_41575783", "pubmed_web_41574838", "pubmed_web_41574403", "pubmed_web_41574142", "pubmed_web_41573391", "pubmed_web_41573381", "pubmed_web_41572956", "pubmed_web_41582482", "pubmed_web_41582304"], "retrieval_mode": "pubmed_web"}, "verification": {"method": "direct_match", "confidence": "high", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "pubmed_web_fallback", "query": "Epilepsy Screening screening early detection risk assessment screening test sensitivity specificity guideline population screening high-risk preventive care follow-up diagnostic threshold", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 8061.354416999848, "llm_ms": 1272.788750000018, "verification_ms": 800.6428329999835, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "pubmed_web", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": true, "docs": 20, "runtime_ms": 3259.1207089999443}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed_web_41582223", "pubmed_web_41581458", "pubmed_web_41581268", "pubmed_web_41580946", "pubmed_web_41579792", "pubmed_web_41578597", "pubmed_web_41578547", "pubmed_web_41577840", "pubmed_web_41576843", "pubmed_web_41576839", "pubmed_web_41576697", "pubmed_web_41575783", "pubmed_web_41574838", "pubmed_web_41574403", "pubmed_web_41574142", "pubmed_web_41573391", "pubmed_web_41573381", "pubmed_web_41572956", "pubmed_web_41582482", "pubmed_web_41582304"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "total": 165.0, "llm": 1272.788750000018, "verification": 800.6428329999835}}}
{"topic": "Asthma", "competency": "Treatment", "stem": "A 28-year-old woman with moderate persistent asthma presents to the clinic for follow-up. She reports using her albuterol inhaler 4\u20135 times per week due to shortness of breath and nighttime symptoms. Which of the following is the most appropriate long-term management strategy to reduce airway inflammation and prevent exacerbations?", "options": {"A": "Initiate daily inhaled corticosteroids", "B": "Add oral theophylline as first-line therapy", "C": "Use inhaled ipratropium regularly", "D": "Increase frequency of short-acting beta-agonist use"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Inhaled corticosteroids are the most effective anti-inflammatory agents for long-term control of asthma and are recommended as first-line therapy for patients with persistent symptoms. Regular use reduces airway inflammation, prevents exacerbations, and improves overall asthma control. The patient's frequent symptoms indicate inadequate control, necessitating initiation of controller therapy.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0045_10934", "title": "Asthma in the 1990s. A new approach to therapy.", "span_text": "Asthma has been shown to be an inflammatory disease. Therefore, it makes sense to base treatment strategies on the selection of an appropriate anti-inflammatory agent, with bronchodilators being used as effective rescue medications. Because of recent concerns raised in the literature about the safety of long-term use of beta 2 agonists, early and appropriate medication in the form of inhaled corticosteroids or cromolyn sodium is recommended for daily control of asthma symptoms, long-term patient management, and prevention of acute exacerbations."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "first-line long-term treatment for persistent asthma", "doc_ids": ["First_Aid_Step2_1063", "Gynecology_Novak_3798", "pubmed23n0014_2698", "pubmed23n0045_10934", "pubmed23n0049_2331", "pubmed23n0058_459", "Gynecology_Novak_3795", "pubmed23n0040_8607", "pubmed23n0060_3301", "pubmed23n0027_11294", "pubmed23n0060_3302", "pubmed23n0028_10431", "pubmed23n0045_4161", "pubmed23n0051_21249", "pubmed23n0056_1175", "pubmed23n0060_10873", "pubmed23n0027_4029", "pubmed23n0060_1506", "InternalMed_Harrison_20148", "First_Aid_Step2_1061", "pubmed23n0014_8318", "pubmed23n0044_9426", "pubmed23n0046_5572", "pubmed23n0014_2828", "Pharmacology_Katzung_2110", "pubmed23n0001_10283", "pubmed23n0036_6908", "pubmed23n0045_11357", "pubmed23n0050_2993", "pubmed23n0044_15835", "pubmed23n0046_8215", "pubmed23n0048_2494", "pubmed23n0049_8549", "pubmed23n0056_9098", "pubmed23n0061_12104", "pubmed23n0018_6146", "pubmed23n0062_7993", "pubmed23n0044_16056", "pubmed23n0054_18928", "pubmed23n0056_3085", "pubmed23n0044_4340", "pubmed23n0054_5551", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1804", "pubmed23n0048_15488", "pubmed23n0050_18176", "pubmed23n0032_10564", "pubmed23n0058_9876", "pubmed23n0045_4558", "pubmed23n0053_6724", "pubmed23n0027_11187", "pubmed23n0006_811", "pubmed23n0015_8342", "Obstentrics_Williams_6999", "pubmed23n0002_9133", "pubmed23n0014_3720", "pubmed23n0043_5063", "pubmed23n0027_5217", "pubmed23n0014_3961", "pubmed23n0022_2866"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["First_Aid_Step2_1063", "Gynecology_Novak_3798", "pubmed23n0014_2698", "pubmed23n0045_10934", "pubmed23n0049_2331", "pubmed23n0058_459", "Gynecology_Novak_3795", "pubmed23n0040_8607", "pubmed23n0060_3301", "pubmed23n0027_11294", "pubmed23n0060_3302", "pubmed23n0028_10431", "pubmed23n0045_4161", "pubmed23n0051_21249", "pubmed23n0056_1175", "pubmed23n0060_10873", "pubmed23n0027_4029", "pubmed23n0060_1506", "InternalMed_Harrison_20148", "First_Aid_Step2_1061"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"confirmed_by": "pubmed23n0045_10934", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Asthma Treatment treatment therapy management drug dosage medication pharmacotherapy first-line second-line guideline intervention acute management long-term management", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 11822.94720799996, "llm_ms": 1327.6226250000036, "verification_ms": 851.9590409998727, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["First_Aid_Step2_1063", "Gynecology_Novak_3798", "pubmed23n0014_2698", "pubmed23n0045_10934", "pubmed23n0049_2331", "pubmed23n0058_459", "Gynecology_Novak_3795", "pubmed23n0040_8607", "pubmed23n0060_3301", "pubmed23n0027_11294", "pubmed23n0060_3302", "pubmed23n0028_10431", "pubmed23n0045_4161", "pubmed23n0051_21249", "pubmed23n0056_1175", "pubmed23n0060_10873", "pubmed23n0027_4029", "pubmed23n0060_1506", "InternalMed_Harrison_20148", "First_Aid_Step2_1061"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "total": 165.0, "llm": 1327.6226250000036, "verification": 851.9590409998727}}}
{"topic": "Psoriasis", "competency": "Prevention", "stem": "A 45-year-old patient with moderate to severe psoriasis is being evaluated for risk factors that may increase the likelihood of developing a related comorbidity. Which factor is most strongly associated with an increased risk of transitioning from psoriasis to psoriatic arthritis?", "options": {"A": "Obesity", "B": "History of atopic dermatitis", "C": "Chronic sun exposure", "D": "Frequent use of topical corticosteroids"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Obesity is a well-established risk factor for the development of psoriatic arthritis in patients with psoriasis. It contributes to systemic inflammation and mechanical stress, both of which are implicated in the pathogenesis of psoriatic arthritis. Early identification of such risk factors allows for timely intervention and potential prevention of irreversible joint damage.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed_web", "doc_id": "pubmed_web_41511760", "title": "Intercepting the psoriasis-psoriatic arthritis continuum: a precision-medicine framework for at-risk, subclinical, and early psoriatic arthritis.", "span_text": "Long-term factors (PsO severity, nail disease, family history, obesity) and short-term indicators (arthralgia, imaging-detected enthesitis or synovitis) help stratify PsA risk."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "risk factors for psoriatic arthritis in psoriasis patients", "doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41567565", "pubmed_web_41560049", "pubmed_web_41524178", "pubmed_web_41522850", "pubmed_web_41511760", "pubmed_web_41508855", "pubmed_web_41508833", "pubmed_web_41506322", "pubmed_web_41497686", "pubmed_web_41491530", "pubmed_web_41489168", "pubmed_web_41482938", "pubmed_web_41480785", "pubmed_web_41478660", "pubmed_web_41469525", "pubmed_web_41465919", "pubmed_web_41462491", "pubmed_web_41458680", "pubmed_web_41450497", "pubmed_web_41435608"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41567565", "pubmed_web_41560049", "pubmed_web_41524178", "pubmed_web_41522850", "pubmed_web_41511760", "pubmed_web_41508855", "pubmed_web_41508833", "pubmed_web_41506322", "pubmed_web_41497686", "pubmed_web_41491530", "pubmed_web_41489168", "pubmed_web_41482938", "pubmed_web_41480785", "pubmed_web_41478660", "pubmed_web_41469525", "pubmed_web_41465919", "pubmed_web_41462491", "pubmed_web_41458680", "pubmed_web_41450497", "pubmed_web_41435608"], "retrieval_mode": "pubmed_web"}, "verification": {"method": "evidence_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "pubmed_web_fallback", "query": "Psoriasis Prevention prevention prophylaxis risk reduction screening vaccination primary prevention secondary prevention lifestyle modification counseling public health early detection follow-up", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 7789.869290999832, "llm_ms": 1186.3739579998764, "verification_ms": 2257.252749999907, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "pubmed_web", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": true, "docs": 20, "runtime_ms": 3033.891249999897}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed_web_41567565", "pubmed_web_41560049", "pubmed_web_41524178", "pubmed_web_41522850", "pubmed_web_41511760", "pubmed_web_41508855", "pubmed_web_41508833", "pubmed_web_41506322", "pubmed_web_41497686", "pubmed_web_41491530", "pubmed_web_41489168", "pubmed_web_41482938", "pubmed_web_41480785", "pubmed_web_41478660", "pubmed_web_41469525", "pubmed_web_41465919", "pubmed_web_41462491", "pubmed_web_41458680", "pubmed_web_41450497", "pubmed_web_41435608"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "processing": 85.0, "total": 205.0, "llm": 1186.3739579998764, "verification": 2257.252749999907}}}
{"topic": "Myocardial infarction", "competency": "Pathophysiology", "stem": "Which of the following mechanisms is most likely to contribute to myocardial ischemia in a patient with coronary atherosclerosis?", "options": {"A": "Impaired autoregulation of coronary blood flow due to endothelial dysfunction and reduced vasodilatory capacity", "B": "Decreased adenosine production during periods of high myocardial oxygen demand", "C": "Enhanced ATP synthesis in hypoxic myocardial cells leading to vasoconstriction", "D": "Reduced platelet aggregation caused by nitric oxide overproduction"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Coronary atherosclerosis disrupts endothelial function in large coronary arteries, impairing vasodilation and predisposing to vasoconstriction, platelet aggregation, and thrombosis. This interferes with the normal autoregulation of coronary blood flow, which depends on adenosine-mediated vasodilation of intramyocardial arterioles in response to hypoxia. As a result, myocardial oxygen supply cannot meet demand, leading to ischemia.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0054_21237", "title": "Physiology, pathophysiology and pharmacology of the coronary circulation with particular emphasis on anesthetics.", "span_text": "Myocardial hypertrophy (from chronic hypertension) or coronary atherosclerosis interfere with this process and result in myocardial ischemia which may cause symptoms (angina), signs (ECG changes, regional muscle dysfunction) or tissue death (myocardial infarction). In addition, coronary atheroma disrupt endothelial function in the large R1 coronary arteries predisposing to vasoconstriction, platelet aggregation and thrombosis. Therapeutic measures for controlling ischemia may include decreasing oxygen demand (especially heart rate) and maintaining supply (R1 vasodilators and anti-thrombotic drugs such as non-steroidal anti-inflammatories)."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "pathophysiology of myocardial ischemia in coronary atherosclerosis", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0007_6635", "pubmed23n0054_21237", "InternalMed_Harrison_20982", "pubmed23n0014_3949", "pubmed23n0056_7477", "InternalMed_Harrison_9290", "pubmed23n0052_15585", "pubmed23n0060_9216", "pubmed23n0047_19810", "pubmed23n0045_4650", "pubmed23n0050_7742", "pubmed23n0054_2992", "pubmed23n0054_8461", "pubmed23n0054_8460", "pubmed23n0050_13381", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1259", "Pathology_Robbins_2649", "pubmed23n0024_1744", "pubmed23n0057_12980", "pubmed23n0011_5074", "pubmed23n0050_3561", "pubmed23n0028_13574", "pubmed23n0048_13624", "pubmed23n0053_16582", "InternalMed_Harrison_19265", "pubmed23n0051_15100", "pubmed23n0046_17672", "pubmed23n0040_2955", "pubmed23n0061_7743", "pubmed23n0058_18274", "pubmed23n0048_435", "InternalMed_Harrison_20981", "InternalMed_Harrison_19227", "pubmed23n0016_6744", "InternalMed_Harrison_19250", "pubmed23n0060_7788", "pubmed23n0058_21587", "pubmed23n0036_10771", "pubmed23n0037_2479", "pubmed23n0052_15315", "pubmed23n0053_9139", "pubmed23n0046_21942", "pubmed23n0037_11402", "pubmed23n0036_54", "pubmed23n0054_8478", "pubmed23n0052_17465", "pubmed23n0053_18573", "Neurology_Adams_6215", "pubmed23n0047_7125", "pubmed23n0059_6295", "InternalMed_Harrison_1743", "pubmed23n0052_2663", "pubmed23n0061_10988", "pubmed23n0051_4242", "pubmed23n0041_8565", "pubmed23n0058_20908", "pubmed23n0012_9147", "Surgery_Schwartz_1046", "pubmed23n0004_5494", "pubmed23n0017_8453", "pubmed23n0015_4639", "pubmed23n0024_2740", "pubmed23n0007_3256", "InternalMed_Harrison_1262", "pubmed23n0046_6199", "pubmed23n0061_13537", "pubmed23n0061_10203", "pubmed23n0043_8909", "pubmed23n0020_6926", "pubmed23n0044_23109", "pubmed23n0056_255", "pubmed23n0050_983", "pubmed23n0033_7111", "pubmed23n0017_775", "pubmed23n0058_1046", "pubmed23n0019_12838"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0007_6635", "pubmed23n0054_21237", "InternalMed_Harrison_20982", "pubmed23n0014_3949", "pubmed23n0056_7477", "InternalMed_Harrison_9290", "pubmed23n0