{"topic": "Deep vein thrombosis", "competency": "Pathophysiology", "stem": "Which of the following best describes a key mechanism by which platelets contribute to the development of deep vein thrombosis?", "options": {"A": "Platelets release microparticles that bind neutrophils and promote the formation of neutrophil extracellular traps, which are prothrombotic.", "B": "Platelets primarily cause venous thrombosis through mechanical obstruction of venous valves.", "C": "Platelets inhibit fibrin formation by suppressing factor Xa activity in venous circulation.", "D": "Platelets prevent endothelial injury by enhancing nitric oxide release in deep veins."}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Activated platelets release microparticles containing proinflammatory mediators that bind neutrophils, stimulating them to form neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs). These NETs contain histones that promote platelet aggregation and thrombin generation, contributing to venous thrombosis. This process is part of the inflammatory component of Virchow\u2019s triad, which underpins the pathophysiology of deep vein thrombosis.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "InternalMed_Harrison_19653", "title": "InternalMed_Harrison Text", "span_text": "Virchow\u2019s triad of inflammation, hypercoagulability, and endothelial injury leads to recruitment of activated platelets, which release microparticles. These microparticles contain proinflammatory mediators that bind neutrophils, stimulating them to release their nuclear material and form web-like extracellular networks called neutrophil extracellular traps. These prothrombotic networks contain histones that stimulate platelet aggregation and promote platelet-dependent thrombin generation."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "platelets pathophysiology deep vein thrombosis", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0061_13507", "InternalMed_Harrison_19653", "pubmed23n0026_7298", "pubmed23n0063_1900", "InternalMed_Harrison_9290", "InternalMed_Harrison_19849", "pubmed23n0048_6008", "pubmed23n0046_3715", "pubmed23n0060_1744", "pubmed23n0038_8200", "Pathology_Robbins_717", "pubmed23n0029_6845", "Pathology_Robbins_2401", "pubmed23n0018_1347", "pubmed23n0050_7742", "pubmed23n0050_6562", "InternalMed_Harrison_19658", "InternalMed_Harrison_9296", "pubmed23n0048_19489", "pubmed23n0037_2479", "pubmed23n0017_3970", "pubmed23n0055_9827", "pubmed23n0056_22812", "pubmed23n0051_1768", "pubmed23n0036_3438", "pubmed23n0058_13213", "pubmed23n0058_18274", "pubmed23n0058_11857", "InternalMed_Harrison_19048", "Surgery_Schwartz_6756", "pubmed23n0016_12071", "pubmed23n0059_18076", "pubmed23n0015_1536", "pubmed23n0061_1163", "Obstentrics_Williams_7242", "pubmed23n0042_5550", "Surgery_Schwartz_6762", "InternalMed_Harrison_25140", "pubmed23n0044_2412", "pubmed23n0033_10472", "pubmed23n0046_16951", "pubmed23n0027_15365", "pubmed23n0050_21776", "pubmed23n0032_9937", "pubmed23n0051_19759", "pubmed23n0026_7053", "pubmed23n0054_1348", "pubmed23n0020_4299", "pubmed23n0015_5132", "pubmed23n0046_11817", "pubmed23n0043_21437", "pubmed23n0021_10771", "pubmed23n0043_10403", "pubmed23n0044_2413", "pubmed23n0054_13010", "pubmed23n0029_11456", "Pathology_Robbins_2359", "pubmed23n0042_1771", "InternalMed_Harrison_19836", "Obstentrics_Williams_7121", "pubmed23n0063_6055", "pubmed23n0046_5629", "pubmed23n0041_10764", "pubmed23n0062_5157", "pubmed23n0054_5613", "pubmed23n0003_4295", "pubmed23n0057_3308", "pubmed23n0057_21160", "pubmed23n0025_6795", "pubmed23n0053_13619", "pubmed23n0042_7913", "pubmed23n0063_192", "pubmed23n0049_8776", "pubmed23n0046_18890", "pubmed23n0059_12617", "pubmed23n0033_8625", "pubmed23n0058_1663", "pubmed23n0057_9480", "pubmed23n0038_1673", "pubmed23n0026_8181", "pubmed23n0028_12060"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0061_13507", "InternalMed_Harrison_19653", "pubmed23n0026_7298", "pubmed23n0063_1900", "InternalMed_Harrison_9290", "InternalMed_Harrison_19849", "pubmed23n0048_6008", "pubmed23n0046_3715", "pubmed23n00