{"topic": "Peptic ulcer disease", "competency": "Complications", "stem": "A 68-year-old man with a history of peptic ulcer disease undergoes renal transplantation. He is placed on maintenance corticosteroid therapy for immunosuppression. Which of the following is the most likely gastrointestinal complication to occur in this patient during long-term follow-up?", "options": {"A": "Gastroduodenal perforation", "B": "Acute pancreatitis", "C": "Diverticulitis", "D": "Esophageal variceal hemorrhage"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Patients who undergo renal transplantation and receive long-term corticosteroid therapy are at increased risk for gastroduodenal perforation as a complication of peptic ulcer disease. Corticosteroids contribute to gastrointestinal complications, particularly in long-term survivors with well-functioning allografts. Preexisting peptic ulcer disease further increases the risk of serious complications such as perforation.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0013_6783", "title": "Alimentary tract complications after renal transplantation.", "span_text": "Pancreatitis, diverticulitis, and gastroduodenal perforation occurred characteristically in long-term survivors with well functioning allografts. Eleven of 32 HLA identical recipients treated with maintenance corticosteroids during stable kidney function developed gastrointestinal disease while only one of 13 HLA identical recipients not given maintenance steroids developed a problem, which strongly suggests a causal role for steroids in the development of late complications. The association of preexisting peptic ulcer and diverticular disease with hemorrhage and perforation supports previous recommendations that documented peptic ulcer disease or diverticulitis should be corrected surgically prior to transplantation."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "complications of peptic ulcer disease in renal transplant patients on corticosteroids", "doc_ids": ["pubme