{"topic": "Malnutrition", "competency": "Pathophysiology", "stem": "Which of the following metabolic abnormalities is commonly observed in malnourished cancer patients and contributes to the difficulty in achieving effective renutrition?", "options": {"A": "Increased protein breakdown", "B": "Decreased glucose production", "C": "Reduced lipolysis", "D": "Enhanced insulin sensitivity"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Malnourished cancer patients commonly exhibit increased protein breakdown, along with increased glucose production and lipolysis, which contribute to the metabolic derangements that interfere with renutrition efforts. These abnormalities are driven by tumor-induced metabolic changes and systemic inflammation.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0048_20231", "title": "Pathophysiology of cancer: hormonal and metabolic abnormalities.", "span_text": "A number of metabolic abnormalities have been demonstrated in malnourished cancer patients, including increased protein breakdown, increased glucose production, increased lipolysis, hypogonadism in male patients, and insulin resistance."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "metabolic abnormalities in malnourished cancer patients", "doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_21618", "pubmed23n0014_6593", "pubmed23n0004_6191", "pubmed23n0048_20231", "pubmed23n0019_12196", "InternalMed_Harrison_5627", "InternalMed_Harrison_5740", "pubmed23n0014_13423", "pubmed23n0014_876"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_21618", "pubmed23n0014_6593", "pubmed23n0004_6191", "pubmed23n0048_20231", "pubmed23n0019_12196", "InternalMed_Harrison_5627", "InternalMed_Harrison_5740", "pubmed23n0014_13423", "pubmed23n0014_876"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Malnutrition Pathophysiology pathophysiology pathogenesis mechanism molecular pathway cellular response inflammation hemodynamics neurohormonal physiology biomarkers dysfunction disease process", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 6394.36995799997, "llm_ms": 1018.0957499999295, "verification_ms": 1226.1753749999116, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["InternalMed_Harrison_21618", "pubmed23n0014_6593", "pubmed23n0004_6191", "pubmed23n0048_20231", "pubmed23n0019_12196", "InternalMed_Harrison_5627", "InternalMed_Harrison_5740", "pubmed23n0014_13423", "pubmed23n0014_876"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "processing": 45.0, "total": 165.0, "llm": 1018.0957499999295, "verification": 1226.1753749999116}}}
{"topic": "Influenza", "competency": "Etiology", "stem": "Which of the following best describes the etiologic agent responsible for seasonal influenza outbreaks?", "options": {"A": "A single-stranded RNA virus with frequent antigenic changes", "B": "A double-stranded DNA virus with high genetic stability", "C": "A gram-positive bacterium transmitted via respiratory droplets", "D": "A retrovirus that integrates into the host genome"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Influenza is caused by single-stranded RNA viruses that belong to the Orthomyxoviridae family. These viruses are known for their frequent antigenic changes, including antigenic drift and shift, which contribute to yearly epidemics and the need for annual vaccine updates. This characteristic distinguishes influenza from other pathogens and underlies its ability to infect individuals of all ages and cause widespread outbreaks.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0062_5865", "title": "Pathogenesis of respiratory infections due to influenza virus: implications for developing countries.", "span_text": "The influenza viruses have an important and distinctive place among respiratory viruses: they change antigenic character at irregular intervals, infect individuals of all ages, cause illnesses characterized by constitutional symptoms and tracheobronchitis, produce yearly epidemics associated frequently with excess morbidity and mortality, and predispose the host to bacterial superinfections."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "influenza etiology antigenic change", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0063_21345", "pubmed23n0062_5865"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0063_21345", "pubmed23n0062_5865"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Influenza Etiology etiology cause risk factors pathogenesis mechanism underlying cause genetic factors environmental factors predisposing factors associated conditions epidemiology incidence", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 6748.421292000103, "llm_ms": 1900.250958000015, "verification_ms": 936.3897080002062, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0063_21345", "pubmed23n0062_5865"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "processing": 45.0, "total": 165.0, "llm": 1900.250958000015, "verification": 936.3897080002062}}}
{"topic": "Tuberculosis", "competency": "Prognosis", "stem": "A 48-year-old male with a history of silicosis is diagnosed with pulmonary tuberculosis. He has moderate bilateral lung involvement on imaging. What is the most significant factor contributing to his increased risk of mortality?", "options": {"A": "Co-occurrence of tuberculosis with silicosis", "B": "Occupational exposure to silica dust", "C": "Age at diagnosis of silicosis", "D": "Smoking history"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The presence of tuberculosis in patients with silicosis significantly increases the risk of death, independent of other factors such as smoking or age. This is due to the synergistic effect of both diseases on respiratory function and overall prognosis.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0054_1177", "title": "Predictors of mortality in silicosis.", "span_text": "For the same extent of silicotic disease, the risk of death was higher if tuberculosis occurred. There was no evidence, however, that patients who smoked were more likely to suffer a worse mortality outcome than those who did not. Age at diagnosis, extent of silicotic disease and the occurrence of tuberculosis were therefore strong predictors of mortality in patients with silicosis."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "tuberculosis silicosis mortality risk factors", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0054_1177", "Obstentrics_Williams_7112", "pubmed23n0037_10470", "Obstentrics_Williams_7116", "pubmed23n0059_20945"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Obstentrics_Williams_7112", "pubmed23n0037_10470", "Obstentrics_Williams_7116", "pubmed23n0059_20945", "pubmed23n0054_1177"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Tuberculosis Prognosis prognosis outcomes mortality survival complications disease course recurrence risk stratification severity long-term outcomes quality of life predictors", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 8939.394457999924, "llm_ms": 1200.244917000191, "verification_ms": 891.960958000027, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 0, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 1}, "failed_checks": ["reviewer_mismatch"], "failed_checks_all": ["reviewer_mismatch"], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Obstentrics_Williams_7112", "pubmed23n0037_10470", "Obstentrics_Williams_7116", "pubmed23n0059_20945", "pubmed23n0054_1177"], "evidence_reused": false}, {"attempt": 2, "reason": "v5_reviewer", "query": "Tuberculosis Prognosis prognosis outcomes mortality survival complications disease course recurrence risk stratification severity long-term outcomes quality of life predictors", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 8379.468374999988, "llm_ms": 1017.0448750000105, "verification_ms": 740.6463339998481, "extra_instructions": "Ensure the correct answer is directly supported by the evidence and quote the exact supporting span.", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Obstentrics_Williams_7112", "pubmed23n0037_10470", "Obstentrics_Williams_7116", "pubmed23n0059_20945", "pubmed23n0054_1177"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "verification": 45.0, "total": 165.0, "llm": 1017.0448750000105}}}
{"topic": "Osteoarthritis", "competency": "Monitoring", "stem": "A 65-year-old patient with knee pain and stiffness has been diagnosed with osteoarthritis. Which of the following approaches is most appropriate for monitoring disease progression over time?", "options": {"A": "Regular radiographic imaging to assess joint space narrowing and structural changes.", "B": "Routine MRI screening for early cartilage inflammation.", "C": "Serial blood tests to monitor inflammatory markers such as CRP and ESR.", "D": "Genetic testing to identify inherited risk factors for joint degeneration."}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Radiographic monitoring is a well-established method for assessing the structural progression of osteoarthritis, including joint space narrowing and osteophyte formation. It provides objective data that can be used over time to evaluate disease course and inform management decisions.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0063_20618", "title": "Clinical features of osteoarthritis, diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis, and hypermobility syndromes.", "span_text": "The work-up of classic data and the use of new information from epidemiologic surveys as well as careful radiographic monitoring of selected groups of patients have brought us broader views on the general course of the condition."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "osteoarthritis monitoring radiographic", "results": ["pubmed23n0063_20618"], "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0063_20618"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0063_20618"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match_in_evidence", "confirmed": "true", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Osteoarthritis Monitoring monitoring follow-up treatment response labs imaging vital signs disease control therapeutic monitoring adverse effects dose adjustment clinical assessment outcome tracking", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 10348.594917000128, "llm_ms": 988.902957999926, "verification_ms": 945.0085829998898, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0063_20618"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "total": 165.0, "llm": 988.902957999926, "verification": 945.0085829998898}}}
{"topic": "Postpartum hemorrhage", "competency": "Complications", "stem": "A 32-year-old woman with pregestational diabetes mellitus delivers a term infant vaginally. Shortly after delivery, she develops excessive vaginal bleeding. Which of the following is a recognized complication of pregestational diabetes that increases her risk for this condition?", "options": {"A": "Postpartum hemorrhage", "B": "Uterine rupture", "C": "Placental abruption", "D": "Amniotic fluid embolism"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Pregestational diabetes mellitus is associated with several maternal complications, including an increased risk of postpartum hemorrhage. This is likely due to impaired uterine contractility and other vascular complications related to long-standing diabetes. Management requires prompt recognition and intervention to prevent severe morbidity.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "First_Aid_Step2_747", "title": "First_Aid_Step2 Text", "span_text": "\u25a0The etiology is unknown, but clinical manifestations are explained by vasospasm leading to hemorrhage and organ necrosis. T AB LE 2.1 1 -1 3. Complications of Pregestational Diabetes Mellitus macrosomia) and need for C-section Preterm labor Infection Polyhydramnios Postpartum hemorrhage Maternal mortality Macrosomia or IUGR Cardiac and renal defects Neural tube defects (e.g., sacral agenesis) Hypocalcemia Polycythemia Hyperbilirubinemia IUGR Hypoglycemia from hyperinsulinemia Respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) Birth injury (e.g., shoulder dystocia) Perinatal mortality"}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "complications of pregestational diabetes mellitus postpartum hemorrhage", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0057_24447", "pubmed23n0048_19624", "pubmed23n0057_21910", "First_Aid_Step2_747", "pubmed23n0048_6744", "pubmed23n0013_6783", "Obstentrics_Williams_5304", "pubmed23n0052_6822", "pubmed23n0025_2644", "pubmed23n0051_9597", "pubmed23n0058_4553", "pubmed23n0036_441", "Obstentrics_Williams_7330", "pubmed23n0030_11307", "pubmed23n0057_19210", "Obstentrics_Williams_5592", "pubmed23n0049_17582", "pubmed23n0047_6166", "Obstentrics_Williams_6951", "pubmed23n0046_14872", "Obstentrics_Williams_8517", "pubmed23n0045_6812", "pubmed23n0063_874", "Obstentrics_Williams_8644", "Obstentrics_Williams_2151", "pubmed23n0047_10217", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3452", "pubmed23n0012_4435", "Obstentrics_Williams_5107", "pubmed23n0001_2108", "Obstentrics_Williams_4341", "Obstentrics_Williams_5516", "pubmed23n0058_9976", "Obstentrics_Williams_8521", "pubmed23n0049_4462", "pubmed23n0031_4593", "pubmed23n0062_585", "pubmed23n0056_7057", "Obstentrics_Williams_5545", "Obstentrics_Williams_7797", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1433", "Obstentrics_Williams_6947"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0057_24447", "pubmed23n0048_19624", "pubmed23n0057_21910", "First_Aid_Step2_747", "pubmed23n0048_6744", "pubmed23n0013_6783", "Obstentrics_Williams_5304", "pubmed23n0052_6822", "pubmed23n0025_2644", "pubmed23n0051_9597", "pubmed23n0058_4553", "pubmed23n0036_441", "Obstentrics_Williams_7330", "pubmed23n0030_11307", "pubmed23n0057_19210", "Obstentrics_Williams_5592", "pubmed23n0049_17582", "pubmed23n0047_6166", "Obstentrics_Williams_6951", "pubmed23n0046_14872"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"source_confirmed": "true", "accuracy": "high", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Postpartum hemorrhage Complications complications adverse outcomes sequelae comorbidity disease progression risk factors mortality morbidity hospitalization long-term effects severity clinical deterioration", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 9859.558375000006, "llm_ms": 2350.632583000106, "verification_ms": 935.7260839999526, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFIC