{"topic": "Pancreatitis", "competency": "Prevention", "stem": "Which of the following statements best reflects the current challenges in preventing pancreatic disorders, particularly pancreatic carcinoma, in patients with pancreatitis?", "options": {"A": "Primary prevention of pancreatic carcinoma is not currently feasible, and secondary prevention is hindered by the invasive nature or limited availability of diagnostic procedures.", "B": "Vaccination strategies have proven effective in the primary prevention of pancreatic carcinoma in high-risk individuals.", "C": "Routine screening with CT scans for all patients with acute pancreatitis significantly reduces mortality from pancreatic cancer.", "D": "Endoscopic ultrasound is widely available and recommended for all asymptomatic individuals to prevent pancreatic carcinoma."}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Primary prevention of pancreatic carcinoma is not yet possible, and secondary prevention remains challenging due to the invasiveness and limited accessibility of current diagnostic methods. This is particularly relevant in the context of pancreatitis, which can be a risk factor for pancreatic cancer.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0012_6841", "title": "Immunologic detection of primary carcinoma of the pancreas.", "span_text": "Primary prevention of this disorder is not yet possible and secondary prevention is difficult because most investigative procedures are either highly invasive or not universally available."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "prevention of pancreatic carcinoma in pancreatitis patients", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0012_6841"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0012_6841"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Pancreatitis Prevention prevention prophylaxis risk reduction screening vaccination primary prevention secondary prevention lifestyle modification counseling public health early detection follow-up", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 6065.9421660000135, "llm_ms": 1619.9097500000335, "verification_ms": 716.4072919999853, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0012_6841"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "verification": 30.0, "llm": 1619.9097500000335}}}
{"topic": "Deep vein thrombosis", "competency": "Treatment", "stem": "A 65-year-old man with a history of deep vein thrombosis and multiple comorbidities is being considered for long-term anticoagulation. He is at high risk for recurrent thromboembolism but also has concerns about bleeding complications. Which of the following treatments has been shown to be as effective as warfarin with a lower risk of bleeding in such patients?", "options": {"A": "Low-dose heparin therapy", "B": "Dextran-70", "C": "Oral anticoagulant therapy for four weeks", "D": "Peripheral retinal cryotherapy"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Low-dose heparin therapy has been shown to be as effective as warfarin in preventing recurrent thromboembolism in high-risk patients, with significantly fewer bleeding complications. It is a suitable alternative for long-term management in patients with complicated medical illnesses.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0013_7566", "title": "Low-dose heparin therapy in the long-term management of venous thromboembolism.", "span_text": "The efficacy of a six-month course of low-dose heparin therapy was compared to a conventional warfarin regimen by a prospective, controlled trial in 48 patients with pulmonary embolism or deep venous thrombosis of the legs. All subjects had complicated medical illnesses and a high risk of recurrent thromboembolism. Bleeding complications were virtually negligible during heparin therapy and occurred significantly more frequently in patients receiving warfarin. Heparin was as effective as warfarin in the prevention of recurrent thromboembolism."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "long-term treatment options for deep vein thrombosis with bleeding risk", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0013_7566", "pubmed23n0032_7557", "pubmed23n0010_3291", "pubmed23n0051_16236", "Obstentrics_Williams_7236", "Pathology_Robbins_1908", "pubmed23n0057_21160", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6275", "pubmed23n0034_2582", "Surgery_S