{"topic": "Smoking cessation", "competency": "Prognosis", "stem": "A 68-year-old male with a 40-pack-year smoking history is diagnosed with coronary heart disease following hospitalization for acute chest pain. He expresses interest in quitting smoking. What is the most accurate statement regarding his prognosis after smoking cessation?", "options": {"A": "He will have a reduced risk of cardiac death within 1 year of quitting, with continued decline in risk over subsequent years.", "B": "His risk of cardiac death will not improve significantly unless he also undergoes coronary revascularization.", "C": "Smoking cessation at his age will have minimal impact on his long-term cardiovascular outcomes.", "D": "Quitting smoking will reduce his cancer risk but not affect his cardiac prognosis."}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Smoking cessation in older adults significantly reduces the risk of coronary events and cardiac death within 1 year of quitting, and this benefit persists and gradually improves over time, regardless of prior coronary disease history. This improvement in cardiac prognosis occurs independently of revascularization procedures and is well-documented in patients with established coronary heart disease.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0052_16686", "title": "Older adults and smoking.", "span_text": "Smoking cessation in older adults markedly reduces the risks of coronary events and of cardiac deaths within 1 year of quitting, and risk continues to decline more gradually for many years. This is true for older adults both with and without a previous history of coronary disease and symptoms."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "smoking cessation prognosis after coronary heart disease in older adults", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0052_16686", "pubmed23n0043_3917", "pubmed23n0036_3361", "pubmed23n0063_4814", "pubmed23n0029_8807", "InternalMed_Harrison_6678", "pubmed23n0028_9430", "Biochemistry_Lippincott_1832", "pubmed23n00