{"topic": "Myocardial infarction", "competency": "Complications", "stem": "A 70-year-old man is hospitalized for acute myocardial infarction. He lives alone, has limited contact with family, and reports feeling isolated. Which of the following factors is most strongly associated with increased 6-month mortality in this patient population, independent of infarction severity and comorbidities?", "options": {"A": "Lack of emotional support", "B": "Elevated serum cholesterol", "C": "History of prior stroke", "D": "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Lack of emotional support is independently associated with increased 6-month mortality after myocardial infarction in elderly patients, even after controlling for disease severity, comorbidity, and sociodemographic factors. This highlights the importance of psychosocial factors in post-infarction outcomes.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0048_7202", "title": "Emotional support and survival after myocardial infarction. A prospective, population-based study of the elderly.", "span_text": "Of 194 patients, 76 (39%) died in the first 6 months after myocardial infarction. In multiple logistic regression analyses, lack of emotional support was significantly associated with 6-month mortality (odds ratio, 2.9; 95% CI, 1.2 to 6.9) after controlling for severity of myocardial infarction, comorbidity, risk factors such as smoking and hypertension, and sociodemographic factors. When emotional support was assessed before myocardial infarction, it was independently related to risk for death in the subsequent 6 months."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "emotional support mortality after myocardial infarction elderly", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0053_17698", "pubmed23n0052_8305", "pubmed23n0048_7202", "InternalMed_Harrison_18242", "pubmed23n0050_13944", "pubmed23n0048_156", "pubmed23n0028_9430", "pubmed23n0057_20992", "pubmed23n0053_15536", "pubmed23n0030_8211", "p