{"topic": "Depression", "competency": "Monitoring", "stem": "A 55-year-old patient with endogenous depression is started on nortriptyline. The clinician is considering using plasma level monitoring to guide dosage adjustments. Which of the following statements best reflects the current evidence regarding nortriptyline plasma concentration monitoring?", "options": {"A": "Monitoring nortriptyline plasma levels can help optimize dosage to maximize antidepressant effect and minimize toxicity.", "B": "Plasma level monitoring is unnecessary because all patients respond uniformly to standard dosing.", "C": "Higher plasma levels of nortriptyline are always associated with better therapeutic outcomes.", "D": "Routine monitoring of nortriptyline plasma levels has been shown to be of no value in any clinical setting."}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Evidence supports that nortriptyline plasma concentration monitoring, particularly using a 48-hour post-dose level, is a reliable method to guide dosage adjustments, improve therapeutic outcomes, and reduce toxicity. Targeting a plasma level around 100 ng/ml appears beneficial when integrated into a structured treatment strategy.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0013_4106", "title": "Dosage adjustment from simple nortriptyline spot level predictor tests in depressed patients.", "span_text": "The single 48 hour plasma nortriptyline concentration following a single oral dose is recommended as a reliable simplified monitoring test suitable for a busy clinic. The test is useful for dosage adjustment to maximise antidepressant action and minimise toxicity. A tentative dosage adjustment schedule for individualising antidepressant treatment with nortriptyline based on the 48 hour or the 24 hour plasma concentration is proposed."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "nortriptyline plasma level monitoring depression", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0003_5466", "pubmed23n0014_750", "pubmed23n0058_8996", "pubmed23n0013_4106", "pubmed23n0051_10931", "pubmed23n0004_4410", "pubmed23n0014_11678", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3439", "pubmed23n0017_3637", "pubmed23n0031_9925", "pubmed23n0018_688", "pubmed23n0017_2434", "pubmed23n0009_856", "pubmed23n0057_12160", "pubmed23n0004_5012", "pubmed23n0043_5253", "pubmed23n0057_16532", "pubmed23n0002_2747"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0003_5466", "pubmed23n0014_750", "pubmed23n0058_8996", "pubmed23n0013_4106", "pubmed23n0051_10931", "pubmed23n0004_4410", "pubmed23n0014_11678", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3439", "pubmed23n0017_3637", "pubmed23n0031_9925", "pubmed23n0018_688", "pubmed23n0017_2434", "pubmed23n0009_856", "pubmed23n0057_12160", "pubmed23n0004_5012", "pubmed23n0043_5253", "pubmed23n0057_16532", "pubmed23n0002_2747"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "evidence_match", "result": "confirmed", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Depression Monitoring monitoring follow-up treatment response labs imaging vital signs disease control therapeutic monitoring adverse effects dose adjustment clinical assessment outcome tracking", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 7811.29270800011, "llm_ms": 2091.498958000102, "verification_ms": 1098.5809160001736, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0003_5466", "pubmed23n0014_750", "pubmed23n0058_8996", "pubmed23n0013_4106", "pubmed23n0051_10931", "pubmed23n0004_4410", "pubmed23n0014_11678", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3439", "pubmed23n0017_3637", "pubmed23n0031_9925", "pubmed23n0018_688", "pubmed23n0017_2434", "pubmed23n0009_856", "pubmed23n0057_12160", "pubmed23n0004_5012", "pubmed23n0043_5253", "pubmed23n0057_16532", "pubmed23n0002_2747"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "processing": 45.0, "total": 165.0, "llm": 2091.498958000102, "verification": 1098.5809160001736}}}
{"topic": "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease", "competency": "Treatment", "stem": "A 65-year-old male with a 50-pack-year smoking history presents with progressive dyspnea on exertion and chronic cough. Pulmonary function tests confirm a diagnosis of moderate chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). Which of the following is the most appropriate initial pharmacologic therapy for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Inhaled anticholinergic therapy", "B": "Inhaled corticosteroids", "C": "Oral theophylline", "D": "Inhaled short-acting beta-agonists alone"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Inhaled anticholinergic therapy is recommended as first-line treatment for patients with COPD. It has been shown to be safe and effective in improving airflow and symptoms in these patients. Anticholinergics are preferred over beta-agonists in some studies for chronic management, and they are specifically indicated in COPD related to smoking. Other therapies may be added based on severity and response, but anticholinergics are foundational in initial treatment.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0048_9447", "title": "COPD: primary care management with drug and oxygen therapies.", "span_text": "Smoking cessation, inhaled anticholinergic therapy, and oxygen supplementation (when indicated) are primary treatment modalities."}, {"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0061_12104", "title": "Anticholinergic bronchodilators in obstructive airway disease.", "span_text": "It is suggested that anticholinergic bronchodilators are safe and effective as first-line therapy for COPD and should be considered as adjunctive therapy for asthma."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "first-line pharmacologic treatment for COPD", "doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_2200", "First_Aid_Step2_1063", "pubmed23n0048_9447", "pubmed23n0059_20077", "pubmed23n0014_2698", "Gynecology_Novak_3795", "pubmed23n0061_12104", "pubmed23n0060_3302", "Gynecology_Novak_3798", "pubmed2