{"topic": "Hyperlipidemia", "competency": "Etiology", "stem": "A 7-year-old child with nephrotic syndrome develops severe hypercholesterolemia and hypertriglyceridemia. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for the development of severe hyperlipidemia in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Multiple interacting factors including metabolic abnormalities of nephrotic syndrome, steroid therapy, genetic predisposition, diet, and diuretic use", "B": "Isolated genetic mutation in the LDL receptor gene", "C": "Excessive dietary intake of saturated fats alone", "D": "Primary hypothyroidism associated with reduced lipid clearance"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Severe hyperlipidemia in children with nephrotic syndrome is often multifactorial. In this case, the combination of metabolic disturbances from nephrotic syndrome, steroid treatment, genetic predisposition (ApoE-2 homozygosity), diet, and diuretic therapy contributed to the development of extreme hyperlipidemia.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0058_16312", "title": "Severe hyperlipidemia due to multiple factors in a child with nephrotic syndrome.", "span_text": "An unusual case of severe hypercholesterolemia and hypertriglyceridemia is described in a child with nephrotic syndrome. The severe hyperlipidemia in this patient was most likely induced by multiple interacting factors which included the metabolic abnormalities of nephrotic syndrome, steroid therapy, the underlying genetic predisposition of ApoE-2 homozygosity as well as diet and diuretic therapy. The result of these factors was an extremely severe type III hyperlipoproteinemia."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "etiology of hyperlipidemia in nephrotic syndrome", "doc_ids": ["Gynecology_Novak_6504", "pubmed23n0028_4925", "pubmed23n0058_16312", "pubmed23n0054_6912", "pubmed23n0020_2185", "pubmed23n0050_603", "Surgery_Schwartz_9601"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Gynecology_Novak_6504", "pubmed23n0028_4925", "pubmed23n0058_16312", "pubmed23n0054_6912", "pubmed23n0020_2185", "pubmed23n0050_603", "Surgery_Schwartz_9601"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "exact_match", "score": "0.98", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Hyperlipidemia Etiology etiology cause risk factors pathogenesis mechanism underlying cause genetic factors environmental factors predisposing factors associated conditions epidemiology incidence", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 7645.985041999666, "llm_ms": 1020.5801669999346, "verification_ms": 859.1790000000401, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Gynecology_Novak_6504", "pubmed23n0028_4925", "pubmed23n0058_16312", "pubmed23n0054_6912", "pubmed23n0020_2185", "pubmed23n0050_603", "Surgery_Schwartz_9601"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "verification": 67.0, "llm": 1020.5801669999346}}}
{"topic": "Dengue", "competency": "Prognosis", "stem": "A 35-year-old patient with confirmed dengue fever develops shock, widespread petechiae, and seizures. Which of the following is the most accurate predictor of poor prognosis in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Development of shock and neurologic signs", "B": "Presence of fever and myalgias", "C": "Platelet count of 90,000/\u03bcL", "D": "Mild malaise and local hemorrhage"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The presence of shock, multifocal hemorrhaging, and neurologic signs such as seizures or coma are strong predictors of poor prognosis in dengue infection. These features indicate progression to severe forms of the disease, such as dengue shock syndrome or dengue hemorrhagic fever, which are associated with significantly higher mortality rates.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "InternalMed_Harrison_9754", "title": "InternalMed_Harrison", "span_text": "Shock, multifocal hemorrhaging, and neurologic signs (e.g., seizures or coma) predict a poor prognosis."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "prognostic factors in dengue fever", "results": ["InternalMed_Harrison_9754"], "doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_9754"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["InternalMed_Harrison_9754"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "direct_quote_match", "confirmed": "true", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Dengue Prognosis prognosis outcomes mortality survival complications disease course recurrence risk stratification severity long-term outcomes quality of life predictors", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 13311.885166999673, "llm_ms": 1333.186416999979, "verification_ms": 982.9191669996362, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["InternalMed_Harrison_9754"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 120.0, "generation": 45.0, "verification": 30.0, "llm": 1333.186416999979}}}
{"topic": "Prostate cancer", "competency": "Diagnosis", "stem": "A 62-year-old man presents with lower urinary tract symptoms. On digital rectal examination, the prostate is found to be hard and nodular. Which of the following findings is most consistent with a diagnosis of prostate cancer?", "options": {"A": "Hard, nodular prostate on digital rectal examination", "B": "Elevated serum CA 19-9 level", "C": "Incidental finding on renal imaging", "D": "Hypercalcemia with no symptoms"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "A hard, nodular prostate on digital rectal examination is a classic physical finding in prostate cancer. While prostate-specific antigen (PSA) testing is commonly used, the DRE remains a key diagnostic component, especially when the prostate feels irregular or indurated. Other options are not specific to prostate cancer diagnosis.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "InternalMed_Harrison_7408", "title": "InternalMed_Harrison Text", "span_text": "The need to pursue a diagnosis of prostate cancer is based on symptoms, an abnormal DRE, or, more typically, a change in or an elevated serum PSA. The urologic history should focus on symptoms of outlet obstruction, continence, potency, or change in ejaculatory pattern. The DRE focuses on prostate size and consistency and abnormalities within or beyond the gland. Many cancers occur in the peripheral zone and may be palpated on DRE. Carcinomas are characteristically hard, nodular, and irregular, while induration may also be due to benign prostatic hypertrophy (BPH) or calculi."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"query": "prostate cancer diagnosis digital rectal exam", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0054_20199", "pubmed23n0054_1438", "pubmed23n0018_4903", "pubmed23n0044_6003", "pubmed23n0046_2311", "InternalMed_Harrison_7408", "InternalMed_Harrison_28583", "Surgery_Schwartz_11662", "Gynecology_Novak_3379", "pubmed23n0036_7308", "pubmed23n0036_8039", "pubmed23n0041_8955", "pubmed23n0058_8723", "pubmed23n