{"topic": "Anaphylaxis", "competency": "Prevention", "stem": "Which of the following infant feeding practices is recommended to help prevent IgE\u2011mediated food allergy and subsequent anaphylaxis?", "options": {"A": "Introduce solid foods at 4 months of age.", "B": "Introduce well\u2011cooked egg and appropriate forms of peanut after solid foods are started, and offer common allergens about once a week.", "C": "Use hydrolysed milk\u2011protein formula as the primary milk source.", "D": "Avoid all common food allergens until the child is 2 years old."}, "answer_key": "B", "explanation": "Guidelines advise that, once infants are developmentally ready for solids (around 6 months), well\u2011cooked egg and appropriate peanut forms should be introduced, and other common allergens offered about weekly to reduce the risk of developing food allergy and anaphylaxis.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed_web", "doc_id": "pubmed_web_41554514", "title": "ASCIA Guideline: Infant Feeding for Food Allergy Prevention", "span_text": "Soon after infants have started solid foods, well cooked egg and appropriate forms of peanut are included in the infant's diet. Other common food allergens included in the family diet should be offered to the infant. Once introduced, common food allergens should be offered around once a week."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41562057", "pubmed_web_41557089", "pubmed_web_41554514", "pubmed_web_41528262", "pubmed_web_41510923", "pubmed_web_41508631", "pubmed_web_41505160", "pubmed_web_41475155", "pubmed_web_41466461", "pubmed_web_41459439", "pubmed_web_41445848", "pubmed_web_41429040", "pubmed_web_41418604", "pubmed_web_41410197", "pubmed_web_41410133", "pubmed_web_41405481", "pubmed_web_41401453", "pubmed_web_41377715", "pubmed_web_41366617", "pubmed_web_41355466"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41562057", "pubmed_web_41557089", "pubmed_web_41554514", "pubmed_web_41528262", "pubmed_web_41510923", "pubmed_web_41508631",