{"topic": "Anaphylaxis", "competency": "Prevention", "stem": "Which of the following infant feeding practices is recommended to help prevent IgE\u2011mediated food allergy that can lead to anaphylaxis?", "options": {"A": "Introduce well\u2011cooked egg and appropriate forms of peanut soon after starting solid foods.", "B": "Delay introduction of all potential food allergens until the child is 3\u202fyears old.", "C": "Avoid all dairy and nut products during breastfeeding.", "D": "Use hydrolysed formula or soy\u2011based substitutes for allergy prevention."}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Guidelines advise that, once infants begin solid foods, well\u2011cooked egg and appropriate forms of peanut should be introduced early, and other common food allergens offered regularly. This early, regular exposure is shown to reduce the risk of developing IgE\u2011mediated food allergy, a major trigger for anaphylaxis.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed_web", "doc_id": "pubmed_web_41554514", "title": "ASCIA Guideline: Infant Feeding for Food Allergy Prevention", "span_text": "Soon after infants have started solid foods, well cooked egg and appropriate forms of peanut are included in the infant's diet. Other common food allergens included in the family diet should be offered to the infant."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41562057", "pubmed_web_41557089", "pubmed_web_41554514", "pubmed_web_41528262", "pubmed_web_41510923", "pubmed_web_41508631", "pubmed_web_41505160", "pubmed_web_41475155", "pubmed_web_41466461", "pubmed_web_41459439", "pubmed_web_41445848", "pubmed_web_41429040", "pubmed_web_41418604", "pubmed_web_41410197", "pubmed_web_41410133", "pubmed_web_41405481", "pubmed_web_41401453", "pubmed_web_41377715", "pubmed_web_41366617", "pubmed_web_41355466"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41554514", "pubmed_web_41528262", "pubmed_web_41510923", "pubmed_web_41508631", "pubmed_web_41505160", "pubmed_web_41475155", "pubmed_web_41466461", "p