{"topic": "Pneumonia", "competency": "Pathophysiology", "stem": "In patients with acute pneumonia, which of the following best describes the primary mechanism responsible for impaired mucociliary clearance?", "options": {"A": "Ciliary impairment of the airway epithelium that is independent of the clinical form of pneumonia and related to endobronchitis activity.", "B": "Direct bacterial invasion of alveolar macrophages leading to their dysfunction.", "C": "Autoimmune-mediated necrotic vasculitis causing reduced blood flow to the bronchi.", "D": "Viral cytopathic damage to type I pneumocytes resulting in loss of ciliary function."}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Studies of patients with acute pneumonia have shown that ciliary activity is markedly reduced during the height of disease and persists in many convalescents. This impairment occurs regardless of whether the pneumonia is lobular or croupous and is linked to the activity of endobronchitis, making it the principal mechanism for decreased mucociliary clearance.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0059_17398", "title": "Ciliary activity of the ciliated epithelium of the airways in patients with acute pneumonia", "span_text": "Ciliary impairment of the epithelium is independent of AP clinical form (lobular or croupous) or of dissemination of the inflammation though is related to endobronchitis activity. The ciliary dysfunction emerged in all the patients at the height of the disease and in 65.3% of convalescents."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"method": "keyword_search", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0005_7701", "pubmed23n0059_17398", "pubmed23n0059_18382", "pubmed23n0004_1032", "pubmed23n0058_11527"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0005_7701", "pubmed23n0059_17398", "pubmed23n0059_18382", "pubmed23n0004_1032", "pubmed23n0058_11527"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"status": "checked", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Pneumonia Pathophysiology pathophysiology pathogenesis mechanism molecular pathway cellular response inflammation hemodynamics neurohormonal physiology biomarkers dysfunction disease process", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 9411.288291000346, "llm_ms": 1173.3991660003085, "verification_ms": 818.1237910002892, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["pubmed23n0005_7701", "pubmed23n0059_17398", "pubmed23n0059_18382", "pubmed23n0004_1032", "pubmed23n0058_11527"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"total": 112.0, "retrieval": 9411.288291000346, "llm": 1173.3991660003085, "verification": 818.1237910002892}}}
{"topic": "Pneumonia", "competency": "Pathophysiology", "stem": "In a large cohort of community\u2011acquired pneumonia (CAP) patients, which clinical variable was identified as the strongest predictor of post\u2011acute mortality within the high\u2011risk subgroup?", "options": {"A": "Body mass index", "B": "Age", "C": "Serum urea level", "D": "Platelet count"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The study using self\u2011organising map clustering found that while age and urea were overall important predictors, body mass index emerged as the strongest predictor specifically in the high\u2011risk CAP cohort. Elevated platelet counts were also associated with risk, but BMI was identified as the dominant factor.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed_web", "doc_id": "pubmed_web_41561102", "title": "Self-organising map clustering identifies high-risk clusters of post-acute mortality in a prospective multicentre study of community-acquired pneumonia", "span_text": "Across the cohort, age and urea were the most important predictors of post-acute mortality, while in the high-risk cohort, body mass index emerged as the strongest predictor, as identified by RSF modelling. In one high-risk cluster, there was an association with elevated platelet counts (HR: 1.13, 95% CI 1.03-1.21, p=0.01; increments of 40 platelets\u00b7nL"}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"method": "keyword search", "query": "community-acquired pneumonia high risk mortality predictors", "doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41578736", "pubmed_web_41578455", "pubmed_web_41576966", "pubmed_web_41576003", "pubmed_web_41574613", "pubmed_web_41572364", "pubmed_web_41569247", "pubmed_web_41568000", "pubmed_web_41566511", "pubmed_web_41564200", "pubmed_web_41564101", "pubmed_web_41562844", "pubmed_web_41561102", "pubmed_web_41558017", "pubmed_web_41555789", "pubmed_web_41544029", "pubmed_web_41543809", "pubmed_web_41538476", "pubmed_web_41536211", "pubmed_web_41536141"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41564101", "pubmed_w