{"topic": "Stroke", "competency": "Pharmacology", "stem": "Which molecular mechanism is most likely responsible for the neuroprotective effect of piperlongumine in ischemic stroke?", "options": {"A": "Binding to actin beta (ACTB) and CFLAR proteins, thereby reducing apoptosis", "B": "Inhibiting platelet aggregation to prevent further thrombus formation", "C": "Blocking NMDA receptors to decrease excitotoxicity", "D": "Enhancing cerebral vasoconstriction to limit edema"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Preclinical studies identified piperlongumine as a potential ischemic\u2011stroke therapy because molecular docking showed it binds to the proteins encoded by ACTB and CFLAR, which are involved in apoptotic pathways. This interaction is thought to attenuate apoptosis after brain injury, supporting its neuroprotective role.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed_web", "doc_id": "pubmed_web_41575950", "title": "Single-cell RNA-seq reveals the piperlongumine is a potential drug for ischemic stroke", "span_text": "Connectivity Map (CMap) analysis and molecular docking suggested that piperlongumine might be an effective drug for the treatment of ischemic stroke by binding to the proteins encoded by Actb and Cflar."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41582546", "pubmed_web_41582192", "pubmed_web_41580787", "pubmed_web_41580529", "pubmed_web_41579204", "pubmed_web_41578552", "pubmed_web_41578320", "pubmed_web_41577922", "pubmed_web_41577920", "pubmed_web_41577216", "pubmed_web_41575950", "pubmed_web_41575631", "pubmed_web_41575609", "pubmed_web_41574686", "pubmed_web_41574553", "pubmed_web_41572775", "pubmed_web_41572337", "pubmed_web_41571953", "pubmed_web_41571952", "pubmed_web_41571757"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41575950", "pubmed_web_41575631", "pubmed_web_41575609", "pubmed_web_41574686", "pubmed_web_41574553", "pubmed_web_41572775", "pubmed_web_41572337", "pubmed_web_41571953", "pubmed_web_41571952", "pubmed_web_41571757", "pubmed_