{"topic": "Osteoarthritis", "competency": "Screening", "stem": "In population screening for early osteoarthritis, which statement best describes the performance of simple clinical signs compared with radiographic diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "They have high specificity but low sensitivity.", "B": "They have high sensitivity but low specificity.", "C": "They have both high sensitivity and high specificity.", "D": "They have both low sensitivity and low specificity."}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Simple clinical signs of osteoarthritis show a high specificity (87\u201199%) but relatively low sensitivity (20\u201149%) when compared with radiographic findings, making them useful for confirming disease but less reliable for ruling it out.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed", "doc_id": "pubmed23n0062_16813", "title": "Clinical signs of early osteoarthritis: reproducibility and relation to x ray changes in 541 women in the general population", "span_text": "The major clinical signs used had a high specificity (87-99%) and lower sensitivity (20-49%) when compared with radiographs graded on the Kellgren and Lawrence scale (2+ = positive)."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"source": "pubmed", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0062_16813"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0062_16813"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"checked": true, "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Osteoarthritis Screening screening early detection risk assessment screening test sensitivity specificity guideline population screening high-risk preventive care follow-up diagnostic threshold", "evidence_top_k": 20, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 7075.473042001249, "llm_ms": 2662.4989159972756, "verification_ms": 0.5662080002366565, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts"