{"topic": "Hyperlipidemia", "competency": "Therapy", "stem": "What is the primary goal of managing hyperlipidemia in patients with increased risk of premature atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease?", "options": {"A": "To reduce serum cholesterol levels through diet and pharmacologic therapy", "B": "To increase serum cholesterol levels through dietary changes", "C": "To manage symptoms of hyperlipidemia without addressing the underlying cause", "D": "To reduce the risk of non-insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The primary goal of managing hyperlipidemia is to reduce the risk of premature atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease by lowering serum cholesterol levels through a combination of diet, weight reduction, and pharmacologic therapy, such as statins.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "InternalMed_Harrison_28216", "title": "InternalMed_Harrison", "span_text": "Because FDBL is associated with increased risk of premature ASCVD, it should be treated aggressively. Other metabolic conditions that can worsen the hyperlipidemia (see above) should be managed. Patients with FDBL are typically diet-responsive and can respond favorably to weight reduction and to low-cholesterol, low-fat diets."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"method": "keyword search", "doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0058_16219", "InternalMed_Harrison_28216", "pubmed23n0062_12872", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1218", "pubmed23n0012_1749"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["pubmed23n0058_16219", "InternalMed_Harrison_28216", "pubmed23n0062_12872", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1218", "pubmed23n0012_1749"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "expert review", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Hyperlipidemia Therapy therapy treatment management drug dosage medication pharmacotherapy first-line second-line guideline intervention acute management long-term management", "e