{"topic": "Gastroenterology", "competency": "Pathophysiology", "stem": "What is the pathophysiological basis for constipation in patients with thoracic spinal cord injury?", "options": {"A": "Colonic dysfunction due to loss of extrinsic parasympathetic innervation", "B": "Gastrointestinal motility disorders associated with diabetes mellitus", "C": "Interventions for treating constipation in pregnancy", "D": "Functional importance of extrinsic parasympathetic innervation to the distal colon and rectum"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The pathophysiological basis for constipation in patients with thoracic spinal cord injury is related to colonic dysfunction due to loss of extrinsic parasympathetic innervation, which affects the normal functioning of the colon and rectum.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Gynecology_Novak_5039", "title": "Gynecology_Novak", "span_text": "Glick ME, Meshkinpour H, Haldeman S, et al. Colonic dysfunction in patients with thoracic spinal cord injury. Gastroenterology 1984;86:287\u2013294."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"time": 100, "doc_ids": ["Gynecology_Novak_5039", "Surgery_Schwartz_9913", "Pharmacology_Katzung_7008", "Pediatrics_Nelson_2861", "Pathology_Robbins_4068", "pubmed23n0004_14765"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Gynecology_Novak_5039", "Surgery_Schwartz_9913", "Pharmacology_Katzung_7008", "Pediatrics_Nelson_2861", "Pathology_Robbins_4068", "pubmed23n0004_14765"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"time": 200, "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Gastroenterology Pathophysiology pathophysiology pathogenesis mechanism molecular pathway cellular response inflammation hemodynamics neurohormonal physiology biomarkers dysfunction disease process", "evidence_top_k": 30, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 7570.817915999214, "llm_ms": 5082.786541010137, "verification_ms": 1185.01204099