{"topic": "Hypertension", "competency": "treatment", "stem": "A 58-year-old man with a long-standing history of hypertension is found to have bilateral adrenal nodules on imaging studies. What is the most likely cause of his resistant hypertension?", "options": {"A": "Primary aldosteronism with lateralized aldosterone secretion from one adrenal gland", "B": "Cushing syndrome due to autonomous cortisol secretion from both adrenal glands", "C": "Concomitant primary aldosteronism and Cushing syndrome due to bilateral macronodular adrenocortical disease", "D": "Renal artery stenosis due to atherosclerosis"}, "answer_key": "C", "explanation": "The patient's resistant hypertension is likely caused by the concomitant presence of primary aldosteronism and Cushing syndrome, which are both associated with bilateral macronodular adrenocortical disease. This condition is characterized by the presence of multiple nodules in both adrenal glands, leading to excessive production of aldosterone and cortisol.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed_web", "doc_id": "pubmed_web_41631058", "title": "Primary Bilateral Macronodular Adrenocortical Disease With Concomitant Cushing Syndrome and Primary Aldosteronism Harboring Distinct ARMC5 Mutations in Individual Nodules", "span_text": "We report the case of a 58-year-old man with a long-standing history of hypertension who was found to have bilateral adrenal nodules on imaging studies. Endocrinological evaluation revealed primary aldosteronism with lateralized aldosterone secretion from the left adrenal gland, along with autonomous cortisol secretion from both adrenal glands."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"time": "2024-02-20T14:30:00Z", "doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41631765", "pubmed_web_41631546", "pubmed_web_41631433", "pubmed_web_41631386", "pubmed_web_41631382", "pubmed_web_41631260", "pubmed_web_41631227", "pubmed_web_41631086", "pubmed_web_41631080", "pubmed_web_41631058", "pubmed_web_41631051", "pubmed_web_41631049", "pubmed_web_4