{"topic": "Anemia", "competency": "Pathology", "stem": "A 7-year-old child with chronic, severe anemia may exhibit which of the following skeletal abnormalities due to bone marrow expansion?", "options": {"A": "Macrocrania", "B": "Microcrania", "C": "Achondroplasia", "D": "Osteoporosis"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "Chronic, severe anemia can lead to bone marrow expansion, which may cause skeletal abnormalities such as macrocrania, an increase in skull thickness.", "evidence": [{"source": "textbook", "doc_id": "Pediatrics_Nelson_4009", "title": "Pediatrics_Nelson", "span_text": "Chronic, severe anemia Hydrocephalus (enlargement of the ventricles; see Chapter 184) Masses Megalencephaly (enlargement of the brain) Embryologic disorder causing abnormal proliferation of brain Accumulation of abnormal metabolic substances"}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"source": "database", "doc_ids": ["Pediatrics_Nelson_4009"], "evidence_doc_ids": ["Pediatrics_Nelson_4009"], "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank"}, "verification": {"method": "expert_review", "gate": {"pass": true, "failed_checks": [], "notes": []}, "attempt_history": [{"attempt": 1, "reason": "initial", "query": "Anemia Pathology disease infection tumor cancer syndrome disorder pathology lesion abnormal pathological malignancy benign acute chronic inflammation necrosis fibrosis metastasis", "evidence_top_k": 30, "candidate_count": 1, "selected_index": 1, "retrieval_ms": 5429.316458001267, "llm_ms": 1036.2622079992434, "verification_ms": 3584.939333006332, "extra_instructions": "", "retrieval_mode": "hybrid_rerank", "pubmed_web": {"attempted": false, "docs": 0, "runtime_ms": 0.0}, "status_counts": {"OK": 1, "INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE": 0, "FAILED_VERIFICATION": 0}, "failed_checks": [], "failed_checks_all": [], "evidence_doc_ids_used": ["Pediatrics_Nelson_4009"], "evidence_reused": false}]}, "timings_ms": {"retrieval": 10.0, "verification": 20.0, "llm": 1036.2622079992434}}}
{"topic": "Anemia", "competency": "Pathology", "stem": "A patient with warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia (wAIHA) and autoantibodies with anti-e specificity is at risk for worsening hemolysis after receiving a transfusion of e-antigen positive packed red blood cells. What is the primary reason for this increased risk?", "options": {"A": "The autoantibodies react with the e-antigen on the transfused red blood cells, leading to their destruction", "B": "The transfused red blood cells are more susceptible to hemolysis due to their increased age", "C": "The patient's immune system is more active after transfusion, leading to increased autoantibody production", "D": "The e-antigen positive red blood cells are more likely to be infected with a hemolytic virus"}, "answer_key": "A", "explanation": "The presence of autoantibodies with anti-e specificity in a patient with wAIHA increases the risk of hemolysis after transfusion of e-antigen positive packed red blood cells, as these autoantibodies can react with the e-antigen on the transfused red blood cells, leading to their destruction.", "evidence": [{"source": "pubmed_web", "doc_id": "pubmed_web_41613625", "title": "Transfusion-induced hemolysis in warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia with autoantibodies that have anti-e specificity: a case report and systematic review of the literature", "span_text": "The presence of specific autoantibodies, especially autoantibodies with anti-e specificity, is rare. The pathophysiology, management, and outcomes in this condition, including with transfusion of e-antigen positive packed red blood cells (pRBCs), remain poorly understood."}], "status": "OK", "meta": {"retrieval": {"time": 100, "doc_ids": ["pubmed_web_41631394", "pubmed_web_41629110", "pubmed_web_41627837", "pubmed_web_41625037", "pubmed_web_41623806", "pubmed_web_41623180", "pubmed_web_41621657", "pubmed_web_41621588", "pubmed_web_41620001", "pubmed_web_41616002", "pubmed_web_41614216", "pubmed_web_41613625", "pubmed_web_41613109"